  "@graph": [
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the bounded self is neither dissolved nor kept apart but carried into a union of two that preserves both parties to it, so that what is transformed persists as one side of a relation rather than as an absolute or as nothing."
      },
      "id": "pm:conjugalTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bräutliches Ziel",
        "en": "Conjugal telos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Distinct from pm:separativeTelos for the same reason from the other side: what persists here is not its own absolute but a party to a bond, and it is transformed on the way rather than fortified.",
          "Distinct from pm:unitiveTelos, and the distinction is not verbal. A dissolution has one term at the end; a marriage has two. A record that files a bridal end state as unitive reports the disappearance of something the source keeps."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The name under which the power is addressed at the moment of asking. When they invoke the huaca they call it Runapcamac, maker of men, and ask it for life, health and food, and they ask it nothing for any other life. Each lineage names its own pacarina, the place it says it came out of, and calls that Camac, maker; and each says it has its own maker, one a hill, one a spring."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Camac",
      "label": {
        "de": "Camac, der angerufene Macher",
        "en": "Camac, the maker that is invoked"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The term is entered at the strength the three passages give it and no higher. All three are the same recorder, and all three show him using the word as evidence that the Indians give to a stone what is due to God alone. What can be taken from them is that camac was the word used in address and that it was understood as making rather than as owning or protecting. What cannot be taken from them is a doctrine of camac as a substance or a force, which is a reading of the Quechua of Huarochirí and stands on a document this run could not open.",
          "The third of the three places is a question of the interrogatory the visitor was to put in every village. That the word had to be asked for by name shows that it was the recorder's own diagnostic and not an incidental report, which strengthens the evidence that it was in use and weakens the evidence about what it meant."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the invocation of the huaca; cap. VII, the pacarina of each lineage; cap. XIV, the third question of the visitor's interrogatory",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Burmese transmission of an accomplished practitioner who prolongs life by alchemy, diagram and mantra in order to await a future buddha."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BurmeseWeikza",
      "label": {
        "de": "Burmesische Weikza-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Burmese weikza transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A living tradition whose claims are made about named contemporaries, which is rare and worth more to an examination than any ancient ascription."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The published weikza and dhat-lonn literature in Burmese",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Late Bronze Age Levantine ritual and incantation, including the address to the deified dead."
      },
      "id": "pmt:UgariticRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ugaritisch-levantinisches Ritual",
        "en": "Ugaritic and Levantine ritual"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The KTU corpus, including the Rapiuma texts and the serpent incantations",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seal made by pressing the perineum with the heel and contracting the anus, by which the downward wind is drawn upward."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Mulabandha",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wurzelsiegel",
        "en": "The root seal"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.61-63 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HathaChapterOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Handbuch ordnet Haltung, Atem, Siegel und Versenkung",
        "en": "The manual orders posture, breath, seal and absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The four chapters run posture, breath, seal, absorption, and that arrangement is often cited as though it were itself a rule. The rule is in II.1; the chapter order is only an arrangement."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NadiPurification"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:presentationOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Asana"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I-IV (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ProphesyingPresupposesMedicineEating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Weissagen setzt das Essen der Medizinen voraus",
        "en": "Prophesying presupposes the eating of medicines"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: the source states a disqualification and not only an order. A novice who has eaten only a few times dances at the seance but does not divine, and the quantity eaten is what the source names as the reason."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A man who has eaten the medicines only a few times is not qualified to take a prominent part in a seance or to prophesy at it."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:SeeingWitchcraft"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 96",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Pāli canon and its commentarial literature, of which the Visuddhimagga of Buddhaghosa is the systematising handbook."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Theravada",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theravāda",
        "en": "Theravāda"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Visuddhimagga citations name the translation they were read from, and Dīgha Nikāya 2 was reopened for this purpose. For Majjhima Nikāya 118, Dīgha Nikāya 11 and Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8 the edition or translation is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those three passages are to be reopened."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The forms of makutu employed to turn back the working of another tohunga, that is, a counter-operation of the same kind rather than a protection of a different kind."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Whakahokitu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Whakahokitu",
        "en": "Whakahokitu"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 35",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SpiritPresupposesBreath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Verfeinerung des Geistes setzt die des Atems voraus",
        "en": "Refining spirit presupposes refining breath"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded from the Yuan-period witness, not from the Han or Song works to which the sequence is commonly credited."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NeidanThreeStages"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Qi"
      },
      "source": "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConfessionYieldsAverting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Beichte wendet den Zorn ab",
        "en": "The confession turns aside the anger"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The whole work of fire and water at a birth is committed to the specialist who holds it as his office, and who among these people ordinarily bears the name and the office of physician."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FireAndWaterRite",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Ritus mit Feuer und Wasser bei einer Geburt",
        "en": "The rite of fire and water at a birth"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sentence is a statement about an institution: the office exists, it has a name, and the rite is not performed by the family. That is the part a later reader can check, and it is why the passage is entered as a practice rather than as an item of belief."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The requirement, set on the parts of the ceremony chanted aloud and in unison, that the words be rendered letter perfect."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe",
        "en": "Letter-perfect delivery"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Prayers held to carry power in themselves, so that having them is a source of power and losing or impairing them is a great danger."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerPossession",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Gebet als Besitz",
        "en": "The prayer as a possession"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The nearest thing the passages read give to a stated consequence of getting a prayer wrong, and deliberately not entered as one: the sentence speaks of loss and impairment of the prayer as a possession, not of the effect of a misrecited performance."
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A living gnostic transmission with its own scripture and priesthood, teaching an ascent of the soul past stations of examination."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Mandaean",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mandäische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Mandaean transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ginzā Rabbā; Drāšā d-Yahia; Qolastā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second body, formed and complete in its faculties, said to be produced from this one; the classical form of the claim the modern draft calls exteriorisation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Manomayakaya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Geistgemachter Körper",
        "en": "Mind-made body"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The diviner by maize makes small heaps of grains without counting them, then takes one away from one side and one from another, and according as they are left even or odd the lot is good or bad. The earlier report gives the same mechanism under another name and with another material: they cast lots with maize and llama dung, and if they came out even or odd they gave their answers accordingly."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParityDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrsagung durch gerade und ungerade",
        "en": "Divination by odd and even"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "In one village a practitioner of this office produced instead a bag of small stones which he said were called by a particular name and that he had inherited them from his grandfather for this purpose. The variant is recorded because it shows the office surviving a change of instrument.",
          "Two recorders, forty-six years and several hundred leagues apart, name different practitioners and the same mechanism. The count is deliberately not made before the drawing in the later account, which means the outcome is not determined by anything the diviner knows; that is the same feature the poison-oracle material in this graph rests on, and it is stated here of a maize heap."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the achicoc",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the diviner by maize"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The performance of the eight limbs, taken together as one course of practice."
      },
      "id": "pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übung der Glieder",
        "en": "Practice of the limbs"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When the feast is over, guinea pigs are sacrificed in some places and it is seen by which part the blood runs in the entrails whether they have fasted well and kept all the ceremonies of the feast. The last act is named, and the name is given in the source."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FastKeptWellIsAlsoChecked",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Fasten gehalten wurde, wird nachträglich geprüft",
        "en": "Whether the fast was kept is checked afterwards"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A second procedure of the same shape as the confession test and applied to a different object: not to a statement but to a period of conduct. Together with the confession test it makes the checking of the participant, rather than of the answer, a recurring feature of this transmission and not a single case."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SitaliYieldsCooling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Einatmung über die Zunge bringt Kühlung hervor",
        "en": "Inhalation over the tongue yields cooling"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because the opening through which the breath is taken is not incidental here but is what the practice consists in. Two of the eight retentions the manual lists take the breath in through the mouth and give it out through the nose; two others require the mouth held shut. The manual therefore assigns the opening per practice and does not set nasal breathing as a general rule, still less mark mouth inhalation as an error. See prima-materia#26."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Sitali"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:CoolingAndQuenching"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the attempt to bring back a dead man failed because a prohibition had been broken, and not because the shamans were unable."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ReturnPresupposesTheSewingProhibition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wiederaufleben setzt die Einhaltung des Nähverbots voraus",
        "en": "The return after death presupposes that the prohibition was kept"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The structure of this passage matters more than its content, and it is the reason the node is here. A failure of the practice is reported, and the account assigns the failure to a third party's breach of a prohibition rather than to the practitioners or to the claim. That move makes the underlying claim unfailable from inside the tradition: any outcome confirms it, since success shows the power and failure shows the breach. Recording the immunisation is not an objection to the passage; it is the part a later protocol has to work against."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The narrator relates that the moon would not let the man come to life again, because his mother had sewn garments of new caribou skin on the island of Oqshoriaq, which is not allowed there; the two shamans who tried to catch the returning corpse did not succeed.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A single large revealed cosmology published in 1955, presenting itself as a series of papers by named celestial authors and describing a graded administration of the universe."
      },
      "id": "pmt:UrantiaBook",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Urantia-Buch",
        "en": "The Urantia Book"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The human circumstances of its production were withheld on purpose by the group that published it, which leaves its provenance the weakest in a block whose provenance is otherwise its strength."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Urantia Book",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first of the two stages of the highest tantra, treated before the completion stage in the training."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GenerationStage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erzeugungsstufe",
        "en": "Generation stage"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When two noaidi wished to know which of them excelled the other in the art, they painted on a drum two reindeer striking each other with their horns, and he whose reindeer had the victory was the better."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ContestOfTwoNoaidi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Wettstreit zweier Praktiker",
        "en": "The contest of two practitioners"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A procedure with a stated criterion, applied by practitioners to practitioners, with an outcome that could fall either way and that neither controls. Nothing else in this file, and very little in this graph, has that shape.",
          "What the source does not say is how the victory of a painted reindeer is determined — whether by the ring, by the movement of the drum, or otherwise. Without that the procedure cannot be reconstructed, only recorded, and no protocol node is written on it."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 477",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SarielTeachesCourseOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sariel offenbart den Lauf des Mondes",
        "en": "Sariel discloses the course of the moon"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:CourseOfTheMoon"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Sariel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A structural correspondence observed across traditions, held as a node so that it can carry its routes of transmission and its counterexamples instead of asserting universality."
      },
      "id": "pm:Converging",
      "label": {
        "de": "Konvergieren",
        "en": "Converging"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Every instance is an ordering made by a compiler and carries pm:compilerInference, never textual attestation. No source says that the concepts correspond; the correspondence is the reading, and it is held as a node so that it can be argued with. Its sources are the sources of the concepts it brings together, and they attest those concepts and not their correspondence.",
          "Independence is also a weaker instrument than it looks, and this is the reason breadth is held beside it rather than under it. It guards against none of the three commonest sources of agreement: a shared physiology that produces the same experience wherever the same thing is done to a body, a structural necessity of the subject matter that makes any competent version converge, and the form of graded institutions, which resemble one another because institutions with grades do. What decides in the end is what can be reproduced, and independence is a way of sorting candidates for that, not a verdict.",
          "Most apparent universality in the Western esoteric record is diffusion from few sources. A graph that counts a citation chain as independent confirmation measures reception history and reports it as structure.",
          "That is a warning against one way of counting and not against reception itself. Reception history is a finding of its own: how many transmissions carry a claim at all, and how deeply the paths between them branch, are two separate numbers and neither cancels the other. Many witnesses on one cable is a different map from many witnesses on three, and both are more informative than the earlier arrangement, in which the first case was simply not shown. The one thing the graph must be able to do is say which of the two maps a reader is looking at: dense transmission and strong effect must never look alike, or the gate of authority returns in statistical dress."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PranayamaPresupposesAsana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atemregulierung setzt Festigkeit in der Haltung voraus",
        "en": "Breath regulation presupposes firmness in posture"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The verse opens the breath chapter by requiring that the practitioner first be established in posture and in a measured diet, and that the breath practice then follow the instruction of a teacher. The condition is in the base text, not in a commentary."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Asana"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.1 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A claim that this ordering act groups. The claims keep their own attestation; only the grouping belongs to the compiler."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Systematizing",
      "id": "pm:ordersClaim",
      "label": {
        "de": "ordnet Behauptung",
        "en": "orders claim"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practice a yielding claim proceeds from."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Yielding",
      "id": "pm:byPractice",
      "label": {
        "de": "durch Praxis",
        "en": "by practice"
      },
      "range": "pm:Practicing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what is required for absorption. The discourses describe it as reached in the ordinary course of the training; the handbook sets a far higher bar, remarking that only one in a hundred or a thousand accomplishes it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AbsorptionThresholdDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wie hoch die Schwelle der Vertiefung liegt",
        "en": "How high the threshold of absorption stands"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The disagreement bears directly on every prerequisite in this file that runs through the fourth absorption. If the handbook's threshold is the right one, the capacities it makes conditional on that absorption are conditional on something very few reach; if the discourses' is, they are not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that the mass of dreams occurring in full sleep carries no divination, and that only the state at the edge of sleep is treated as a sign of divine presence."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DivineDreamIsNotOrdinarySleep",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der göttliche Traum ist nicht der gewöhnliche Schlaf",
        "en": "The divine dream is not ordinary sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Load-bearing against any claim that sleep as such is an access. The source that comes closest in the whole collection to naming the threshold state is also the one that refuses the wider claim."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity, practice or pursuit a warning is directed at."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Cautioning",
      "id": "pm:cautionsAbout",
      "label": {
        "de": "warnt vor",
        "en": "cautions about"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaIsInnerToThePrecedingLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drei inneren Glieder sind innerlicher als die vorhergehenden fünf",
        "en": "The three inner limbs are inner to the preceding five"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The base text ranks the last three limbs as inner with respect to the preceding five, and then ranks even those three as outer with respect to the seedless absorption. It sets a relation of inner and outer, not a rule that one cannot be attempted before the other, and it says nothing about what follows from disregarding the ranking."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.7",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.8"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1950",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorders state that the material was put in order in the sense that the sayings of the four principal informants were fused into one and the same exposition. What the printed system presents as a single doctrine is therefore a composite, and no sentence in it can be assigned to a named speaker."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FusionOfTheFourInformants",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vier Gewährsleute zu einer Darstellung verschmolzen",
        "en": "Four informants fused into one account"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an ordering act because the recorders describe it as one in the same breath in which they claim to give the raw documents. The two claims are in tension on the same page: material that has been fused across four speakers is no longer raw, and the fusion is the step at which a disagreement between informants would have become invisible.",
          "One informant is described as having supplied the system in its entirety and the others only its essential parts, and one of them obtained a supplement between the visits of the enquirers, from people elsewhere. The account is thus composite in a second sense: part of it was gathered by an informant on the enquirers' behalf between their stays."
        ]
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen",
      "pm:ordersClaim": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:EmmeYaTolo"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:PoTolo"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:PoToloAsAxis"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:PoToloOrbit"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 273-274",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:LovePresupposesApatheia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Liebe setzt Apatheia voraus, Erkenntnis die Liebe",
        "en": "Love presupposes apatheia, and knowledge presupposes love"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The chain is stated as generation and not as sequence: each term is the child or the flower of the one before it. That is why the strength here is necessity, and why a consequence can be quoted at all."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "Love is called the offspring of apatheia and is said to keep the door to the contemplation of created things, so that without it the door is not passed.",
      "pm:dependentStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Agape"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Physike"
        }
      ],
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Agape"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Apatheia"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The opening stage of the inscription: the breath is taken deeply, whereupon it gathers."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiDepth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Tiefe als erste Stufe",
        "en": "Depth as the first stage"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The combined application of dhāraṇā, dhyāna and samādhi to a single object, presented as the general operator from which the capacities of the third chapter follow."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Samyama",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama",
        "en": "Saṃyama"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-3",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A ceremony celebrated once in sixty years whose stated purpose is the renewal of the world. The star that governs its reckoning is named after it: sigi tolo, star of the Sigui."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Sigui",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sigui",
        "en": "Sigui"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274 and 280",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AbsorptionsAreGraded",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jede Vertiefung setzt die vorhergehende voraus",
        "en": "Each absorption presupposes the one before it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The series is strictly graded in both canon and handbook, each stage defined by what it has let go of. No passage was found that forbids skipping in so many words, which is why this is recorded as an order and not as a necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourthJhana"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the man and his animal are one body for the purpose of injury: after a threatened person had a fight in the river with a caiman or in the open with some other animal, the man who had uttered the threat was afterwards found bearing the same wounds the animal had taken from the fight, although he had been elsewhere while it happened."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WoundsSharedWithTheAnimal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Mensch trägt die Wunden, die das Tier davontrug",
        "en": "The man is found with the wounds the animal took"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the most examinable claim in what was read, and it is examinable in the ordinary way: it asserts a coincidence of injuries between two bodies at a distance, with a stated time relation. Nothing about it requires an opinion on whether the transformation occurs. No protocol stands on it yet; see prima-materia#231."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that the raising of the body hair is codified as a sign of an inner state in two Indian genres that have no common subject matter: a meditation handbook and a treatise on dramatic performance."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HorripilationCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Entsprechung der Haaraufrichtung",
        "en": "The horripilation correspondence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The strongest item in this file, and still not what it first appears. The independence is generic and not cultural: both texts belong to the Indian sphere, so a common background cannot be excluded. What can be excluded is that one borrowed the observation from the other, since neither is about the other's subject. A universal physiology codified twice is a finding about the physiology."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:KhuddikaPiti"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Romanca"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "Here the ground is not a date and the node says so rather than borrowing an argument it does not have. The commentary is of the fifth century of the common era and the treatise on performance is older, so priority runs the wrong way for an argument from age. What carries the independence is that neither text treats the other's subject: a handbook of meditation had no occasion to take an observation about gooseflesh from a manual for actors, or the reverse. Both are received texts, so no gathering situation is at issue. A common Indian background remains available and is not excluded by this ground.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": [
        {
          "id": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmt:Theravada"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "Both codifications arise within the Indian cultural sphere; independence holds at the level of genre and subject matter, not at the level of culture.",
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmt:ChristianDiscernment",
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      }
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The fourth state: pure awareness that is neither outward- nor inward-turned, and not a negation of either."
      },
      "id": "pm:TranscendentState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Transzendenter Zustand (Turīya)",
        "en": "Transcendent state (Turīya)"
      },
      "source": "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 7",
      "type": "pm:AwarenessContext"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree, marked in the chart by the shell placed at the top of the head."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideThoughtReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lesen der Gedanken und Absichten anderer",
        "en": "Reading the thoughts and intentions of others"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of heka as an operative power held by gods, kings and the transfigured dead, carried in mortuary and temple literature."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EgyptianRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pharaonisch-ägyptisches Ritual",
        "en": "Pharaonic Egyptian ritual"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The transformation spells are a capacity list in the strict sense: each states what the speaker becomes and what that state permits."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Pyramid Texts; Coffin Texts; Book of the Dead; Papyrus Westcar; the demotic magical papyrus of London and Leiden",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The generation of inner heat, presented in three components: the channels, the syllables, and the heat meditation itself."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Tummo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tummo",
        "en": "Tummo"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A class named apart from all the other specialists and said to be the only ones who properly deserve the name of witch. They are said to gather at night under a master, who goes to a chosen house scattering a powder made of the bones of the dead, by which everyone in the house is put so deeply asleep that neither person nor animal stirs; he comes to the one he means to kill, draws a little blood with his nail from any part of the body and sucks what he can, for which they are also called suckers; what is so taken is carried to the assembly, where it is said to be multiplied into flesh, and is cooked and eaten, and the person dies within two or three days. The common phrase for the assembly is that tonight we are to eat the soul of such a one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheEatersOfMen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Menschenesser",
        "en": "The eaters of men"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The recorder states how the class came to be known and the account is unusually traceable for this file: it was not established until one man volunteered it after his examination was over, naming others, who were then arrested and examined singly and against one another. Sixty-three were found culpable in four villages of the coast, none at all in the highland villages at that time, and more later in two other provinces. He also reports the confessions in the words of the accused, including one who said he had been made to eat by force and threatened with death if he did not come again.",
          "The whole of it was obtained under an examination conducted by a tribunal with the power to punish, and the recorder says the punishment given during the visitation was deliberately light in order to keep them quiet until a heavier one could be arranged. What such an examination produces is a well-known thing and the count of sixty-three is not evidence of sixty-three witches. It is entered because it is what the record says the transmission says about itself, and because the shape of the claim — the night assembly, the taking of a little blood, the eating of the soul, the death within two or three days — is the material a comparison is made from, not because the events are credited."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the cauchus or runapmicuc",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The late Victorian transmission that welded kabbalistic, grimoire and Egyptian material into a graded initiatory curriculum with examinations."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GoldenDawn",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn",
        "en": "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The syncretism is documented in its own papers, which makes it the best available record of how a modern system is assembled from older parts and then presented as one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The cipher manuscripts and knowledge lectures; Regardie, The Golden Dawn",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Dibbasota",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Ohr",
        "en": "Divine ear"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stage at which the gathered and extended breath goes downward, after which it settles and becomes firm; the sequence then turns and rises."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiDescent",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Abstieg als Stufe der Folge",
        "en": "Descent as a stage of the sequence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The inscription assigns direction to a place in a sequence, not to a phase of breathing. Descent follows gathering and extension; ascent follows sprouting and growing. The closing lines set the two directions against the two powers, the dynamism of heaven showing in what rises and that of earth in what descends. Nothing here binds a direction to inhaling or to exhaling. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A prayer of stereotyped phrasing, held by an appointed keeper for each kiva and taught over four nights after each planting of prayer sticks, the instructor intoning while the pupils join in by degrees and afterwards reciting it back to him."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ZuniPrayer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zuni-Festgebet",
        "en": "Zuni fixed prayer"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The teaching method is itself a check: the pupil returns the prayer to the keeper, so that a divergence is caught before it is carried into the ceremony. Because the phrasing is stereotyped the witness names the sequence, and not the wording, as the hard part of learning a long prayer."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from the sun."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Sonne",
        "en": "Signs of the sun"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the state of another consciousness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Cetopariyanana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins",
        "en": "Knowledge encompassing another mind"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A closed transmission teaching the transfer of the soul into a new body at death, with an internal literature restricted to the initiated."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Druze",
      "label": {
        "de": "Drusische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Druze transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A case where the gate the project exists to remove is still shut and still enforced. Registration says so rather than pretending the corpus is available."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rasāʾil al-Ḥikma",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Mindfulness of in-breath and out-breath, set out as a sixteen-step progression in the discourse and as a meditation subject in the handbook."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Anapanasati",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ānāpānasati",
        "en": "Ānāpānasati"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Majjhima Nikāya 118"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A light held to be uncreated, and held to shine through the whole person, body as well as soul; the defence is explicitly that the body participates and is not merely the site of an event in the soul."
      },
      "id": "pmc:UncreatedLight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ungeschaffenes Licht",
        "en": "Uncreated light"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is a claim about what shines through the practitioner, not about an emanation the practitioner produces or directs."
      },
      "source": "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts (trans. Gendle 1983)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the confession is made for: the faults of a man are held to anger his ancestors and his huacas, and the anger to be the reason he falls sick. The confession, the blowing of the powders, the rubbing with the pardon-stone and the washing of the head are done so that the anger is turned aside and the sickness with it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Abwenden des Zorns der Huacas und der Ahnen",
        "en": "Turning aside the anger of the huacas and the ancestors"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of placing one concept in relation to another."
      },
      "id": "pm:Relating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Beziehen",
        "en": "Relating"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaCaksus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama auf die Sonne bringt Weltkenntnis hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama on the sun yields knowledge of the world"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksus"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Who devised or carried the protocol. A protocol without a named author is unaccountable, and an unaccountable protocol is not evidence of anything."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:examinedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "geprüft von",
        "en": "examined by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Zuni transmission of fixed prayer, memorised verbatim and held to be ineffective if altered."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ZuniRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zuni-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Zuni ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A tradition that states its own falsifying condition: the words must be exact. Whether exactness is in fact required is examinable.",
          "Read for the first time in the introduction to the ritual poetry, pages 611 to 620 of the printed report. The body of the collection, the prayers themselves, was not read. The corpus stands in the language of its speakers with a facing translation, which is what makes it admissible here at all; the recorder is an outsider and the question of ethnographic first fixation raised at prima-materia#91 is not settled by this file."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, with the Zuni texts",
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 611-835",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DreamDivinationPresupposesPurityOfTheVehicle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumdivination setzt die Reinheit des Fahrzeugs voraus",
        "en": "Dream divination presupposes purity of the vehicle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The condition is on the state of the seer, not on a technique of sleeping. Recorded as order rather than necessity because the treatise makes the clarity of the dream depend on the vehicle without stating at that place what befalls one who proceeds with an unpurified one. Chapter divisions vary between editions; the work and its subject are given so that the passage can be found in whichever edition is at hand."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:DreamDivination"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:PneumaticVehicle"
      },
      "source": "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission the claim was taken over from. The work and the place stay in dcterms:source; this names the transmission, so that the depth of a chain can be counted."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Reworking",
      "id": "pm:receivedFrom",
      "label": {
        "de": "übernommen von",
        "en": "received from"
      },
      "range": "pm:Tradition",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A graded emptying in which listening passes from the ears to the heart-mind and from the heart-mind to the qi, until only emptiness remains for the way to gather in."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fasten des Herzens",
        "en": "Fasting of the heart"
      },
      "source": "Zhuangzi 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The fourth of the five degrees of rapture, described as strong enough to lift the body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:UbbegaPiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erhebungsverzückung",
        "en": "Uplifting rapture"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The offices are named separately because they are separate. The one who speaks with the huaca, keeps it, carries the offerings, sets the fasts and answers the people in its name; the ones who do the same for the ancestor bodies, for the lightning and for the sun; the lesser minister who assists each of them; the one who cures; the one who hears confession; the one who makes the chicha; and a series of diviners named by their instruments — by heaps of maize counted odd or even, by the twitching of the upper arms, by the legs of a large spider broken one by one, by dreams, and by opening a guinea pig with the thumbnail and reading where the blood runs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GradedSpecialists",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die gestuften Spezialisten",
        "en": "The graded specialists"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Every office in it is held by women as well as men, the confessing included, on the recorder's express statement; he adds that the principal offices were more often men's and the lesser ones, divining and making the chicha, more often women's.",
          "The indigenous chronicler of sixteen fifteen names four classes with Quechua terms and the extirpation account of sixteen twenty-one names two of the same as its general terms. Two recorders of opposite standing therefore agree on the vocabulary while disagreeing about almost everything else, which is the strongest thing that can be said for this list."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno (1615), Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 2232 4°, p. 278 [280], the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The nineteenth century transmission of extended texts produced by a writer who reports not composing them, and issued as scripture rather than as message."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Spiritistisches automatisches Schreiben",
        "en": "Spiritualist automatic writing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The earliest layer of the block and the template for all of it: a named person, a dated production, a printed volume, and the claim that the author is elsewhere."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Newbrough, Oahspe; Davis, The Principles of Nature, Her Divine Revelations, and a Voice to Mankind",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The western Christian transmission of graded interior states, with criteria for telling one state from another and warnings against taking consolation for progress."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LatinMysticism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lateinische Mystik",
        "en": "Latin mystical transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Teresa's dwellings and John of the Cross's nights are the western material closest to a stage map with failure modes, and the failure modes are stated more plainly than the attainments."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Richard of St Victor, Benjamin Major; Meister Eckhart, German sermons; The Cloud of Unknowing; Teresa of Ávila, Las Moradas; John of the Cross, Subida del Monte Carmelo and Noche oscura",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Not all who address the huaca see it. Although everyone knows in which direction it stands, few see it, because they hang back and only the priest goes forward, speaks and offers. Places where a huaca is said to have rested, called zamana, and places from which it is invoked, called cayan, are reverenced too."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AddressWithoutSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Haupt-Huaca wird angesprochen, ohne gesehen zu werden",
        "en": "The principal huaca is addressed without being seen"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "In one village a woman who kept the huaca told the people to cover their eyes when they adored it, because the divine essence cannot be seen with bodily eyes. That is the transmission stating a reason for the arrangement, and it is entered because a reason given from inside is worth more than the recorder's explanation, which is that the devil had put fear into them.",
          "The arrangement has a consequence the recorder reports with satisfaction and which is a finding whoever reports it: when the villagers were finally made to look at what they had feared, that was itself the strongest argument against it. A cult whose object is addressed without being seen is a cult that can be undone by showing the object."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II and cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earliest organised Daoist transmission, in which illness is treated as the consequence of fault and healing proceeds by confession and petition."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CelestialMasters",
      "label": {
        "de": "Himmelsmeister",
        "en": "Celestial Masters"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Xiang'er commentary on the Laozi; Zhengyi fawen; the Taiping jing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The part given over to the life of Christ up to Palm Sunday, and to the following of it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SecondWeek",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die zweite Woche",
        "en": "The second week"
      },
      "source": "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The working handbooks of late antique Egypt: procedures with materials, hours, purity requirements, spoken formulae and stated signs of success."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri",
      "label": {
        "de": "Griechische und demotische Zauberpapyri",
        "en": "Greek and demotic magical papyri"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The densest procedural corpus available to the project, and the one most likely to carry falsifying conditions, because a handbook has to tell its user when the working has failed."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Papyri Graecae Magicae and Papyri Demoticae Magicae, including the Mithras Liturgy at PGM IV.475-829",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rite at Eleusis, held in the month Boedromion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GreaterMysteries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die großen Mysterien",
        "en": "The greater mysteries"
      },
      "source": "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EleusinianMysteries"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondWeekPresupposesFirst",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die zweite Woche setzt die erste voraus",
        "en": "The second week presupposes the first"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The same annotation that sets the order removes any fixed duration from it: the weeks need not have seven or eight days, and one who needs longer in the first is to be given longer. The order is set, the schedule is not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:SecondWeek"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FirstWeek"
      },
      "source": "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of a figure is reported to follow an established rule, and the rule is reported to be known only to those initiated into it. The fee paid to a priest for being taught the art is reported as very heavy."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaRuleOfInterpretationClosed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Deutungsregel ist fest und verschlossen",
        "en": "The rule of interpretation is fixed and closed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two gates named in one sentence, and they are of different kinds. That the rule is fixed is a claim about the system and is checkable against any later account of it; that it is known only to the initiated is a claim about access, and the fee gives its price. The account is an outsider's and reports what he was not told, which is a weaker witness for the content of the rule than for the fact of its closure."
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tantric transmission of a sixfold yoga whose completion is marked by a sequence of signs appearing in a darkened retreat."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Kalacakra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kālacakra",
        "en": "Kālacakra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sign sequence is stated in fixed order and is therefore comparable with the Dzogchen visions and with the phosphene literature, which is a convergence question the graph cannot currently pose."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Vimalaprabhā; Sekoddeśa and its commentary",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Spirits belonging to a particular shaman, called up by him and reported to act in the world on his behalf: to guard a child, to raise a barrier against the dead, to find what has been lost."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitHelpingSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hilfsgeister",
        "en": "Helping spirits"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Rapture as a bodily felt state, graded in the handbook into five degrees of increasing pervasion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Piti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pīti",
        "en": "Pīti"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The five degrees are khuddikā, khaṇikā, okkantikā, ubbegā and pharaṇā."
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Chinese transmission holding that awakening is sudden and that the mind is not to be polished but recognised."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Chan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chan",
        "en": "Chan"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Platform Sūtra; Linji lu; Biyan lu",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of method. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other: a passage, an edition, and the counter-search."
      },
      "id": "pm:methodOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung der Methode",
        "en": "method orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No domain is declared, deliberately. The axis is stated of whatever a source states it of — a transmission as a whole, a single practice, a stage within one — and a declared domain would force the level before the material has settled it."
      },
      "range": "pm:MethodOrientation",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second entry: three feasts to the instructing and officiating priests, three chanted prayers to the named manidō, and a second shooting of the shell."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree",
      "label": {
        "de": "Einweihung in den zweiten Grad",
        "en": "Initiation into the second degree"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "comment": {
        "en": "Consciousness-first meta-ontology. Processes, not substances."
      },
      "id": "https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology",
      "license": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/",
      "title": {
        "en": "prima-materia core ontology"
      },
      "type": "owl:Ontology"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The art of fixing speech in ink on paper, treated in the text as a transgression rather than a gift."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WritingWithInk",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schreiben mit Tinte",
        "en": "Writing with ink"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stopping of a fluid held to fall from above and to be consumed below, whose loss the manual makes responsible for ageing and death."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Zurückhalten des herabfließenden Nektars",
        "en": "The retention of the descending nectar"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.77 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A four-part examination of an inner movement, set out in the image of the money-changer who tests a coin: for the metal, for the stamp, for the lawfulness of its provenance, and for its weight."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TestingOfThoughts",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Prüfung der Gedanken",
        "en": "The examination of thoughts"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Ignatius adds the rule that the whole course of a train of thought is to be examined, since a bad end reveals the origin, and that a consolation arriving after the fact is to be examined well before it is believed."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "John Cassian, transmitting Abba Moses; supplemented by Ignatius of Loyola",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A movement fails the test if its metal is false, its stamp is not that of right teaching, its provenance is unlawful, or it weighs light of the fear of God and heavy of human vanity; and it fails if the end of the train of thought is bad, however good its beginning.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-28",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins"
      },
      "source": [
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 334",
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Testing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of consciousness-only doctrine together with the most detailed Indian description of meditative signs and the stages at which they appear."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yogacara",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yogācāra",
        "en": "Yogācāra"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Asaṅga, Yogācārabhūmi, particularly the Śrāvakabhūmi; Vasubandhu, Triṃśikā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MedicineEatingYieldsSeeing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Essen der Medizinen bringt das Sehen hervor",
        "en": "Eating the medicines yields the seeing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is not circular in the sense SPEC §11 forbids: the medicines are vegetable preparations named by their plants and the capacity is a perception ascribed to the eater, so the practice is not the capacity under another name."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SeeingWitchcraft"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Abulafia's transmission of letter permutation, breath and head movement as a technique for reaching a state he calls prophecy."
      },
      "id": "pmt:PropheticKabbalah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prophetische Kabbala",
        "en": "Prophetic Kabbalah"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered apart from Kabbalah because it is a manual and not a doctrine. On the level of stated procedure it is the closest western counterpart to the Yogasūtra that exists, including its warnings about what befalls the unprepared."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Abulafia, Or ha-Sekhel; Abulafia, Ḥayyei ha-Olam ha-Ba",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A capacity ascribed to a named ruler, in an account whose author remarks that those who were present still tell it although there is now no reward for the lie."
      },
      "id": "pmc:VespasianHealing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Heilung durch Berührung, Vespasian zugeschrieben",
        "en": "Healing by touch ascribed to Vespasian"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The remark about eyewitnesses is part of the ascription, not an endorsement of it. It records how the historian wanted the claim received."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:HealingByTouch"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Vespasian"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": [
        "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The leader of the Watchers who binds the others by oath on Mount Hermon and is named as the teacher of spells and root-cutting."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Shemihazah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schemichasa",
        "en": "Shemihazah"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Amezarak"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Semjaza"
        }
      ],
      "source": "1 Enoch 6:3, 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The disclosure of harmful operations: the striking of spirits and demons, the killing of the embryo in the womb, the serpent's bite and the stroke of the noonday heat."
      },
      "id": "pmc:StrikingOfSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schlagen der Geister und der Leibesfrucht",
        "en": "Striking of spirits and of the womb"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rite by which a person is admitted to the practice, set out in the tantra and reproduced with commentary in the Kashmirian exegesis."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Diksa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Initiation",
        "en": "Initiation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The correspondence between the Indian channel-and-heat material and its Tibetan form, held as a node in order to be counted out: the route is documented, so the agreement is reception and carries no weight as evidence of an independently arrived-at structure."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IndoTibetanReception",
      "label": {
        "de": "Indo-tibetische Rezeption, keine Konvergenz",
        "en": "Indo-Tibetan reception, not convergence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because a graph that only holds findings cannot show its own exclusions. Any later count of independent attestations must skip this pair."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:InnerHeat"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Kundalini"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Rtsa"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "The six yogas reach Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator; the transmission is named in the Tibetan sources themselves.",
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission that relocates the alchemical work into the body, with a staged refinement of essence, breath and spirit and an embryo said to form and depart."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Neidan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neidan, innere Alchemie",
        "en": "Neidan internal alchemy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian; Zhong-Lü chuandao ji; Xingming guizhi",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, made by the narrator about her second husband, that when a knife was lost he would hold a spirit calling and in that way recover it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SpiritCallingYieldsRecoveryOfLostThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Geisterrufen soll Verlorenes wiederbringen",
        "en": "Spirit calling is said to recover what has been lost"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The most examinable claim in the portion read, and the reason it is entered separately from the capacity it names. It states a repeatable occasion, a named class of object, and an outcome that either occurred or did not; it is not a claim about a state of the practitioner. What it does not state is any case in which the calling was held and the knife stayed lost."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:InuitSpiritCalling"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConsultationPresupposesQualifyingWood",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung setzt sonnenläufiges Holz voraus",
        "en": "Consulting the drum presupposes wood that turns with the sun"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drum must be of such wood, and does not say what follows from using a drum that is not. The strength is the strength the text gives, not the strength the requirement sounds like.",
          "This is the condition the graph nearly lost. It was read at the wording in the same run as the rest of this file but reached only a local working copy, not the branch, and was recovered only because an uncommitted file was noticed. Recorded as a process finding in prima-materia#351."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The society names a figure of its own kind who employs the powers for evil purposes, who can assume the form of any animal and in that shape destroy the life of his victim. The warning is not against an outsider but against a member who has passed the same degrees."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BadMideCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der böse Midē, der die Vermögen zum Schaden wendet",
        "en": "The bad Midē who turns the powers to harm"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A tradition that catalogues capacities and warns against their use is the shape this collection meets in Pātañjala Yoga as well, and the two warnings are not the same warning. There the capacities are called obstacles to the goal of the practice; here they are called dangerous in the hands of a man who wants harm. One is a caution about the practitioner's direction, the other about his intention."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideDistantTouch"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideSoulSeizing"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168-169",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named in the Aramaic-Greek teaching list as the one who disclosed the signs of the shooting stars. The position he occupies is held by Ezeqeel in the Ethiopic; no identification of the two is asserted here."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ziqel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ziqel",
        "en": "Ziqel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operation by which a figure is obtained: sixteen palm nuts are held loosely in one hand and thrown through the half-closed fingers into the other. One nut left behind is marked with a double stroke and two nuts left behind with a single stroke, and the throw is repeated until two columns of four marks stand on the whitened board."
      },
      "id": "pmp:IfaCasting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Wurf der sechzehn Palmnüsse",
        "en": "Casting the sixteen palm nuts"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The mark is set by what remains in the hand and not by what the diviner judges, so the figure is not chosen. The source describes the mechanism and does not draw that consequence itself; the observation is recorded here and not asserted as the source's."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a named person always knows in advance when anything remarkable is going to happen. The claim is examinable because it is universal and because the tradition itself counts the announcement, not the interpretation, as the thing that happens."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitSecondSightExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Vorherwissens-Behauptung ihre eigene Allgemeinform übersteht",
        "en": "Whether the foreknowledge claim survives its own universal form"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One confirming case is related in the same passage and no failing case is. The case does not count against the condition and does not count for it either: it was told after the event it is said to have foretold, by the wife of the man it is told about, and a dream of a friend eaten by his kin is a description that the encounter afterwards fitted rather than a prior announcement of that encounter.",
          "The remainder of the volume was not read in the run that opened this protocol, and it is precisely there that a failing case would be found if one is recorded at all. The state is therefore procedure-without-cases and not cases-without-deviation: the search has begun and has not been carried."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/inuit-angakkuq.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "One recorded remarkable event in the bearer's presence that he did not announce beforehand. The claim says always, so a single unannounced case refutes it as stated. A weaker form, that such announcements occur more often than chance would give, would require announcements collected before the events they name, with the failures kept alongside the hits.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-31",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second of the two stages, to which the six yogas belong."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CompletionStage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vollendungsstufe",
        "en": "Completion stage"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The medieval transmission of prayers and figures held to confer the liberal arts and a retentive memory upon a practitioner who keeps a stated schedule."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ArsNotoria",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ars Notoria",
        "en": "Ars Notoria"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "One of the few pre-modern western claims formulated so that it could fail: a named capacity, a named period, a named regimen."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ars Notoria in the medieval recensions; Liber Juratus Honorii",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the goddess as sound and diagram, with an initiatory sequence tied to the levels of a single mantra."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Srividya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śrīvidyā",
        "en": "Śrīvidyā"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Yoginīhṛdaya; Nityāṣoḍaśikārṇava; Bhāskararāya, Setubandha",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The disclosure of the distinction of bitter from sweet, given in the text alongside the secrets of wisdom."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unterscheidung von bitter und süß",
        "en": "Discernment of bitter and sweet"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The preparatory rite at Agrai, held in the month Anthesterion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LesserMysteries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die kleinen Mysterien",
        "en": "The lesser mysteries"
      },
      "source": "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EleusinianMysteries"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. The manual calls it the accomplished form of the practice and says nothing in the three worlds is beyond one who holds it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka",
      "label": {
        "de": "Alleiniges Anhalten",
        "en": "Retention alone"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The third of the three parts, the knowledge of God."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Theologike",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theologikē",
        "en": "Knowledge of God"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Anishinaabe medicine society, with graded degrees and a mnemonic record incised on birchbark."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Midewiwin",
      "label": {
        "de": "Midewiwin",
        "en": "Midewiwin"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in the report of nineteen ninety-one for the first time, pages 143 to about 180 of the printed text: the account of the society, of the initiation, of the four degrees and of the pictographic charts. The song scrolls, the medicine formulas and the extensive appended vocabularies were not read. The recorder worked among the Ojibwa in eighteen eighty-seven to eighteen eighty-nine; his chief informants are named in the report, the principal one a second-degree priest of Mille Lacs first initiated about eighteen thirty.",
          "The birchbark scrolls are a written transmission by their own means, which puts this tradition closer to the project's source rule than most of the registry it came from.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and not as absent. What is disputed is the age of the society itself, not a route to any tradition this graph holds: one side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, the other points to a dated scroll fragment from before any documented European contact in the region. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all. Note that the axis of the dispute and the axis a convergence asks about are not the same axis; see prima-materia#101."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The birchbark scroll record; Hoffman, The Midewiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa",
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 143-300",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from terrestrial phenomena."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheEarth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Erde",
        "en": "Signs of the earth"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A capacity ascribed to a figure who is not available to report it, in a historian's account of what was told of him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AristeasJourney",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Seelenreise, Aristeas zugeschrieben",
        "en": "The soul-journey ascribed to Aristeas"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SoulJourney"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": [
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The eastern European transmission of devekut as a continuous attachment attainable within ordinary life, and of contemplation as its means."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hasidism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chassidismus",
        "en": "Hasidism"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Toledot Yaakov Yosef; Keter Shem Tov; Shneur Zalman, Tanya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a person is a machine governed by outward influences, that everything happens and nobody does anything, and that in order to do it is first necessary to be; together with the account of a person as lacking unity and consisting of a multiplicity of I's rather than one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DoingPresupposesBeing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Es wird nichts getan, solange niemand da ist, der tut",
        "en": "Nothing is done until there is someone to do it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It bears directly on the disagreement recorded in convergences/left-hand-path-divergence.ttl. Both sides there dispute what becomes of the self at the end; this source holds that at the beginning there is no single self to be kept or surrendered, and that one has to be made first. That is not a third answer to their question but a claim that the question has a precondition neither states.",
          "The interest for this graph is the order of precedence. The claim is not that people do the wrong thing but that the doer is not yet assembled, which puts every question of method downstream of a question about who is applying it. A path chosen by a multiplicity is chosen by whichever part happens to be present."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A finding of an older school, transmitted rather than argued.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the first talks on the human machine and in the chapter on the absence of unity",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The songs belonging to a rite, held by the priest of the rite and his assistants, containing expressions that are obsolete or without current meaning and that have therefore to be carried by memory rather than by sense."
      },
      "id": "pmp:NavajoSacredSong",
      "label": {
        "de": "Navajo-Kultgesang",
        "en": "Navajo sacred song"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Platonic dialogues and the ascent they describe from particular to intelligible beauty."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Platonic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Platonische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Platonic tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition the Symposium was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The Stephanus pagination is stable across the modern editions, so the citations locate the text; what is unknown is whether the Greek or a translation was read."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 210a-212a",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the contents of another consciousness, said to follow from saṃyama on the presented idea."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins",
        "en": "Knowledge of another mind"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "20th century",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of the eight limbs as a ladder that must be climbed in order, each limb a precondition of the next."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SerialReadingOfTheEightLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die serielle Lesart der acht Glieder",
        "en": "The serial reading of the eight limbs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The inner and outer categories come from the commentarial layer, and the hardening of the ranking into a rule of practice belongs to twentieth-century yoga pedagogy. Recorded here so that the reading is available as what it is. The base text at II.29 enumerates and does not order."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "The commentarial tradition, sharpened in twentieth-century yoga pedagogy",
      "pm:ordersClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsForesight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Grad bringt den Blick in das Künftige hervor",
        "en": "The second degree yields the look into futurity"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideForesight"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Indian tantric completion-stage practices in Tibet, of which the six yogas of Nāropa are the best documented cycle."
      },
      "id": "pmt:TibetanTantric",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tibetisch-tantrische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Tibetan tantric tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Tsongkhapa and Bar do thos grol citations name the translation they were read in. For Patrul Rinpoche's Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The harvest has been carried out for the works actually opened, and what was left unread is recorded. The state claims completeness for what was opened, never for the corpus."
      },
      "id": "pm:corpusHarvested",
      "label": {
        "de": "Korpus geerntet",
        "en": "Corpus harvested"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Cuban transmission of the orichas, carrying the Yoruba divinatory corpus in a shortened form alongside Catholic imagery."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LucumiSanteria",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lucumí",
        "en": "Lucumí"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Its relation to Ifá is a documented case of a corpus surviving a forced transfer with measurable loss, which is examinable rather than merely regrettable."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Cabrera, El Monte; the published libretas",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, made within a tradition, that one step of a path presupposes another: that a practice, state or capacity is not reached except by way of something else."
      },
      "id": "pm:Presupposing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Voraussetzen",
        "en": "Presupposing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A node and not an edge, for the same reason as pm:Attributing and pm:Yielding. A prerequisite claim differs enormously in what carries it, and the difference is not visible on an edge. It matters whether a text sets an order or a reader inferred one from the order of the chapters."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Giftorakel bringt die Benennung des Hexers hervor",
        "en": "The poison oracle yields the naming of the witch"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 35-36 and pp. 141-143",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Every death is held to be caused by witchcraft and to be avenged by lethal magic, and the death of the witch so struck is in turn avenged by his own kin. The transmission keeps the names of those killed by avenging magic secret between the kin and the prince, and the source states that were the names known the whole procedure would stand exposed as futile. Some Azande are reported as doubting the honesty of the princes who hold the oracles, and a few as having seen that the system is fallacious."
      },
      "id": "pmc:VengeanceSystemVeiledBySilence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Racheverfahren trägt nur, solange die Opfer ungenannt bleiben",
        "en": "The vengeance system holds only while the victims are unnamed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The defence offered when the recorder put the objection is recorded with it: a prince whose oracle has declared that one man died of another's vengeance-magic will refuse to put the second death to his oracle at all, so that the chain is cut by the authority that holds the instrument.",
          "The strongest self-warning in this file, and one reported as spoken rather than inferred. It names the exact condition under which the transmission's own central procedure collapses, and names the arrangement by which that condition is kept from arising."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 6",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Eight bodily signs held to arise from the inner constitution rather than from will: paralysis, perspiration, horripilation, change of voice, trembling, change of colour, tears, and fainting."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SattvikaBhava",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unwillkürliche Körperzustände",
        "en": "Involuntary bodily states"
      },
      "source": "Nāṭyaśāstra VII",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Called the offspring of apatheia and the keeper of the door to the contemplation of created things."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Agape",
      "label": {
        "de": "Liebe",
        "en": "Love"
      },
      "source": [
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The narrator relates that her father, called a great shaman, understood hidden things and perceived that the children she wanted to run out and play with were the souls of her dead brothers and sisters, whereupon his helping spirits set a barrier between her and them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FatherPerceivesHiddenThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dem Vater der Erzählerin wird zugeschrieben, die verborgenen Dinge verstanden zu haben",
        "en": "The narrator's father is said to have understood hidden things"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The case is worth the node because of its shape rather than its content. The perception is not reported as a vision or a journey but as a correction of an ordinary judgement: the child saw children, the shaman saw who they were. A claim in that form is answerable in a way that a claim about an inner state is not."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitHiddenThings"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": "The narrator Takornâq's father, named in the narrative as a great shaman",
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 23-24",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is said to arise, on the wearing away of impurity, and to continue up to discriminative discernment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightOfKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Leuchten der Erkenntnis",
        "en": "The light of knowledge"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaSrotra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama bringt das göttliche Ohr hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama yields the divine ear"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AraqielTeachesSignsOfTheEarth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Araqiel offenbart die Zeichen der Erde",
        "en": "Araqiel discloses the signs of the earth"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheEarth"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Araqiel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account, given by a named Māori authority in a letter, that what comes back from a third party for a valuable passed on is the hau of the valuable first received, and that keeping it back for oneself would be fatal to the keeper."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HauOfTheGift",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Hau der Gabe",
        "en": "The hau of the gift"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held at mediated attestation because the letter is an archival manuscript and was read in the study that prints and collates it, not in the archive. The published version of 1909 could not be opened in this run either; the paths tried are recorded in prima-materia#106. This node is therefore an open post and is to be raised to textual attestation when either the manuscript or the 1909 printing is read."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "Manuka Hēnare, \"Ko te hau tēnā o tō taonga…\": The words of Ranapiri on the spirit of gift exchange and economy, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457",
      "source": "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A questioner addresses the oracle while a second man, the operator, administers a measured number of doses of the poison benge to a fowl. The address alternates: sentences framing the matter so that an affirmative answer is asked for and ending with the command that the oracle kill the fowl, then sentences framing it so that a negative answer is asked for and ending with the command that it spare the fowl. Whether the death of the fowl means yes or no is therefore decided by the form of the question and not by the death."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung des Giftorakels",
        "en": "Consulting the poison oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The operators are generally boys between twelve and sixteen, old enough to keep the food taboos and young enough to keep the sexual one. The consultation is held in public, and the bystanders may interrupt the questioner to have a point put that he has left out."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 136-138 and p. 153",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act, within a tradition, of warning against a capacity, a practice, or the pursuit of either."
      },
      "id": "pm:Cautioning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Warnen",
        "en": "Cautioning"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node rather than as commentary, because what a tradition warns against is part of what it teaches. A record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports the tradition it claims to hold."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is not open to ordinary sight: the state of the sick, the whereabouts of what is lost, the presence of the dead among the living. The tradition treats this as a domain that some persons perceive and that others are ill advised to form opinions about."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitHiddenThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die verborgenen Dinge",
        "en": "Hidden things"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Relevant to prima-materia#127, which asks whether a perception of the distant, hidden or concealed is attested outside the spheres already held. The passage here attests the domain and one case of its perception; it does not attest a procedure for acquiring it, and that part is not in the portion read."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19, 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A transmission resting on one man's sustained reports of conversation with the dead and with angels, kept as a dated journal over decades."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Swedenborgian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Swedenborgische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Swedenborgian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Quantitatively the largest body of first-person reports available to the project, and dated throughout, which makes internal consistency checkable in a way most such material is not."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Geʿez transmission of protective scrolls, of names held to bind, and of a canon that preserves works lost everywhere else."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Äthiopisch-orthodoxe Zaubertradition",
        "en": "Ethiopian Orthodox magical transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "African, literate, canonical, and already present in the graph at one remove: the fullest witness of the Enochic book the graph holds is the Ethiopian one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Lefāfa Ṣedeq; the asmāt scroll corpus; the Geʿez canon",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AzazelTeachesAdornment",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azazel offenbart Schmuck und Kosmetik",
        "en": "Azazel discloses adornment and cosmetics"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Azazel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The vital breath or energy held to circulate in the body and to be susceptible of deliberate direction."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Qi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Qi",
        "en": "Qi"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the corpus of this tradition has been carried into the graph."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Tradition",
      "id": "pm:coverageState",
      "label": {
        "en": "coverage state"
      },
      "range": "pm:CoverageState",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of mountain austerity, in which power is held to follow from ordeals performed at named places in a fixed order."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shugendo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shugendō",
        "en": "Shugendō"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The self-mummification practice gives the project something almost nothing else does: a stated procedure with a documented outcome and documented failures, both counted."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Shozan engi; the Shugendō shūyō himitsu shū",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the corpus of a tradition has been carried into the graph. The scale exists so that a transmission nobody has opened and a transmission whose corpus is genuinely thin never read alike."
      },
      "id": "pm:CoverageState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erschließungsstand",
        "en": "Coverage state"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The enumerative transmission of the evolutes of nature and of a witnessing principle distinct from them, on which the yoga of the graph already rests."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Samkhya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sāṃkhya",
        "en": "Sāṃkhya"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Īśvarakṛṣṇa, Sāṃkhyakārikā; Yuktidīpikā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the striking of spirits, the killing of the embryo and the serpent's bite."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kasdeja",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kasdeja",
        "en": "Kasdeja"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The named author with whom the claim begins."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Originating",
      "id": "pm:originatedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "aufgestellt von",
        "en": "originated by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SarielTeachesSignsOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sariel offenbart die Zeichen des Mondes",
        "en": "Sariel discloses the signs of the moon"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Given under the name Sahriel; stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the course of the moon."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheMoon"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Sariel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArmarosTeachesLoosingOfSpells",
      "label": {
        "de": "Armaros offenbart das Lösen von Beschwörungen",
        "en": "Armaros discloses the loosing of spells"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:LoosingOfSpells"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Armaros"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that several traditions mark the passage between sleeping and waking as the place where a communication is received, while none of them makes it the place where a faculty is exercised."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThresholdReceptionCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schwelle als Ort des Empfangs",
        "en": "The threshold as a place of reception"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two axes have to be held apart, and the modern draft that prompted this examination runs them together. On the reception axis the threshold is well attested: a voice, a light and an immobility of the eyes in the Neoplatonic account, the nightly ascent of the soul in the Kabbalistic one, the true dream in the Islamic one. On the execution axis, where sight, hearing and the mind-made body are actively exercised, the sources bind the capacities to waking absorption instead. No source found so far puts an actively exercised faculty in the threshold state."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:ClearLightOfSleep"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "The correspondence must not be counted as independent attestation. The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa and is reception, as the file that holds it records. The Mediterranean witnesses are worse still for this purpose: the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory rests on a Neoplatonic substrate, so an agreement between the Greek, the Jewish and the Islamic account is a shared inheritance and not three independent observations. What would count as a finding is a tradition outside both blocks, and none has been located.",
      "source": [
        "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How a path stands to the norm of the order it is transmitted in: whether its operative means keep that norm, deliberately break it, or decline the distinction. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."
      },
      "id": "pm:MethodOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung der Methode",
        "en": "Method Orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Absence of pm:methodOrientation on a node means the axis was not determined for it. It does not mean the middle value, and it does not mean pm:normDistinctionDeclined, which is a positive claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other.",
          "Held apart from pm:TelicOrientation deliberately, and that separation is the whole reason both classes exist. The two vary independently: a path can take the forbidden as its vehicle and still aim at dissolution into what exceeds the self. A scheme with one axis cannot state that case, and the terms that carry one axis — right-hand path, left-hand path — therefore report a transgressive means as though it were a separative goal. See prima-materia#52."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account of a final state in which the mother returns within the fullness and receives the saviour as her spouse; those who call themselves spiritual are stripped of their souls, made purely intellectual spirits, and become brides of spiritual angels; the creator, being merely animal, moves into the mother's place; and the souls of the righteous rest in an intermediate place."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ValentinianConsummation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vollendung als Hochzeit, nicht als Verschmelzung",
        "en": "The consummation as a marriage rather than a merging"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Placed on the telic axis as pm:conjugalTelos. The placement is a reading and carries the same burden as the claim it sits on: it rests on the bridal state reported in the passage, which has two parties and therefore neither one term nor an absolute. It is stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted.",
          "Recorded because it does not fit either end of the axis it is regularly assigned to. The spiritual ones are not dissolved: they are stripped and transformed, but a bridal state presupposes two, so what is transformed persists as a party to it. Nor are they made into absolutes of their own: they are married, not set apart. The end is a union that keeps what it unites, and that is a third case, distinct from the one in pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution, where the creature stays with itself against dissolution rather than entering a twoness. See prima-materia#70 and prima-materia#52.",
          "The passage is a report by an opponent and is attested as such. What can be said on this source is that the doctrine was ascribed in this form in the second century, not that its holders stated it so. See the caution on pmt:ValentinianGnosis.",
          "Two further things in the same passage that a summary loses. The demiurge is not destroyed but relocated, which makes the account less anticosmic than its modern reception; and the souls of the righteous, who are not of the spiritual class, are neither saved into the fullness nor lost, but given a place between. A scheme with two outcomes cannot hold either detail."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:telicOrientation": {
        "id": "pm:conjugalTelos"
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I, in the account of the Valentinian consummation, at the passage on the mother's return, the brides of the angels and the removal of the creator (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ValentinianGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A centre of the subtle body, described in the source with its seed syllables and its position along the central channel."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Cakra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Cakra",
        "en": "Cakra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The count varies across the older tantras, which give four, five or six. The tradition does not assign the centres the psychological qualities that modern presentations attach to them."
      },
      "source": "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Ohr",
        "en": "Divine ear"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transfer of the seat of awareness into a body other than one's own."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eintritt in einen anderen Körper",
        "en": "Entering another body"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SixteenStepsOfBreathing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sechzehn Schritte der Atemachtsamkeit",
        "en": "The sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The discourse sets out four tetrads of four steps and assigns them to the four foundations of mindfulness. It presents a progression; it does not say that a step is unreachable without the one before it, and the question whether the order is binding or expository is contested among practitioners to this day."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:presentationOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "source": "Majjhima Nikāya 118",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā of Svātmārāma and the manuals around it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hathayoga",
      "label": {
        "de": "Haṭhayoga",
        "en": "Haṭhayoga"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Every verse cited in this file was read in the Sanskrit of the widely circulated vulgate, in the recension that the 1915 edition and translation of Sinh carries, and each citation now says so. The critical edition drawn from the manuscript tradition numbers differently in places; pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute holds what is at issue."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Roman emperor to whom the Alexandrian healings are ascribed by two independent historians."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Vespasian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vespasian",
        "en": "Vespasian"
      },
      "source": "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:InitiationPresupposesSaktipata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Initiation setzt die Herabkunft der Kraft voraus",
        "en": "Initiation presupposes the descent of power"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The exegesis asks who is qualified for initiation and answers by taking up the descent of power, which places the descent before the rite and before the path. Later and other traditions have read the descent as the fruit of practice instead; the direction recorded here is the one Abhinavagupta sets."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Diksa"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Saktipata"
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The East Syriac transmission of solitary prayer, of the stages of stillness and of the wonder held to interrupt prayer rather than complete it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SyriacAsceticism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Syrische Askese",
        "en": "Syriac ascetic transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Isaac of Nineveh, Ascetical Homilies; John of Dalyatha, Letters; Evagrius, Kephalaia Gnostica in the Syriac recension",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KhecariYieldsRetention",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die über den Gaumen gelegte Zunge bringt das Zurückhalten des Nektars hervor",
        "en": "The tongue set above the palate yields the retention of the nectar"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The contact at the palate is attested, and what it is said to do is stop a descent. It is not bound to a phase of breathing anywhere in the chapter, and it is not said to carry anything upward. The same holds for the seal at the throat, which is described as closing off a downward flow. A scheme that pairs the palate with inhalation and with upward carriage takes a stopping and reports it as a conveyance. See prima-materia#28."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Khecari"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.42 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the one part of the account that leaves objects a later worker can count and date without asking anybody: the great masks carved for each sixtieth-year ceremony and kept in rock shelters beside their predecessors, and the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each and hung in the hogon's house. The account infers from series of nine, of eight and of fourteen that ceremonies were held back to the fifteenth and, on one reading, the twelfth century."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Masken- und Fermentträgerserien als datierbarer Nachweis der Zeremonie",
        "en": "The mask and ferment-holder series as a datable record of the ceremony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered because it is the falsifiable half of the account and the half nobody examined. The astronomical claims cannot be tested against anything but memory; wood and fibre can be dated. The protocol asks whether the intervals between the objects of a single shelter are compatible with sixty years, and whether the oldest are as old as the inference requires.",
          "No case series has been collected here and none is known to this graph. The state is therefore procedure without cases and not cases without deviation: nobody has carried the examination, which is a different report from having carried it and found nothing. See prima-materia#253 for the radiocarbon and dendrochronological work on Bandiagara material that would have to be searched before this protocol can be advanced."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A dated series from one shelter whose intervals depart from sixty years beyond what the dating method's error admits, or whose oldest object is materially younger than the count of objects requires. A single shelter dated cleanly against the count would equally carry the claim.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-01",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Sigui"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named in the Ethiopic teaching list as the one who disclosed the knowledge of the clouds."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ezeqeel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ezeqeel",
        "en": "Ezeqeel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The middle-way school of Buddhist philosophy founded on the verses of Nāgārjuna and its commentarial literature."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Madhyamaka",
      "label": {
        "de": "Madhyamaka",
        "en": "Madhyamaka"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: to the Śaiva and Śākta material a route exists and is not in dispute, so a correspondence with it would be reception and not a finding; towards the Greek or the Western esoteric material none is documented. Edition: the verses are read in Stcherbatsky's translation of 1927, named on the node, which carries the commentary of Candrakīrti alongside them; the Sanskrit has not been consulted. Number of citable passages: one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (trans. Th. Stcherbatsky, The Conception of Buddhist Nirvāṇa, Leningrad 1927)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative means keep the norm of the order the path is transmitted in: what is prohibited there stays prohibited here."
      },
      "id": "pm:conformingMethod",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ordnungstreue Methode",
        "en": "Conforming method"
      },
      "type": "pm:MethodOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to touch friends and enemies at a distance, however remote, for good or for evil, marked in the chart by lines drawn from the hands; alongside it the ability to traverse all space, marked by lines from the feet."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideDistantTouch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Berühren in der Ferne, zum Guten wie zum Bösen",
        "en": "Touching at a distance for good or ill"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account that the eighth supreme chief, having discovered the star, escaped the sacrifice his seven predecessors had undergone by feigning death, returned claiming to have been inside the star and to know its secrets, and then taught men its characteristics and reordered the reckoning of time."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EighthHogonTeaching",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der achte Häuptling lehrte die Menschen die Merkmale des Sterns",
        "en": "The eighth chief taught men the characteristics of the star"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "An ascription inside a myth of origin, and the article gives it as such: it is offered expressly as an indication of the changes and combinations of reckoning that appear in the course of what the recorders put in quotation marks as the history of the Blacks."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": "the eighth supreme chief, in the Dogon account of the origin of the sixty-year reckoning",
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-284",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Brazilian transmission of the orixás, with an initiation that seats a spirit in the head of the initiate."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Candomble",
      "label": {
        "de": "Candomblé",
        "en": "Candomblé"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Bastide, Le candomblé de Bahia; Verger, Notes sur le culte des Orisa et Vodun",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement between the recorders about what happens at the moment the whole transmission turns on. The indigenous chronicler of sixteen fifteen says outright that these priests deceived men, saying that the huacas eat and drink and speak, which they did not do. The extirpation account of sixteen twenty-one says that the one who speaks with the huaca answers the people what he pretends it tells him, and then adds that sometimes the devil does speak to them through the stone. The earlier report of about fifteen seventy-five says without qualification that they had many huacas and temples where the devil gave his answers."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhetherTheHuacaSpeaks",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Huaca spricht oder der Priester für sie",
        "en": "Whether the huaca speaks or the priest speaks for it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The chronicler's denial has a provenance he states himself: he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. Whether that makes his denial better evidence, as an insider's, or worse, as a man writing for the side that was burning them, is exactly what a later reader has to weigh, and it is why the sentence is in this note.",
          "The disagreement is not the ordinary one between a believer and a sceptic. All three recorders are Christians who hold the huacas to be nothing or to be demons, and the one who denies the speaking outright is the one who is himself Andean. What separates them is whether the office rests on an event or on a performance, and that is a question about the transmission and not about their theology.",
          "What the three do agree on is that few laymen ever saw the principal huaca and that only the specialist went forward and spoke. That much is common ground between the denial and the two affirmations, and it is entered separately as its own node."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmp:MocharTheHuaca"
      },
      "source": [
        "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v",
        "Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno (1615), Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 2232 4°, p. 280 [282], the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the huacapvillac"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese office of yin-yang divination and calendrical prohibition, practised by a licensed bureau of the state."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Onmyodo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Onmyōdō",
        "en": "Onmyōdō"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hoki naiden; the onmyō provisions of the Engishiki",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission divides dreams into witchcraft-dreams and oracular dreams, a nightmare being of the first kind and a pleasant dream of the second. A bad dream is held to be not a symbol of witchcraft but an actual undergoing of it: in waking life a man learns that he was bewitched only from a subsequent misfortune or from an oracle, but in a dream he sees the witches themselves and may speak with them. The soul is held to leave the body in sleep, and the witch to send the soul of his witchcraft to eat the soul of his victim while both sleep."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WitchcraftDreamIsExperienceNotSymbol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein böser Traum ist eine Erfahrung der Hexerei, kein Zeichen für sie",
        "en": "A bad dream is an experience of witchcraft, not a sign of it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because it stands squarely against a claim this graph holds open elsewhere, that the figures met at the threshold of sleep are the signature of the sleeper's own state and not visitors. Here the transmission asserts the opposite in as many words. It is one side and not a refutation; the other side is not in this file, so no dispute node is written."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix II, pp. 230-231",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A warning against treating the given states as a further ladder: they descend and pass, and no course of effort produces them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:StatesAreNotOrdered",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zustände werden nicht der Reihe nach erlangt",
        "en": "The states are not attained in order"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:Ahwal"
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SixtyYearReckoningPresupposesTheDiscovery",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Sechzigjahres-Comput setzt die Entdeckung des Same-Sterns voraus",
        "en": "The sixty-year reckoning presupposes the discovery of the seed-star"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable, and not because the sequence is historical. What the passage establishes is that the tradition itself makes the calendar depend on the star, which is the claim the outside reception took over and the claim the examination addresses."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The account states what held before the discovery: the year was not yet fixed as a unit, the only reckoning known was by seven harvests, and at the end of the seventh the supreme chief was sacrificed. The consequence of the prior step being absent is therefore named twice over, as a defect of the calendar and as a death.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Sigui"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-283",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:LegalActionPresupposesPoisonOracle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rechtsverfolgung setzt das Giftorakel voraus",
        "en": "Legal action presupposes the poison oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is stated flatly and is not a matter of degree: the action cannot be brought. The passage dates from the period of the monograph, in which the declarations of the oracle had already ceased to be recognized as evidence in the courts the administration had set up, so the necessity is the transmission's own and not the colonial law's."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Legal action cannot be taken without a decision of the poison oracle."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KokabelTeachesSignsOfTheStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kokabel offenbart die Zeichen der Sterne",
        "en": "Kokabel discloses the signs of the stars"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the constellations."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheStars"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Kokabel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second of the three parts, the contemplation of created things."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Physike",
      "label": {
        "de": "Physikē",
        "en": "Contemplation of nature"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act, within one tradition, of taking over a claim from another and adding to it something the source it came from does not contain: a term, a consequence, a caution, a sign, a condition of failure."
      },
      "id": "pm:Reworking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bearbeiten",
        "en": "Reworking"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Deliberately expensive: pm:addedElement points at a node, so the addition has to be entered before the reworking can be claimed. A station that added something and cannot say what added nothing that this graph can use. As with pm:Testing, absence is informative and is not to be stubbed: no instance means nobody has shown an addition, not that none was made.",
          "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which is an ordering act performed by an editor in the present and named as his. A reworking is an act inside a transmission, attested in a work like any other claim, and it belongs to the tradition that performed it.",
          "The class exists because reception is two things and the record could not tell them apart. Textual transmission is somebody copying a sentence, and it shows that the sentence was handed on. Operative uptake is somebody taking the instruction, carrying it out, and afterwards writing what was not in the exemplar. Only the second turns a chain of copies into a series of attempts, and only the second is proof against feedback: what makes the Dogon Sirius case weak is not that it is received but that nothing was added at the second station which was not already in the question.",
          "This is therefore the quantity worth counting, and it is not the number of independent witnesses. A tradition that takes over a claim about an effect and attaches a condition of failure to it has delivered more than three that pass it on word for word — and the addition is the material a pm:falsifiedBy is made from, so the examining layer comes out of the reception history rather than against it."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AzazelTeachesMetalworking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azazel offenbart Metallverarbeitung",
        "en": "Azazel discloses metalworking"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Metalworking"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Azazel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BaraqelTeachesSignsOfLightning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Baraqel offenbart die Zeichen der Blitze",
        "en": "Baraqel discloses the signs of the lightning flashes"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with astrology."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfLightning"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Baraqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act the whole transmission turns on. The priest sits on the ground, extends the left hand towards the huaca, names it, and makes with the lips the sucking sound that is what the word properly means; he says that he comes and brings what its children and creatures offer it, asks it to receive them and not be angry and to give life and health and good fields, pours chicha before it and sometimes over it, smears it with the blood of guinea pigs or llamas, and burns or blows the rest of the offerings."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MocharTheHuaca",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mochar: der Akt der Anrede",
        "en": "Mochar: the act of address"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder states expressly that to have mochado a huaca does not mean to have seen it or knelt before it, because the adoration does not consist in that. It consists in having given the offering to the priest at the appointed place and joined the invocation with the left hand raised and open. That correction is his, and it is the most useful sentence in the chapter: it says that the act is participation and not sight, which is what makes the next node possible."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Indian alchemical transmission of mercurial preparations held to make the body proof against decay, with named substances, processes and durations."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Rasashastra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Rasaśāstra",
        "en": "Rasaśāstra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Among the most testable claims available anywhere in the corpus, and testable in two directions at once: the promised outcome and the toxicity of the means."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rasārṇava; Govindabhagavatpāda, Rasahṛdayatantra; Vāgbhaṭa, Rasaratnasamuccaya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The session in which a shaman calls up his helping spirits and puts a question to them, held in the household on the occasion that requires it rather than at a fixed time."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitSpiritCalling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Geisterrufen",
        "en": "Spirit calling"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The portion read gives the occasions and the outcomes and not the procedure. Nothing was found here about the posture, the words, the darkness or the binding that the later chapters of this volume are known to describe, and none of that is asserted by this node."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A numbered course of mastery over the meditation objects, among them the order of the kasiṇa and its reverse, skipping the absorption, skipping the object, and the definition of the object."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FourteenfoldMastery",
      "label": {
        "de": "Beherrschung des Geistes in vierzehn Weisen",
        "en": "Control of the mind in fourteen ways"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.2 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When a man or woman is struck by a sudden illness, loses judgement and is left as if mad, the senior ministers say that this has come because the huacas want him for their villac and priest; and on his coming to himself they make him fast and learn the office. The ground given is that they themselves lose judgement when they speak with the huacas. From this all who fall in fits are counted as chosen for these ministries."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SeizureTakenAsCall",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein plötzlicher Anfall gilt als Ruf ins Amt",
        "en": "A sudden seizure is taken as a call to the office"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The transmission gives its own explanation of the sign, and the explanation is an inference from the practitioner's state back to the novice's: the office is recognised by the symptom that the office produces. The recorder adds his own competing explanation, that the loss of judgement comes from the devil or from the strength of the chicha they drink before speaking with the huaca. Both are recorded; neither is the graph's."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A lost renunciant transmission teaching a fixed destiny through which every being passes, known only through the reports of its opponents."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Ajivika",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ājīvika-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Ājīvika transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as the limiting case: a tradition with no surviving corpus of its own. Whatever it yields will be an ascription by hostile witnesses and must be recorded as one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "No transmitted corpus; the doctrine is reported in the Buddhist and Jain canons, chiefly Sāmaññaphala Sutta and the Bhagavatī Sūtra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The raising of the body hair, counted among the eight involuntary signs and treated as a mark of an inner state, not as something to be produced for its own sake."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Romanca",
      "label": {
        "de": "Haaraufrichtung",
        "en": "Horripilation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Attested outside the dramaturgical treatise in the epic corpus, where trembling and horripilation are named together as signs of an overwhelming state."
      },
      "source": [
        "Bhagavadgītā 1.29",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Inward-turned awareness of subtle objects generated by the mind."
      },
      "id": "pm:DreamingState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumzustand (Svapna)",
        "en": "Dreaming state (Svapna)"
      },
      "source": "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 4",
      "type": "pm:AwarenessContext"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:NavajoEfficacyPresupposesExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie setzt die Genauigkeit der Gesänge voraus",
        "en": "The efficacy of the ceremony presupposes exactness in the songs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The consequence is graded rather than absolute: an error may be fatal, and only in some cases does a single syllable do irreparable harm. That grading is worth keeping, because the parallel claims elsewhere are usually reported without it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The witness says an error made in singing a song may be fatal to the efficacy of a ceremony, and that in some cases the error of a single syllable works an irreparable injury.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NavajoSongExactness"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A bounded subgraph that carries emergent meaning irreducible to its individual triples. Designed for vector embedding as a unit."
      },
      "id": "pm:MeaningCluster",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bedeutungs-Cluster",
        "en": "Meaning Cluster"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Implements the Sphoṭa principle (Bhartṛhari): meaning resides in the whole utterance, not in isolated phonemes. The subgraph is the phoneme-analog; the cluster is the sphoṭa."
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1927",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The assignment of a fixed set of seven centres to spectral colours and to psychological qualities such as will, expression and security. An ordering imposed in the twentieth century on material that did not carry it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zuordnung von sieben Zentren zu Eigenschaften",
        "en": "The seven-centre quality mapping"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Colour scheme in Leadbeater 1927, rainbow spectrum in Hills 1977, on the textual basis made available by Avalon's 1919 translation of the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa. Recorded here so that the mapping is available in the graph as what it is, and cannot be mistaken for the tantric material it was built on."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "C. W. Leadbeater, extended by Christopher Hills and Ken Dychtwald",
      "pm:ordersClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:Cakra"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. Avalon, The Serpent Power (1919)",
        "C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras (1927)",
        "Christopher Hills, Nuclear Evolution (1977)",
        "Ken Dychtwald, Bodymind (1977)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The north Indian transmission of an inner sound held to be audible under instruction, and of union without image or rite."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SantNada",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sant- und Nāda-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Sant and inner-sound transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Kabīr granthāvalī; Ādi Granth; Nāda-bindu Upaniṣad",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the creature's proper movement is to hold itself as itself against a dissolution that would end it, while denying that the movement is towards difference or towards becoming an absolute; what is sought is the creature's own being and not its distinction from others."
      },
      "id": "pm:individuatingTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Individuierendes Ziel",
        "en": "Individuating telos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Between the two older values and reducible to neither. It refuses dissolution, so it is not unitive; and it refuses the striving after difference, so it is not separative. The distinction is fine and load-bearing: a scheme that reads every refusal of dissolution as self-deification cannot state it, and that misreading is common enough to be worth a term."
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention counted in heartbeats: the breath is drawn in through the nose and held, then released in the smallest quantities through the mouth, the count being raised only by degrees."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Biqi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Verschließen des Atems",
        "en": "Closing off the breath"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A route of transmission to traditions already held is documented. Agreement with them counts as reception."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg belegt",
        "en": "Contact route documented"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Stoic doctrine of a fiery breath that pervades matter and holds it together."
      },
      "id": "pmt:StoicPhysics",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stoische Physik",
        "en": "Stoic physics"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition of Galen the fragment was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The section numbering of De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis differs between the older editions and the critical one, so V.3.8 locates the passage only in the latter."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Heat held to be generated within the body by deliberate practice rather than received from outside it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InnerHeat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Innere Hitze",
        "en": "Inner heat"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:JourneyYieldsDistantSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reise bringt das Wissen um den fernen Ort hervor",
        "en": "The journey yields the knowledge of the distant place"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DistantSight"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A count of the corroborated consultations printed in the source: how often the second test fell opposite to the first, as the transmission's rule of validity requires, and how often it fell alike and voided the verdict."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeCorroborationProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rate, mit der der Bestätigungstest scheitert",
        "en": "The rate at which the corroborative test fails"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Of the two seances printed, the first carries two corroborated pairs and both are recorded as contradictory: a question about taking on the taboos of vengeance against a question about the questioner's own survival under them, and a question about a man's safety in a named settlement against its explicit corroboration. The second seance carries one explicitly corroborative question, and it agreed; the recorder calls that seance a wholly successful consultation. Three pairs, two of them void by the transmission's own rule.",
          "The count is the recorder's material and the counting is this graph's. He states outright that the seances are given so that the reader may judge for himself the proportion of fowls that die and the order of deaths and survivals, so the arithmetic is invited by the source and is not imposed on it.",
          "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is from the transmission's own stated requirement rather than from a base rate of chance. A base rate is deliberately not entered: the poison is administered in a number of doses that varies with the size of the fowl and with the operator's reading of its earlier answers, so the probability that a given fowl dies is not fixed and no honest chance rate can be stated from what was read.",
          "Three pairs decide nothing about a rate and the number is entered so that a later reader can see how small it is. What it does establish is that the falsifying condition is not hypothetical: it occurred in two of the three cases this graph has actually read, and the source says elsewhere that the oracle frequently kills or spares both fowls.",
          "What the deviation is not. It is not evidence against the oracle in the transmission's own terms, because the transmission assigns every occurrence of the condition to one of eight causes that leave the instrument untouched. That is why the finding of this protocol is about the standing of the falsifying condition inside the transmission and not about the oracle's accuracy, which nothing read here measures."
        ]
      },
      "pm:caseCount": 3,
      "pm:evidenceFrom": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), the two seances printed at pp. 141-143",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "The Azande, by the corroborative second test their own rule prescribes; the two consultations counted here were recorded and printed at length by E. E. Evans-Pritchard, who was present at both",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The two tests of one consultation fall alike, both fowls dying or both surviving, so that by the transmission's own rule the verdict is contradictory and void."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A subtle vehicle carried by the soul, whose purity is held to decide whether what appears in a dream is clear and true or clouded."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PneumaticVehicle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das pneumatische Seelenfahrzeug",
        "en": "The pneumatic vehicle of the soul"
      },
      "source": "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source instructs that one comes before the other, without saying what follows from disregarding it."
      },
      "id": "pm:prescribedOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Angeordnete Reihenfolge",
        "en": "Prescribed order"
      },
      "type": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An operative procedure enacted within a tradition."
      },
      "id": "pm:Practicing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Praktizieren",
        "en": "Practicing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that the naming of two Śākta branches as a right-hand and a left-hand ritual reports a division the Indian material carries, rather than imposing one it does not."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BinaryPredatesItsReportersExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Zweiteilung älter ist als ihre Berichterstatter",
        "en": "Whether the two-way division is older than the reports of it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No case answering the condition has been found so far, and two passages read for other purposes tell against it. The Kulārṇava gives a ladder of seven steps, not a pair, and ranks the left above the right; the Mahānirvāṇa divides practitioners into three dispositions rather than two paths. Neither was sought as a counterexample, which is what makes them worth recording here — they came out of a search for something else.",
          "The modern scholarship that argues the same point — the construction of the category by nineteenth-century orientalism — has not been read and is therefore not cited at pm:evidenceFrom. Naming it unread would make this protocol look better supported than it is. See prima-materia#71.",
          "What this protocol does not establish. It has not searched the Indian corpus exhaustively, and absence of a case in two texts is not absence in the corpus. The state is procedure-without-cases and not cases-without-deviation, and the difference matters: the first says the search has begun, the second would say it has been carried."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/saiva-sakta.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A pre-colonial Indian source that presents dakṣiṇa and vāma as a clean two-way division with a moral polarity, using union with the divine against self-deification as the criterion that separates them. One such passage refutes the claim that the division was imposed rather than found.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-29",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Turkic and Mongol transmission of a sky power that confers mandate and fortune, and of practitioners who address it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:InnerAsianTengrism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Innerasiatische Tengri-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Inner Asian sky transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Secret History of the Mongols; the Orkhon inscriptions; the published Manas cycle",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account in which one twin tore away a piece of the placenta that became the earth, was turned into the pale fox for it, and communicated his own impurity to the earth so that it became dry and barren; his fate is to pursue his twin, who is also his female soul, until the end of time. On the mythical plane the seed-star is this fox, held in space and turning tirelessly around Sirius, that is around the pursued twin, without ever reaching her."
      },
      "id": "pmc:YuruguPursuit",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yourougou in Verfolgung Yasiguis",
        "en": "Yourougou in pursuit of Yasigui"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The pursuit that never arrives is the same figure as the orbit that never closes on its centre. Whether the star is the image of the myth or the myth the reading of the star is exactly the question the article declines to raise."
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 292",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MideDegreesPresupposeOneAnother",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jeder Grad setzt den darunter liegenden voraus",
        "en": "Each degree presupposes the one below it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passages read rather than of the society. The report sets the sequence, names the payment and the feasts due before each step, and says nothing about what happens to a man who takes a degree out of turn or without the preparation. A consequence would raise this to a necessity; none was found in the pages read. See prima-materia#119."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-170",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tummo bringt innere Hitze hervor",
        "en": "Tummo yields inner heat"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The one pairing in this file that names a capacity with an outward, measurable side. Any later protocol has a clearer target here than anywhere else in the collection."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Tummo"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InnerHeat"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1950",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a people without instruments held, as transmitted knowledge, the movements and characteristics of a star that cannot be seen: its period around Sirius, its extreme density, and its priority in creation. The claim is put forward in an article of nineteen fifty which sets the question of how such knowledge could exist and expressly declines to answer or even to pose it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Dogon kannten den Begleiter des Sirius",
        "en": "The Dogon knew the companion of Sirius"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an origination and not as a dispute, because a dispute needs two sides that each say something about the matter. Here one side says nothing: no transmission carries the identification, and the tradition file holds only the seed-star. The identification begins in the work named below.",
          "The decisive sentence is the one the reception never quotes. On p. 274 the recorders write that the question of how men without instruments know the movements and certain characteristics of practically invisible bodies has not been decided, nor even raised. That sentence grants the premise and withholds the enquiry in one move, and everything the claim later became rests on the granted premise.",
          "The later form of the claim, in which the knowledge is explained by contact with visitors from the Sirius system, begins in a work of nineteen seventy-six that has not been read at the wording in this run and is therefore not entered. See prima-materia#251.",
          "The second decisive sentence is a footnote. Where the article gives the orbital period as some fifty years, the note observes that this period is very close to that of the companion of Sirius and cites a French astronomical periodical of nineteen thirty-one for it. The identification is therefore the recorders' and is made in the recorders' own apparatus; the body of the text never says Sirius B. What the informants are reported as saying and what the note makes of it are separable on the page, and separating them is what this node exists for."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "ancient Dogon astronomy: a pre-contact African tradition holding the existence, period and density of Sirius B, cited both as evidence of a lost indigenous science and as evidence of extraterrestrial contact",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen",
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274, 282 with its first footnote, and 287",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim stands in a received work, at a passage that can be cited and read independently of this graph."
      },
      "id": "pm:textualAttestation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Textzeugnis",
        "en": "Textual attestation"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A route has been proposed and is contested in the scholarship. Agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation until the dispute is entered as its own node."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg strittig",
        "en": "Contact route disputed"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity the oracle is consulted for: to learn beforehand how a matter stands and will fall out. The answer is delivered through the channel of parables, and the account of eighteen ninety-four reports that nothing of importance is undertaken without the consultation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaForeknowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vorauswissen über den Ausgang einer Sache",
        "en": "Foreknowledge of how a matter will fall out"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShamsielTeachesSignsOfTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schamsiel offenbart die Zeichen der Sonne",
        "en": "Shamsiel discloses the signs of the sun"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheSun"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
