      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Nāṭyaśāstra and the theory of aesthetic states that follows from it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sanskritische Dramaturgie",
        "en": "Sanskrit dramaturgy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both citations the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter division of the Nāṭyaśāstra differs between the recensions, so the number seven does not by itself locate the passage on the bodily signs."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Nāṭyaśāstra VII",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stated reading of the divination: if the fork falls the sick person will die, if it stays upright she will live. In the case related the fork settled upright and the sick child lived."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitForkSignOfSurvival",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das an der Gabel gelesene Zeichen",
        "en": "The sign read from the fork"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because the sign is stated in advance and in both directions, which is rare in the material this graph holds. A divination that names its own failure condition before the outcome is known is examinable; one that is interpreted afterwards is not. The single case related is of course selected by the narrator's survival."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that was taken over, as it stands in the tradition performing the reworking."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Reworking",
      "id": "pm:reworkedClaim",
      "label": {
        "de": "bearbeitete Behauptung",
        "en": "reworked claim"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Christian Egyptian operative texts in which biblical names, angelic invocation and older Egyptian procedure stand side by side without apology."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CopticMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Koptische Zaubertradition",
        "en": "Coptic magical transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Coptic magical papyri and ostraca in the standard collections; the Testament of Solomon as its adjacent Greek witness",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seat defined by sitting evenly with the body straight; the straightness is part of what the posture is, not a separate instruction."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gerade Rumpf als Merkmal einer Sitzhaltung",
        "en": "The straight trunk as the mark of a seat"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.21 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A graded order of ways of practice in which each is declared superior to the one before: the Veda above all others, the Vaiṣṇava above the Veda, the Śaiva above the Vaiṣṇava, the Dakṣiṇa above the Śaiva, the Vāma above the Dakṣiṇa, the Siddhānta above the Vāma, the Kaula above the Siddhānta, and nothing above the Kaula."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AcaraLadder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die aufsteigende Ordnung der ācāras",
        "en": "The ascending order of the ācāras"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The chapter and verse are not recoverable from the copy read, which is an OCR of a printed translation whose running structure the scan does not carry reliably. Work, section and edition are named instead, which meets the standard of findability; a later reader working from another edition should expect a different numbering. See prima-materia#45 and prima-materia#56.",
          "This is the passage Wilson translates in 1832 without naming a place in the work, and on which pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature rests. It is now located in an edition, so the order is a claim of the tantra and no longer only a claim of its reporter.",
          "Two things follow that the Western pair of names cannot carry. The order has seven steps and not two, so a scheme with two is not a simplification of it but a replacement. And within the order the left stands above the right, so the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses its valuation. Whoever explains the modern left-hand path out of this passage explains it out of a text that ranks the left higher than the right and both below two further steps."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Kulārṇava Tantra, in the section on the superiority of the Kula-dharma, at the verses giving the order of superiority of the ācāras (text with English translation by Ram Kumar Rai, Varanasi 1999)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source says what fails to happen without the prior step: that the later step is not possible, or does not arise, or turns harmful."
      },
      "id": "pm:statedNecessity",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausdrücklich gesetzte Notwendigkeit",
        "en": "Stated necessity"
      },
      "type": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tamil transmission of bodily perfection, rejuvenation regimens and a deliberately obscure poetic idiom that resists the commentary it invites."
      },
      "id": "pmt:TamilSiddhar",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tamilische Siddhar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Tamil Siddhar transmission"
      },
