      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Tirumūlar, Tirumandiram; the Bogar and Agastyar corpora; the kāya-kalpa literature",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The least of the five degrees of rapture, described as able to raise the hairs of the body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KhuddikaPiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Geringe Verzückung",
        "en": "Minor rapture"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the passage that attests piloerection as a recognised concomitant of a meditative state, not as its object."
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Philological and historical mode of assertion: claims are reports about what a tradition holds, not claims made from within it."
      },
      "id": "pm:ScholarlyState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wissenschaftlicher Zustand",
        "en": "Scholarly state"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Default context for tradition files in Phase 1."
      },
      "type": "pm:AwarenessContext"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A concept that participates in the observed correspondence."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:convergingConcept",
      "label": {
        "de": "konvergierender Begriff",
        "en": "converging concept"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission begun by three anonymous manifestos that announced an order and a knowledge, and that produced both by being read."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Rosicrucian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Rosenkreuzerische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Rosicrucian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A tradition whose founding documents describe a body that did not exist before them. Of interest to the project precisely as a documented case of a transmission created by its own announcement."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Fama Fraternitatis; Confessio Fraternitatis; Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:GreaterPresupposeLesser",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die großen Mysterien setzen die kleinen voraus",
        "en": "The greater mysteries presuppose the lesser"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The requirement is institutional and concerns admission, not efficacy. The inscription regulates participation in both rites; nothing in it says that the greater rite would fail for one who had skipped the lesser, only that such a one is not admitted."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:GreaterMysteries"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:LesserMysteries"
      },
      "source": "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EleusinianMysteries"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two traditions assign direction inside the body to something other than inhaling and exhaling. One assigns it to two winds driven against each other in a single operation; the other to a place in a graded sequence, with descent in the middle of it and ascent at its end. Neither pairs a direction with a phase of the breath."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DirectionIsNotThePhaseOfBreathing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Richtung trägt eine gestufte Bahn, nicht die Atemphase",
        "en": "Direction is carried by a graded course, not by the phase of breathing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second correspondence sits alongside this one and is deliberately not asserted as a separate node yet: both traditions sanction the wrong handling of the breath with death, the one saying that the breath kills the practitioner who forces it, the other that going against the sequence is to die. Two death sanctions are a thinner finding than they look, since a warning of death is the commonest form a strong prohibition takes anywhere, and the two do not agree on what the fault is: one names haste and force, the other names reversal of order. Recorded here so that the next reader can weigh it rather than rediscover it.",
          "The independence is asymmetric and the node says so rather than claiming more than it has. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows, which is earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China; for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions, so its side of the correspondence cannot be declared contact-free with the same confidence. What the two share is therefore better read as one early independent witness and one late witness of uncertain independence, which is weaker than a convergence of two early ones and stronger than a citation chain."
        ]
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:XingqiDescent"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "One side carries the claim and the other has been withdrawn from it. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era, earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China, and for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions; it cannot be declared contact-free, and it was listed here as an independent attestation until this ground was required, which is what the requirement is for. Neither witness is an ethnographic gathering, so no recorder's feedback is at issue.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": {
        "id": "pmt:Daoist"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TrainingPresupposesPreliminaries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Training setzt die Vorbereitungen voraus",
        "en": "The training presupposes the preliminaries"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:GenerationStage"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Preliminaries"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that those at whose birth such words were uttered can, if they die, come to life again and return among people."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte sollen das Wiederaufleben ermöglichen",
        "en": "Words spoken at birth are said to make the return after death possible"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The interval between the act and its effect is a whole life, which puts the claim beyond any protocol that could be run on it and is worth recording as a property of the claim rather than as a defect of the record."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transfer of power from one who has it to one who does not, graded in the Kashmirian exegesis into nine degrees."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Saktipata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Herabkunft der Kraft",
        "en": "Descent of power"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sources give transfer by glance, by touch and by word. The specifically manual modality of the modern draft is not attested here."
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PenemueTeachesBitterAndSweet",
      "label": {
        "de": "Penemue offenbart bitter und süß",
        "en": "Penemue discloses bitter and sweet"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Penemue"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission founded on Claude Vorilhon's reported meeting of 1973, holding that humanity was made by a non-terrestrial people and reading scripture as the record of that making."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Raelism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Raëlismus",
        "en": "Raëlism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered because it performs the characteristic move of the block in its clearest form: it rereads received texts as accounts of contact. That reinterpretation is a claim about the older corpora and belongs in the graph beside them."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Vorilhon, Le livre qui dit la vérité; Vorilhon, Les extra-terrestres m'ont emmené sur leur planète",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second companion said to run a wider course in the same direction and the same time as the seed-star, four times lighter and larger than it, called the sun of the women, and named as the seat of the female souls of all beings living or to come. It is said to have a satellite of its own, the star of the Women."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EmmeYaTolo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Emme ya tolo, der Weibliche-Hirse-Stern",
        "en": "Emme ya tolo, the Female Sorghum star"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 287-288",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The non-dual commentarial tradition on the Vedānta corpus, represented here by the commentary on the Brahmasūtra."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AdvaitaVedanta",
      "label": {
        "de": "Advaita Vedānta",
        "en": "Advaita Vedānta"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Kept separate from the Upaniṣadic file although it comments on that corpus. A commentary is a later act of reading and states things its base text does not: what is held here is the commentator's argument, and a record that filed it under the older heading would date a ninth-century position to the age of the verses it expounds.",
          "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Greek or Chinese material; a correspondence with either would count as a finding, and none is asserted yet. Edition: the Thibaut translation of 1890 in the Sacred Books of the East, named on the node. Number of citable passages: one, read in the introductory section on superimposition."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The treatment of the state after release under four theories that are all refused: that the released one exists after death, that he does not exist, that he both exists and does not, and that he neither exists nor does not; with the parallel refusal of the corresponding four theories about the limits of the world."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourfoldNegationOfTheEndState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vierfache Verneinung des Endzustands",
        "en": "The fourfold negation of the end state"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "For the telic axis in prima-materia#52 this is the plain form of pm:selfBoundaryDeclined, and it is worth keeping distinct from the case in traditions/advaita-vedanta.ttl, where the question is not refused but assigned to a level that is itself superseded. The two look alike from a distance and are not the same move. No orientation is asserted on this node yet; the placement needs the verses on the self read as well. See prima-materia#63.",
          "The point of holding this is that it is not a third answer. The four alternatives are laid out in order to be declined together, and declining the fourth is what keeps the move from collapsing into the third: a position that said only neither would be the fourth alternative rather than the refusal of it.",
          "The same source guards against the reading that is nearest to hand. Reality is said to be neither existent nor non-existent, and the dialectic is described as negating not only every affirmation about it but every negation as well; it is expressly not the claim that the absolute is a non-entity, only that it is inaccessible to thought. A record that files this as nihilism reports the objection and not the position."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā XXV, at the four theories concerning the state after release and the four concerning the limits of the world (trans. Th. Stcherbatsky, The Conception of Buddhist Nirvāṇa, Leningrad 1927)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Madhyamaka"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from lightning, given in place of astrology in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfLightning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Blitze",
        "en": "Signs of the lightning flashes"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Undifferentiated awareness in which no object is apprehended."
      },
      "id": "pm:DeepSleepState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tiefschlafzustand (Suṣupti)",
        "en": "Deep sleep state (Suṣupti)"
      },
      "source": "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 5",
      "type": "pm:AwarenessContext"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The absorption from which the handbook has the supernormal powers proceed, and which it calls their natural plane."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourthJhana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vierte Vertiefung",
        "en": "The fourth absorption"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect the fixed prayer is held to obtain when it is delivered as required."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit des Zuni-Gebets",
        "en": "The efficacy of the Zuni prayer"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The regulation of the breath as a distinct limb of practice, following posture and preceding the withdrawal of the senses."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Pranayama",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prāṇāyāma",
        "en": "Prāṇāyāma"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.49-53",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Zande transmission of witchcraft as an inherited substance in the body, of a graded set of oracles by which it is located, and of a corroborative second question by which an oracular verdict is held valid or void."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AzandeOracle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azande-Orakelüberlieferung",
        "en": "Azande oracular transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance rather than on demand. The transmission states a condition under which its own principal oracle delivers no verdict at all, names eight causes to which a failure is attributed, and prints two consultations at length so that a reader may count the outcomes for himself. Very little else in this graph names a failure condition its own practitioners apply.",
          "Read in this run: the abridged edition of nineteen seventy-six in full for chapters I, III, V, VI, VIII, IX and X and for the appendix of terms and the appendix on dreams. Left unread in that edition: chapters IV, VII, XI, XII and XIII, on whether witches are conscious agents, on the place of witch-doctors in the society, on magic and medicines, on the association for communal magic, and on the situation of death; and the third appendix. The unabridged edition of nineteen thirty-seven was not opened: it is in copyright and stands in the large archives only as a lending copy or as a search-only index, which is a property of the work and not of the search. Every place below is therefore given from the abridged edition, and the abridgement is named in each reference because an abridgement is a selection and a later reader must be able to see that he is looking at one.",
          "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the correction is not a finding but the removal of one: the registry had recorded no route because none had been looked for. What a search finds is on both sides. Against a route: the poison oracle stands already in the earliest account, taken down at the first sustained European contact, so it cannot have entered with that contact. For a route: the same monograph places the belief in a witchcraft substance in the body among many peoples of Central and West Africa and calls Zandeland the north-eastern limit of its distribution, which is an areal connection to the West African sphere in which this graph already holds a transmission; and the corridor from the Islamic north was open from the eighteen-sixties, since the earliest witness travelled in with an ivory company out of Khartoum. Neither has been carried out for the oracular material in particular. Until it is, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation.",
          "Two further witnesses were opened for the counter-search and are cited where they carry something: the travel account of eighteen seventy-three, which is the earliest European description of these oracles and predates the anthropological literature by two generations, and the Belgian ethnography of nineteen twenty-one, which describes the same transmission from the other side of the colonial border and sixteen years earlier than the monograph. Both were read at the wording in full-text reproductions of the printed editions named in the references."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande; de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande; Larken, An account of the Zande, in Sudan Notes and Records; Lagae, Les Azande ou Niam-Niam",
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, the list of Zande terms, pp. 226-228",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the breath is drawn in over the tongue, protruded past the lips, and released through the nostrils."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Sitali",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śītalī",
        "en": "Śītalī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the disagreement is about."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Disputing",
      "id": "pm:disputedClaim",
      "label": {
        "de": "strittiger Gegenstand",
        "en": "disputed claim"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The office is not sought but suffered. The one marked for it is seized in infancy by an illness in which he is haunted by apparitions, and from these he learns, according to his years, what belongs to the art. Those seized a second time see more visions and gain greater knowledge. Those seized a third time — which is seldom without great torment or the utmost danger of life — arrive at the perfection of the art and become so knowing that without the drum they can see things at the greatest distances."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThreefoldSeizure",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die dreifache Heimsuchung",
        "en": "The threefold seizure"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A graded scale with a stated cost at each step and a stated end state, which is the material an examination is made of. The recorder's own explanation of the seizure is that the devil takes a liking to the person in infancy; that explanation is his and is not entered as the transmission's."
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account by which the society traces itself: the culture hero instructed the otter in its mysteries, gave him the sacred rattle and the drum, shot the shell into his body, and conferred the secrets on him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MidewiwinOrigin",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Ursprung der Gesellschaft und die Kette ihrer Übertragung",
        "en": "The origin of the society and the chain of its transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The chain runs through an animal and not through a lineage of men, which is why the society's own account of its age cannot be read as a genealogy and does not bear on the dispute recorded above."
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 166-167",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Associates a concept with one or more symbolic representations (sigils, diagrams, glyphs)."
      },
      "id": "pm:hasSymbolicForm",
      "label": {
        "en": "has symbolic form"
      },
      "range": "pm:Symbolizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TraversalPresupposesSequence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Durchquerung setzt voraus, dass ihre Stationen der Reihe nach genommen werden",
        "en": "The traversal presupposes that its stations are taken in order"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, on purpose and against the reading a modern draft makes. The sources say kramaśaḥ and kramāt kramam, in sequence and step by step, and they say it more than once. What no source consulted says is what happens to one who skips a station. A prohibition needs a consequence somebody can quote, and this one has none; without it the claim is an instruction that a later reader hardened into a rule. See prima-materia#36."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:WorldPathTraversal"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre"
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of quiet sitting and of the investigation of things as a discipline of attention that claims no transcendent object."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NeoConfucianCultivation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neokonfuzianische Selbstkultivierung",
        "en": "Neo-Confucian self-cultivation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as a control condition. A contemplative practice with the same postures and much of the same phenomenology, and without the metaphysical claim, is what any examination of the others needs to have alongside them."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhu Xi, Zhuzi yulei on jingzuo; Wang Yangming, Chuanxilu; Mencius 2A.2 on the flood-like qi",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The belly of a dead man is opened and the substance looked for. A post-mortem in which none is found clears his paternal kin of the suspicion, and cases so decided are quoted afterwards by that kin in their defence."
      },
      "id": "pmp:PostMortemForWitchcraftSubstance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Leichenschau auf die Hexereisubstanz",
        "en": "The post-mortem for witchcraft substance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A procedure inside the transmission with an outcome that could have fallen otherwise, applied to a claim about a class of persons, and reported as having been used to settle actual accusations. It is the second reason this tradition was opened."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 3-4",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "At least one passage has been read in a named edition and entered as a node. This is the state in which a tradition begins to witness."
      },
      "id": "pm:placesEntered",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stellen eingetragen",
        "en": "Places entered"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What a path says becomes of the bounded self at its end: whether the self is to be dissolved into what exceeds it, strengthened and preserved as its own absolute, or whether the path declines the question. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."
      },
      "id": "pm:TelicOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung des Zieles",
        "en": "Telic Orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The values name what a source claims, not what happens. Nothing on this axis is examined by holding it; where somebody takes up the examination, that is a pm:Testing node pointing at the claim, and the examination states of the two values are not symmetric.",
          "This is the axis the Western terms were built to carry, and the axis they carry badly, because they carry the other one at the same time. A record that wants to state where a path claims to end has to be able to state that without also stating how it proceeds. See prima-materia#52."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A breath practice inscribed in forty-five characters on a twelve-sided jade block, setting out the deliberate movement of qi through the body."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Xingqi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zirkulation des Qi",
        "en": "Circulating the qi"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two axes of disagreement belong to the claim and are recorded with it. Dating: Harper places the inscription in the late fourth or early third century BCE, Needham in the middle of the sixth. Reading: Harper takes the opening two characters as the title and begins the text at the second line, where Roth counts and construes differently. A citation that names only the inscription conceals both."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two cases are reported together. A man of a Lapp village whose reindeer were dying prayed to his idol, strewed fir boughs about it, offered the skins, horns and skulls of reindeer, and prostrated himself with his whole family beseeching it to testify by some sign that it was the true god; after a whole day of prayers and devotions, finding no sign given, he set fire to the wood he had brought and burned the idol down, and asked the neighbours who sought to kill him why they would not let the god avenge himself. And a man whose mother was grievously sick sought remedy from the drum; his mother died notwithstanding, whereupon he cut his drum in pieces, alleging that he saw no use of it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AbandonmentAfterFailure",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zwei Praktiker, die das Instrument nach dem Versagen aufgaben",
        "en": "Two practitioners who gave up the instrument after it failed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Both cases have the shape a protocol has: a question, a stated period, an outcome that could have fallen either way, and an act that follows from the outcome. In the first the period is a whole day and the demanded sign is named in advance. Neither man explains the failure away, and that is the whole point of entering them: it is the counter-case to a transmission that meets every disappointment with a named excuse.",
          "The provenance is the reason no more is made of them. Both stand in the chapter on the reception of Christianity, both are credited to a named priest of the mission, and both end with the man becoming a Christian or being praised as one. A missionary reporting that heathens destroyed their own idols after they failed is reporting the thing he most wanted to find. The cases are entered as claims standing in the work, and what they are evidence of is a separate question from what they say."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. VIII, Of the Religion of the Laplanders, p. 33",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ZiqelTeachesSignsOfShootingStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ziqel offenbart die Zeichen der Sternschnuppen",
        "en": "Ziqel discloses the signs of the shooting stars"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with Ezeqeel and the knowledge of the clouds."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfShootingStars"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Ziqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to bring back an object that has been lost, named in the narrative for iron and steel knives, which were rare at the time and often lost."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wiederfinden verlorener Dinge",
        "en": "The recovery of lost things"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what the sixteen figures of the oracle are. One account has them as the base figures of the system itself, called mothers, from whose columns every further figure is compounded, and gives the practice no derivation from outside Yorubaland. The other, written by a Sierra Leonean author and reprinted in the same appendix as the bishop's extract, counts the sixteen as twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, one judge and a further judge got by combining the judge with the figure of the first house, and calls the operations of the diviners geomantic throughout."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaGeomanticIdentificationDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die sechzehn Figuren der Apparat der Geomantie sind",
        "en": "Whether the sixteen figures are the apparatus of geomancy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Neither side of this was read in a modern study at the wording in this run. What the twentieth-century literature makes of the question — whether the figures were taken over and grafted on an older practice, whether the sand science itself has North African roots, and how either is dated — is untouched here and is the first thing the next run on this tradition should open.",
          "The second account is the reason pm:contactRoute on this tradition stands at disputed. Twelve houses, two witnesses and a judge are the working parts of the Arabic sand science and of the European geomancy descended from it, and a system that has them is not without a route to the Mediterranean and Islamic material this graph already holds. The first account describes the same casting and the same sixteen without any of those parts.",
          "The second account was read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints it, and carries no imprint there. It is entered as a side of a dispute and not as a finding about the history of the system."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:IfaSixteenMothers"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
        "J. A. Abayomi Cole, Astrological Geomancy in Africa, the account of the sixteen palm nuts, read in R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Andean transmission of huacas as powers that are addressed and answer, of camac as the making and animating that a huaca is invoked by, and of the graded specialists who speak with them, fast for them and hear confession before them."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Andean",
      "label": {
        "de": "Andine Überlieferung",
        "en": "Andean transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Left unread: chapters VIII to XIII and XV to XX of the extirpation account, which are about the causes of idolatry and the conduct of the visitation rather than about what is transmitted; the whole of the report on the Incas apart from the section on the specialists; and the rest of the chronicle of sixteen fifteen. The Quechua prose of Huarochirí, which is the one document of this transmission written in its own language, was not opened at all: neither the manuscript nor any of its three standard translations could be reached in full text, which is a property of the works and is recorded as its own issue.",
          "Read in this run, all three at the wording: the account of the extirpation of idolatry of sixteen twenty-one, chapters I to VII and XIV, in a digitised copy of the first edition; the report on the fables and rites of the Incas of about fifteen seventy-five, in the critical edition of two thousand and ten, at the folios given; and the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom in the autograph chronicle of sixteen fifteen, in the transcription published with the manuscript by its holding library, at the author's own page numbers.",
          "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent. The registry's absent was not wrong about the Andes before fifteen thirty-two; it was wrong about the corpus. Every word of this transmission that can be read was written down after the conquest, and two of the three recorders wrote in order to destroy what they described. The categories they carried in — sin, confession, penance, priesthood, the devil — are the categories under which the material is reported, and the confession complex entered below is exactly where that matters most. Whether a validity test for a confession is Andean, or Christian confession seen through an Andean procedure, or an Andean procedure seen through Christian confession, is not decidable from these three witnesses, and the entry says so rather than settling it by silence.",
          "The first edition of the extirpation account carries folio numbers that its digitisation does not reproduce reliably, so the places below are given by chapter and by the passage within it. A modern transcription of the same text was held alongside to disambiguate the reading of the sixteen twenty-one type; the two give the same wording at every place used here. No place rests on the transcription alone.",
          "The third recorder states his own provenance and it cuts both ways. He writes that he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. That makes him a witness from inside the transmission who learned much of what he reports while helping to extinguish it, which is a stronger warrant than either priest for the terminology and a weaker one for the practice."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Huarochirí manuscript; Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú; Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas; Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno; Polo de Ondegardo, Los errores y supersticiones de los indios; Cobo, Historia del Nuevo Mundo, book XIII",
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, the summary of what is worshipped",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol addressing the claim that Dogon transmission carries knowledge of an invisible companion of Sirius. It asks whether the star names and the doctrine can be found among Dogon outside the circle of the original informants, and whether any account of them leads anywhere but back to the recording work."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DogonSiriusExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Steht die Kenntnis des Begleiters in der Überlieferung?",
        "en": "Is the knowledge of the companion found in the transmission?"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The counter-testimony has not been read at the wording in this run. A reply appeared in the same journal in the following volume, and a further exchange in the two years after; the deviation recorded here therefore rests on one side of a controversy that has two. See prima-materia#252. Under SPEC §13 this state is a one-sided dispute waiting for its second side, not a refutation, and the state below is set on the cases and not on the conclusion.",
          "The one trace that remains is itself the finding. Informants in that village say that in former times people knew of a star appearing in the year of the ceremony, and further enquiry leads back to the recorder. What looks like independent survival is feedback from the published account into the field it describes, and any later confirmation must be tested against that path before it counts.",
          "The re-study reports the ethnographic facts as straightforward. Dogon know Sirius as a star and call it the hunter's star; knowledge of the stars matters neither in daily life nor in ritual, the sun and the phases of the moon being what reckoning runs on. No Dogon outside the circle of the original informants had heard the star names the account turns on, and no one, not even inside that circle, had heard or understood that Sirius was double. The scheduling of the ceremony is done in several ways in the village the account draws on, none of them astronomical.",
          "The re-study's explanation of how the account arose is not adopted here as a finding, only recorded: that an informant spoke of stars of different generations, meaning two adjacent visible stars held as father and son to Sirius as grandfather, and that the recorder, who had read astronomy in Paris, made a double and triple system of it. That is a reconstruction of an intention and cannot be examined; the absence of the doctrine in the field can."
        ]
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": "Walter E. A. van Beek, Dogon Restudied: A Field Evaluation of the Work of Marcel Griaule, Current Anthropology 32/2 (1991), pp. 149-150 with its footnote 10, and pp. 156-157",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Walter E. A. van Beek, field re-study among the Dogon of Sanga and Yugo Doguru, reported 1991",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A report of the star names or of the doctrine of an invisible companion from Dogon speakers outside the original circle of informants, where the chain of the report can be shown not to run back through the published account; or a fixation of the doctrine in any text or record datable before nineteen thirty-one. Either would return the claim to contested and undo the deviation recorded here.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-01",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that a body or seat of awareness distinct from the physical one is claimed in the Pāli handbooks, in the Yogasūtra, and in Greek reports of men whose souls left them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ExteriorisationCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Entsprechung der Exteriorisation",
        "en": "The exteriorisation correspondence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Indian pair share a sphere and a technical vocabulary and are not independent of each other. The Greek reports are of a different kind entirely: they are second-hand ascriptions to figures long dead, made by historians rather than by practitioners, and a route of contact with the Indian material has been proposed but not shown."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Manomayakaya"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:SoulJourney"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "The Greek witness is fixed in the fifth century before the common era, before any documented route by which Indian technique could have reached the Greek world; a Scythian route has been proposed in the scholarship and not shown. The ground carries the independence and not the strength: the Greek report is a third-party ascription to figures long dead, made by a historian and not by a practitioner, which is a weakness of the testimony and not of its separateness from the Indian material.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": {
        "id": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
      },
      "pm:transmissionPath": "Theravāda and Pātañjala Yoga share the Indian sphere and a common technical vocabulary. A Scythian or shamanic route to the Greek reports has been proposed in the scholarship but is not documented.",
      "source": [
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where the claim was put forward, given as an identifier rather than as a bibliographic reference: an issue number, a draft designation. Deliberately not dcterms:source, because what is named here is not a work a reader can obtain and must never be counted as one."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Positing",
      "id": "pm:positedIn",
      "label": {
        "de": "gesetzt in",
        "en": "posited in"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that several traditions name something conducted or borne in the body and susceptible of deliberate direction."
      },
      "id": "pmc:VitalEnergyCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Entsprechung der Lebensenergie",
        "en": "The vital-energy correspondence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The correspondence is weaker than it looks. Pneuma is a principle of cosmic cohesion and not a faculty; the breath of the Sefer Yetzirah is a medium of formation and not a conduction through the limbs. Only the Chinese and the Indian material carries the operative sense the modern draft assumes."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:InnerHeat"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Kundalini"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Piti"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Pneuma"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Prana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Qi"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Ruach"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "The two witnesses that carry the claim of independence are fixed long before any route between them. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows; the Chāndogya passage belongs to the early Upaniṣads and is older still. The earliest documented traffic that could have carried Indian breath technique into China is the Buddhist transmission of the first centuries of the common era, and both fixations precede it. Neither is an ethnographic gathering, so no question of a recorder's feedback arises; what each carries is the wording of a received text.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": [
        {
          "id": "pmt:Daoist"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmt:Upanisadic"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator and is therefore reception, not independent attestation. Buddhist and Daoist exchange along the trade routes is documented and forbids treating the Chinese and Buddhist testimony as wholly separate. The Greek and Hebrew items are neither route-linked nor operatively parallel.",
      "source": [
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A famous idol of one province was consulted through its priests by an Inca passing with an army, about whether he would return victorious from a battle or die in it; the answer was that he would die, and so it fell out. From this the huaca came to have so great a name that people came from very remote provinces to consult it and to make offerings, and it grew rich and had a sumptuous temple. His son, passing later and seeing that greatness, and learning that it was the huaca which had announced his father's death, ordered the temple and all in it to be burned; the priests stole the idol out of the fire and carried it elsewhere and built it another temple."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CatequillaPrediction",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Huaca, die einen Tod voraussagte und dafür verbrannt wurde",
        "en": "The huaca that foretold a death and was burned for it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A stated hit, with its consequence stated too: the reputation of the huaca is derived from a single prediction that came true, by the recorder and presumably by the tradition he heard it from. It is the only place in this file where a claim about accuracy is made about a particular occasion rather than about a method.",
          "Entered as a claim standing in the work and not as a report of an event. Its chain is long — an extirpator writing in sixteen twenty-one about what another visitor was told about a consultation before the conquest — and no part of it was checked against another witness in this run.",
          "The second half is the more useful. The correct answer cost the huaca its temple, because the person it was correct about was the father of the man who burned it. A transmission in which an accurate oracle can be destroyed for its accuracy is not one in which reputation tracks accuracy, and the story says both things at once."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, on the idol Catequilla",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The wind whose course is downward is said to be made to go upward by force; the manual states the natural course and the reversal in the same verse."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ApanaForcedAgainstItsCourse",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der abwärts gerichtete Wind wird mit Gewalt aufwärts getrieben",
        "en": "The downward wind driven upward by force"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the direct reversal of any scheme that assigns downward carriage to an operation in the lower abdomen. The manual has the abdominal operation carry upward, and has the operation at the throat press the other wind down. Both assignments are the opposite of the one the modern draft makes. See prima-materia#28."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.62 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to hear what is transpiring at a great distance, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the chart by lines drawn from the ears."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideDistantHearing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hören dessen, was in großer Ferne geschieht",
        "en": "Hearing what happens at a great distance"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the balance, of elixirs and of the generation of life by art, carried under the name of Jābir by a corpus larger than one man."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ArabicAlchemy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Arabische Alchemie",
        "en": "Arabic alchemy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Jābirian corpus; al-Rāzī, Sirr al-Asrār",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The African American transmission of workings for protection, luck, love and revenge, carried by practitioners without a priesthood or a central corpus."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hoodoo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hoodoo",
        "en": "Hoodoo"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Hyatt collection is thousands of practitioner statements taken down verbatim with the speakers identified only by place. As a body of first-person practice testimony it has no equal in the project's reach."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hyatt, Hoodoo, Conjuration, Witchcraft, Rootwork, five volumes",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who led Eve astray and disclosed the weapons of death."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Gadreel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gadreel",
        "en": "Gadreel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about where the higher perceptual capacities are exercised. The handbooks of absorption place them in a waking, fully concentrated state and say so with a stated necessity; the ascetic literature marks the light half-sleep as the place of counterfeit consolation; the theurgic account, by contrast, treats the edge of sleep as the mark of a genuine divine communication."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThresholdIsNotTheExecutionState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der Schwellenzustand die Vermögen trägt",
        "en": "Whether the threshold state carries the faculties"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Two of the three sides name the edition or translation they were read in. For Diadochus the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; the passage is to be reopened, and until then the chapter number stands on whichever numbering the earlier session used.",
          "Recorded as a dispute between sources and not as a verdict on the modern draft. A draft is not a side: it carries no attestation, so a claim it makes has no standing here. What the sources actually disagree about is narrower and more interesting than the draft's question, namely whether the threshold is evidential at all, and both sides of that are attested."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PrayerPresupposesWatchfulness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Herzensgebet setzt Wachsamkeit und Führung voraus",
        "en": "The prayer of the heart presupposes watchfulness and guidance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The writers of this corpus set watchfulness and the direction of a spiritual father before the prayer, and describe the way as passing from purification through illumination to perfection. The chapter divisions vary between the editions of the individual texts; the works and their titles are given so that the passages can be found in whichever edition is at hand."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Watchfulness"
      },
      "source": [
        "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness",
        "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Shield, breastplate and the sword for slaughter, disclosed as instruments of killing rather than as craft."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WeaponsOfDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Waffen des Todes",
        "en": "The weapons of death"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The American transmission holding that mental states determine bodily and material conditions, and that the determination can be exercised deliberately."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NewThought",
      "label": {
        "de": "New Thought",
        "en": "New Thought"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is stated in a form that invites counting, and it is the ancestor of most of what circulates today as manifestation."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Quimby, The Quimby Manuscripts; Emma Curtis Hopkins, Scientific Christian Mental Practice; Troward, The Edinburgh Lectures on Mental Science",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The manual layer of Theravāda: the objects of concentration, the marks by which each absorption is recognised, and the higher knowledges said to follow from mastery."
      },
      "id": "pmt:TheravadaCommentarial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theravādische Kommentarhandbücher",
        "en": "Theravāda commentarial manuals"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered apart from the canon because the canon states that the attainments occur and the manuals state how to reach them and how to tell whether one has."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga; Upatissa, Vimuttimagga; Paṭisambhidāmagga",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The power is imagined rising in the form of lightning, at each centre in turn and step by step, upward as far as three fists, and at the end of that ascent stands the great awakening."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Aufstieg, der in einer Bewegung jedes Zentrum durchläuft",
        "en": "The ascent that passes through every centre in one movement"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two phrases that carry this are praticakram, at each centre, and kramāt kramam, step by step. Recorded because it settles two questions at once. It gives a body measure in the base text, three fists for the upward reach, where a modern draft gives an arm's length; and it makes the passage through several centres a single exercise, where the same draft allows one centre per session and calls a change of centre a break. See prima-materia#38 and prima-materia#40."
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "By succession, the son inheriting from the father, and where the heir is too young the nearest kinsman standing in until he is fit. By election, where succession fails, the other ministers choosing whom they judge fittest with the agreement of the lords. Or by a man taking the office himself, which is said to be ordinary among the old for the lesser offices of diviner and healer, and to be done for a living."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays",
      "label": {
        "de": "Drei Wege in das Amt",
        "en": "Three ways into the office"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The third way is entered with the others because the recorder gives it the same weight and because it is the one a later reader would drop. A transmission that admits self-appointment for its lesser offices is saying where its own boundary is soft."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KasdejaTeachesStrikingOfSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kasdeja offenbart das Schlagen der Geister",
        "en": "Kasdeja discloses the striking of spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:StrikingOfSpirits"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Kasdeja"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Attic mystery cult of Demeter and Kore, with a lesser rite at Agrai in the spring and the greater rite at Eleusis in the autumn."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EleusinianMysteries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eleusinische Mysterien",
        "en": "Eleusinian mysteries"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citation names the corpus in which the inscription is edited, which for an epigraphic source is the edition; what is not recorded is whether the text was read in that corpus or in a secondary report of it, and that is to be established before the reading of face C is relied on."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from the stars, given in place of the constellations in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Sterne",
        "en": "Signs of the stars"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Daoist transmission of visualised journeys, of deities resident in the body, and of nocturnal encounters recorded as they were received."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shangqing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shangqing",
        "en": "Shangqing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Zhen'gao is a dated record of revelations with the recipient named, which places it among the few pre-modern first-person corpora available anywhere."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao; Huangting neijing jing; Dadong zhenjing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The number of cases recorded under this protocol so far."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:caseCount",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fallzahl",
        "en": "case count"
      },
      "range": "xsd:integer",
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The same extract that states the standard states that it is not reached: scarcely anyone has been found who has learnt the whole of the narratives and can recite them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaMemorisationStandardUnmet",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der als unerreicht berichtete Gedächtnismaßstab",
        "en": "The memorisation standard reported as unattained"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The node exists because the shortfall stands in the same breath as the demand. A record that carried the demand alone would report a feat of memory that the tradition does not claim is performed, and this is one of the few places where a transmission states the distance between its own rule and its practitioners.",
          "This is the point at which the tradition touches the exactness material already in the graph, and it touches it from the other side: there the rule is enforced, here the rule is conceded to be unfulfilled. No convergence node is written for it. The contact route of this tradition stands at disputed, so an agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to know in advance that something remarkable is about to happen, reported in the narrative as a standing property of a person rather than as the outcome of an exercise."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zweites Gesicht",
        "en": "Second sight"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The English term is the translator's. What the node carries is the claim reported under it and not the term's own history in Scottish and Scandinavian usage; whether the Inuktitut word behind it has that range is not decidable from the portion read."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShemihazahTeachesRootCutting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schemichasa offenbart das Wurzelschneiden",
        "en": "Shemihazah discloses root-cutting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:RootCutting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Shemihazah"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The lineage of Gorakhnāth, teaching a body perfected by practice and a state in which mind is said to be absent rather than concentrated."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Nath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nāth-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Nāth transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Siddha-Siddhānta-Paddhati; Gorakṣaśataka; Amanaska",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect an invocation is held to obtain, which the closing petition asks the invoked ancestor to grant."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit der Karakia",
        "en": "The efficacy of the karakia"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 11-12",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The solemn ritual and its exegesis, in which efficacy rests on stated equivalences between the act, the body and the world."
      },
      "id": "pmt:VedicRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vedische Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Vedic ritual transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa; Aitareya Brāhmaṇa; the Śrautasūtras",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt, whose abandoning the discourse describes immediately before entry into the first absorption."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FiveHindrances",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die fünf Hemmnisse",
        "en": "The five hindrances"
      },
      "source": "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DrumMustBeInherited",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Trommel muss ererbt sein",
        "en": "The drum must be inherited"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drums were not thought sufficiently fit for their uses unless got by inheritance, and does not say what follows from using one that was not. The rule sits oddly beside the same recorder's report of a practitioner explaining how his own drum was made, and the oddity is left standing rather than resolved."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:SunCourseRule"
      },
      "source": "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 474",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity the whole transmission is consulted for: to know what is happening at a place too far off to be seen or reached, and to report it on returning."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DistantSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sehen des Entfernten",
        "en": "Seeing what is at a distance"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47 and pp. 55-57",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "One party ascribes the capacity to another, who does not report it. Common for capacities said to be lost, since no bearer remains to be asked."
      },
      "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fremdzuschreibung",
        "en": "Third-party ascription"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PenemueTeachesWriting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Penemue offenbart das Schreiben mit Tinte",
        "en": "Penemue discloses writing with ink"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:WritingWithInk"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Penemue"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A work of the corpus has been obtained and read and its edition is settled, but no passage has yet been entered as a node."
      },
      "id": "pm:workOpened",
      "label": {
        "de": "Werk geöffnet",
        "en": "Work opened"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The senior specialist gives notice when the time comes, the chicha is brewed, and he goes through the households collecting the offerings. On the appointed day he goes with his assistants to the principal huaca and makes the offering, then to the lesser huacas and the ancestor bodies. On his return nobody sleeps that night: they sing, dance and tell tales, and children are beaten if they fall asleep. This night is called the pacaricuc, and from it the fast begins — no salt, no chilli, no sleeping with wives — lasting five days in some places and longer in others according to different traditions. Three such feasts are held in the year, tied to the Pleiades, to the first rains and to the maize harvest."
      },
      "id": "pmp:HuacaFeast",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fest der Huaca",
        "en": "The feast of the huaca"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Hawaiian transmission of a chanted genealogy of the world, recited as a continuous descent from darkness to the living."
      },
      "id": "pmt:HawaiianCosmogony",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hawaiische Kosmogonie",
        "en": "Hawaiian cosmogonic transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Kumulipo in the Hawaiian text with Beckwith's edition",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of the end state. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other."
      },
      "id": "pm:telicOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung des Zieles",
        "en": "telic orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Where a source claims one end state for a stage and another for the whole, both are stated on their own nodes rather than averaged. An averaged orientation reports a path no source teaches, in the same way an averaged reading reports a text no witness gives."
      },
      "range": "pm:TelicOrientation",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The early modern attempt to give magic a philosophy: sympathies, images, memory as an operative art, and angelic communication kept as a record."
      },
      "id": "pmt:RenaissanceMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gelehrte Magie der Renaissance",
        "en": "Renaissance learned magic"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Dee's diaries are a working log with dates, which makes them a different kind of witness from the treatises around them."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ficino, De vita libri tres; Agrippa, De occulta philosophia; Bruno, De umbris idearum; Dee, Monas Hieroglyphica and the spiritual diaries",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission founded by Kardec on answers obtained through mediums and arranged as a doctrine, with survival and communication asserted as facts open to verification."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Spiritism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Spiritismus",
        "en": "Spiritism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered because of how it states its claims. A tradition that submits itself to examination in its own founding text is the natural first object of the project's examination layer, and the largest single body of nineteenth-century investigation is attached to it."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kardec, Le Livre des Esprits; Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Associates a process instance with the pronominal reach of its awareness."
      },
      "id": "pm:hasAwarenessSpace",
      "label": {
        "en": "has awareness space"
      },
      "range": "pm:AwarenessSpace",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first stage of a secret knowledge, named with a Bambara expression. The account states that the rules of the Sirius system revealed to initiates at this stage are simplified in some parts and complicated in others, in order to turn attention away from calculations that remain more secret still."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Leichte Kenntnis",
        "en": "Light knowledge"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Associates a process instance with the tradition in which it is situated."
      },
      "id": "pm:withinTradition",
      "label": {
        "en": "within tradition"
      },
      "range": "pm:Tradition",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Suhrawardī's transmission of knowledge by presence and of an intermediate world of images held to be neither material nor mental but a place with its own standing."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Illuminationism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Illuminationismus",
        "en": "Illuminationism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim that the imaginal world is a place and not a faculty is an ontological commitment the graph will have to sort against every tradition that treats vision as internal."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Suhrawardī, Ḥikmat al-Ishrāq; Mullā Ṣadrā, al-Asfār al-Arbaʿa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BhastrikaPresupposesLevelNeckAndTrunk",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Blasebalgatem setzt einen gerade gehaltenen Hals und Rumpf voraus",
        "en": "The bellows breath presupposes neck and trunk held level"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The verse binds the lotus seat, a level neck and trunk, and a carefully closed mouth into the one instruction that opens the bellows breath. It is the only place in the breath chapter where the alignment of the spine is stated as a condition of a breath practice, and it is stated for one practice, not for the chapter. See prima-materia#29."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.60 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The general and the tantra-specific preliminaries, placed before the training in either stage."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Preliminaries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vorbereitungen",
        "en": "The preliminaries"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A dating warning belongs here. The preliminaries are set before the training in the fifteenth-century treatise, but the sharpest codification of them as an obligatory course is nineteenth-century, in Patrul Rinpoche's manual. Their presence in the older text does not license reading the later regime back into it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patrul Rinpoche, Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung (19th century)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ValidVerdictPresupposesCorroboration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein gültiger Spruch setzt den zweiten Test voraus",
        "en": "A valid verdict presupposes the second test"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source names what fails to happen without the second test and not merely the order of the two. The qualification that a single test is often accepted stands on the practice node and does not weaken this: where the transmission requires the corroboration, it also says what the want of it costs."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Without the second test no judgement is accepted as valid; where both tests fall alike the verdict is contradictory and void and the matter must be put again on another occasion."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted. This is a statement about the means of examination, not about the claim."
      },
      "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kein Verfahren ersonnen",
        "en": "No procedure devised"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of an animal magnetism held to pass between bodies and to be directed by passes of the hand, together with the induced states its practitioners described."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Mesmerism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mesmerismus",
        "en": "Mesmerism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as a tradition because it transmits by testimony and lineage. Its examination by the royal commission is evidence and belongs at an examination node, not here. The pair is the cleanest illustration the project has of the two predicates."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Mesmer, Mémoire sur la découverte du magnétisme animal; Puységur, Mémoires pour servir à l'histoire du magnétisme animal",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Dogon transmission of a renewal of the world celebrated once in sixty years, of the ways by which the interval is counted, and of a seed-star said to be the smallest and the heaviest of things."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Dogon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dogon-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Dogon transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Griaule and Dieterlen 1950 in full, pp. 273-294 of the first printing, in the digitised page images of the journal. Nothing else of the principal corpus was opened: neither Dieu d'eau, nor Masques dogons, nor Le renard pâle, nor Les âmes des Dogon. The Sirius article was chosen first because it is the single text the outside reception rests on.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent. The reason is in the article and not in a later judgement about it: on p. 282 the recorders attach to the fifty-year orbit a footnote observing that this period lies very close to that of the companion of Sirius, and they cite a French astronomical periodical of 1931 for it. Whatever the informants said, the account that reached print was written by readers of European astronomy, and the central enquiry ran from 1946 to 1950 in a region under French administration with a mission station in it. A route to the material this graph already holds therefore exists at the point where the corpus was fixed, and it runs through the recorders. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry.",
          "The registration named van Beek's re-study as part of the principal corpus. That was wrong under SPEC §14: a field re-study is a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise, which makes it evidence and not testimony. It has been removed from the corpus here and stands at pm:evidenceFrom on the examination node instead.",
          "The tradition most widely reported outside itself, and reported wrongly. Registered in this graph from the start as the standing warning of the whole registry, because a body of teaching that circulated for decades as ancient African astronomy could not be found by later workers among the people it was ascribed to."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Griaule, Masques dogons; Griaule, Dieu d'eau; Griaule and Dieterlen, Le renard pâle; Dieterlen, Les âmes des Dogon; Griaule and Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius",
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 273-294",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KnowledgePresupposesContemplation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gotteserkenntnis setzt Naturschau voraus",
        "en": "Knowledge of God presupposes contemplation of nature"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Theologike"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Physike"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The five elements of the rite belonging to heroic practice, named in the source as wine, meat, fish, parched grain, and the union of man and woman."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Panchatattva",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die fünf Tattvas des heroischen Ritus",
        "en": "The five tattvas of the heroic rite"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The fourth element is parched grain and not a ritual gesture. Wilson renders the same list in 1832 with mudrā glossed as gesticulations of the fingers and maithuna as women; the divergence is his and not the text's, and it matters because grain makes that element a food where a gesture would make it an act. Recorded so that the current gloss can be checked against a verse instead of against another summary. See prima-materia#53.",
          "The same passage carries its own caution, and the caution is not a Western addition: the source says that the men of this age, being greedy and lustful, will neglect the discipline, drink for the pleasure of it, and lose the distinction of right and wrong. The warning against the rite stands inside the text that prescribes it."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.58-59 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.61 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MaoriEfficacyPresupposesExactRecitation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit einer Karakia setzt die fehlerfreie Wiederholung voraus",
        "en": "The efficacy of a karakia presupposes its repetition without error"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable. The same sentence is the tradition's own falsifying condition and is what makes the claim examinable at all; the protocol stands at pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination in convergences/recitation-exactness.ttl."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The witness says that to make an error, or even to hesitate, in repeating a karakia was held fatal to its efficacy. The consequence is stated for the whole address and not for a part of it, and hesitation is named alongside error.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:MaoriExactRecitation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that an experience of luminosity or consolation may be counterfeit, and that the counterfeit is recognised by its circumstances rather than by its brightness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AngelOfLightDeception",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Täuschung, die als Licht erscheint",
        "en": "The deception that appears as light"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Diadochus distinguishes the true consolation, which comes with the body awake and in warm love, from the false, which comes in a light half-sleep."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins"
      },
      "source": [
        "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 39"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Before a seance the performers eat some of their medicines. The medicine goes into the stomach and is shaken through the body by the dancing, where it is held to become an active agent. The whole apprenticeship consists in the slow transfer of the knowledge of which plants the medicines are made from, paid for in a long string of fees, and a plant is known by stalk and leaf and not by root, which is why laymen may sit at the communal meal without learning anything."
      },
      "id": "pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Essen der Medizinen der Hexendoktoren",
        "en": "Eating the medicines of the witch-doctors"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89 and pp. 96-97",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity a yielding claim arrives at. That the tradition says so is the claim; that the practice produces it is not asserted."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Yielding",
      "id": "pm:yieldsCapacity",
      "label": {
        "de": "bringt Vermögen hervor",
        "en": "yields capacity"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rite by which a person or place under tapu is made common again, in the case witnessed by the eating of food cooked in an oven opened for the purpose and distributed to everyone present."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Whakanoa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Aufheben des Tapu",
        "en": "Making noa"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The rite the recorder describes as an eyewitness was already carried out with a Christian morning service inside it, and he remarks that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial. The passage is the clearest single reason the contact route of this tradition is entered as disputed rather than absent."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 27",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A documented route of historical contact between the traditions in which the correspondence appears."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:transmissionPath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übertragungsweg",
        "en": "transmission path"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The earlier definition here said that the presence of a route downgrades the correspondence from convergence to reception. That was a judgement wearing the clothes of a definition, and it is withdrawn. A route says how a claim travelled, not whether anything was in it. Survival through many hands is selection, because most claims do not survive it, and a record that treats transmission as noise throws away the only evidence it has about what kept being worth copying.",
          "What the route does decide is narrower and stays in force: an agreement reached along it may not be counted as independent attestation, because the same statement would be counted twice. Both may stand on one node, and on several they already do. The route bars a particular way of counting; it does not devalue the correspondence."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A preliminary in which the channels are cleared by visualisation before the heat practice proper; the tradition's own form of the notion that a blockage is a place the energy does not reach."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ChannelPurification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Reinigung der Kanäle",
        "en": "Purification of the channels"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the ascetic literature of the Egyptian desert and its Byzantine continuation, in which the path is set out as an ordered ascent."
      },
      "id": "pmt:DesertMonasticism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wüstenmönchtum",
        "en": "Desert monasticism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter numbering of the Praktikos and the step division of the Ladder both vary between editions, so the numbers here stand on nothing until the passages are reopened."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The examination of an inner movement for its origin, distinguished threefold as angelic, human or demonic."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unterscheidung der Herkunft",
        "en": "Discernment of origins"
      },
      "source": [
        "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 8",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 80"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second question, put with a second fowl, in which the oracle is told what its first answer was and asked to confirm it by the opposite outcome. The first test is called bambata sima and the second gingo. If the first fowl died the second must survive, and if the first survived the second must die."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der bestätigende zweite Test am Giftorakel",
        "en": "The corroborative second test at the poison oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two tests may be made at one seance where the poison suffices, and are often separated by weeks or months. The transmission names six situations in which a single test is accepted instead, among them the case where the answer came decisively and the case where a lesser oracle had already been asked; but two tests are said to be indispensable in any question concerning the relations between two persons, and especially where a legal issue is involved."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-140",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1888",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that humanity divided at a datable point of its history into two opposed paths of knowledge, a right-hand and a left-hand one, and that the two magics called white and black were sown as seeds at that moment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TwoPathsOfKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Verzweigung der Menschheit in einen rechten und einen linken Pfad des Wissens",
        "en": "The branching of humanity into a right-hand and a left-hand path of knowledge"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Checked against the earlier work rather than assumed from it. The full text of Isis Unveiled (1877), both volumes, carries no occurrence of a right-hand or a left-hand path, and none of the tantric vocabulary the names come from. The usual account, which dates the introduction of the terms to 1877, is wrong by eleven years: the passage stands in the work of 1888. See prima-materia#58.",
          "The claim is presented as a quotation from a commentary of unstated antiquity, and it is the commentary rather than the author that later reporting cites. That is what pm:circulatesAs records here, and it is why this node is an origination and not an ordering: an ordering names what it arranges, and this one arranges nothing.",
          "What is new here is not the pair of names, which stands in the orientalist literature from 1832 and is held as pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature. What is new is that the pair becomes a history: two parties, a moment of origin, and a lineage of adepts on either side. Everything the later occult milieu inherits — that there are two camps and that a person may join one of them — rests on this move and not on the naming."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "The ancient meaning of the two paths, credited to a commentary of unstated age rather than to the work of 1888 in which it first stands.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Helena P. Blavatsky",
      "source": "H. P. Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine I: Cosmogenesis (London 1888), in the discussion of the fourth race, at the sentence on the branching of humanity into the right- and left-hand paths of knowledge or Vidya",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Diné transmission of chantways: long ordered ceremonials for restoring a condition named as order, with the sequence fixed and its errors held to be consequential."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Navajo-Zeremonialüberlieferung",
        "en": "Navajo ceremonial transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Read for the first time in the introduction to the legends, sections 41 and 42 on the sacred songs, printed pages 24 and 25. The legends themselves and the ceremonial texts were not read, and the fuller ceremonial account of the night chant published in nineteen hundred and two was not opened in this run."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Wyman, Blessingway, with the recorded Navajo texts; Matthews, The Night Chant",
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), pp. 24-25",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from the moon, given in place of its course in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen des Mondes",
        "en": "Signs of the moon"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Australian transmission of an ancestral order laid into country, held in song and in the routes the song follows."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AustralianDreaming",
      "label": {
        "de": "Australische Aboriginal-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Australian Aboriginal transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered and deliberately left at corpus named. There is no corpus in the project's sense, much of the material is restricted by rules that are still enforced, and a public-domain dedication of restricted knowledge would be a taking and not an opening. The gate here protects ownership, not authority, and the project's criterion does not apply to it unchanged."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "No transmitted written corpus; the ethnographic record stands in its place and much of it is restricted",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Byzantine tradition of the prayer of the heart and of the vision of the uncreated light, defended in the fourteenth century against the objection that such a light must be created."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hesychasm",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hesychasmus",
        "en": "Hesychasm"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The Philokalia exists in Greek and in several translations whose contents and numbering differ, so a citation naming only the volume does not yet locate a text."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A set of sixteen named stations of the body, called supports, which the seal at the throat is said to bind; the manual names the number in a compound and treats the binding of all sixteen as one operation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SixteenSupports",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sechzehn Stützen",
        "en": "The sixteen supports"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because the number is the whole of the finding. A modern draft gives thirteen segments as the path along which the force is led; sixteen is what the base text names here, and other works of the wider corpus give eighteen. The count therefore varies across the transmission, which means there is no single path to be had, and a scheme that presents one number as the path has chosen a branch without saying so. The other witnesses to the sixteen and the eighteen have not been read at their wording and are not entered here. See prima-materia#35."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tungus and Sakha transmission of an office entered through an illness that resolves only on acceptance, with a costume, a drum and a stated itinerary."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SiberianShamanism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sibirische Schamanenüberlieferung",
        "en": "Siberian shamanic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The region the word shaman was taken from. The graph uses the category and holds no witness from its source."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus; the published Olonkho epics; Anisimov's field records",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "2004",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that emotions form a graded ladder that indicates how near a person stands to a desired state, with joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love and appreciation at its top rung."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HicksEmotionalGuidanceScale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Skala der emotionalen Führung",
        "en": "The emotional guidance scale"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The upper block of this scale reappears almost word for word in later lists that present themselves as older. Recorded so that the wording has a dated first appearance to be checked against."
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A law of the emotions, presented as a description of how feeling works rather than as one author's arrangement.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Esther Hicks and Jerry Hicks",
      "source": "E. Hicks and J. Hicks, Ask and It Is Given (2004), ch. 22",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The entry rite of the society: instruction under a paid teacher, often carried over years, gifts to the officiating priests, a feast, and the shooting of the shell into the candidate by the officiating priests."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree",
      "label": {
        "de": "Einweihung in den ersten Grad",
        "en": "Initiation into the first degree"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The report says candidates sometimes spend a number of years in preparation and impoverish themselves in the payment of fees and the preparation of the feast. The cost is stated as a fact of the institution and not as a warning."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 164",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state of being free or common, that is, not under the restriction of tapu. The witness states that persons and things under tapu can be brought into this state by ceremonies, so that the two are not fixed conditions but ends of an operation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Noa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Noa",
        "en": "Noa"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 26",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Ordinary outward-turned awareness of gross objects."
      },
      "id": "pm:WakingState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wachzustand (Jāgrat)",
        "en": "Waking state (Jāgrat)"
      },
      "source": "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 3",
      "type": "pm:AwarenessContext"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tibetan transmission that claims descent from Zhangzhung rather than from India, with its own dzogchen cycles and its own founder."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Bon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bön",
        "en": "Bön"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The independence claim is the point. If it holds, Bön is a witness beside Tibetan Buddhism rather than a variant of it; if it fails, the failure is one of the better documented cases of a tradition constructing its own antiquity. Either outcome is a finding."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud; the Bön dzogchen cycles in the published Bön canon",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DescentPresupposesDepth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Abstieg setzt die Tiefe voraus, die den Atem sammelt",
        "en": "The descent presupposes the depth that gathers the breath"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at stated necessity because a consequence is quotable, which is unusual: most sequences in this collection state an order and leave the consequence unsaid. The strength rests on the closing line, not on the chain of conditionals, which on its own would carry only a prescribed order."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The inscription closes by setting the two outcomes against each other: to go with the sequence is to live, to go against it is to die. The consequence is stated for the order as a whole, and every stage is joined to the one before it by the same conditional form.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:XingqiDescent"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:XingqiDepth"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to see what is to come, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the pictographic chart by lines drawn upward from the eyes."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideForesight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Blick in das Künftige",
        "en": "Looking into futurity"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The qualifying material of the drum. It must be a pine, fir or birch grown in a particular place and turning directly according to the sun's course, which the recorder defines: the grain of the wood, running from the bottom of the tree to the top, winds itself from the right hand to the left. A tree so grown is held acceptable to the sun."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Holz, das sich mit dem Sonnenlauf dreht",
        "en": "Wood that turns with the sun's course"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second condition on the same material stands in the same passage: the piece must be taken from the root, cleft asunder and hollowed on one side, and the skin is stretched over the hollow. It is entered here rather than as a node of its own because it names no criterion by which a piece could fail, only a manner of working.",
          "The direction is stated twice in this file in frames that do not obviously agree. Here the sun's course is the grain winding from the right hand to the left as it rises; at pmc:SunCourseRule it is the ring travelling round towards the right hand. Whether a spiral seen along a rising trunk and a ring seen on a flat skin describe one direction or two is not decidable from the passages, and is left standing rather than harmonised.",
          "The recorder gives the sun-acceptability as his own conjecture — 'from this perhaps they believe' — not as something told to him. The requirement is reported; its reason is his."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Sight beyond the range of the physical eye."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Dibbacakkhu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Auge",
        "en": "Divine eye"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the sun."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Shamsiel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schamsiel",
        "en": "Shamsiel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earlier step the later one is said to require."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:priorStep",
      "label": {
        "de": "vorausgesetzter Schritt",
        "en": "prior step"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The deliberate raising and sustaining of a chosen affective state as a meditation subject in its own right."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BrahmaviharaBhavana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kultivierung der göttlichen Verweilungen",
        "en": "Cultivation of the divine abidings"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IX (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of apprehending form within an awareness context."
      },
      "id": "pm:Perceiving",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrnehmen",
        "en": "Perceiving"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacities of the third chapter are named obstacles with respect to samādhi and attainments only with respect to the outward-turned state. The tradition that catalogues them is the same one that warns against them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Kräfte sind Hindernisse für samādhi",
        "en": "The powers are obstacles to samādhi"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksus"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.37",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The later step: the practice, state or capacity said not to be reached without the prior one."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:dependentStep",
      "label": {
        "de": "abhängiger Schritt",
        "en": "dependent step"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1832",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The English naming of the dakṣiṇācāra and vāmācāra divisions of the Śākta sect as the followers of a right hand and a left hand ritual, together with the characterisation of the second as impure, as running contrary to what is usual, and as something its adherents will not publicly avow."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wiedergabe der beiden Śākta-Zweige als rechtshändiges und linkshändiges Ritual",
        "en": "The rendering of the two Śākta branches as a right and a left hand ritual"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an ordering act because the naming is regularly credited to Blavatsky, who wrote in 1877 and in 1888. It stands in Wilson forty-five years earlier, in the volume of the Asiatic Researches for 1832, in those terms and with the moral charge already attached. Whoever first coined it, it was not she, and a record that repeats the later attribution reports a reception as an origin. See prima-materia#57.",
          "No pm:ordersClaim is given, because the division it orders is not yet in the graph as a claim of its own: that would need a passage in a tantra rather than a passage in Wilson. See prima-materia#53.",
          "The same section carries a ranking the naming does not survive. Wilson translates a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava that grades the Veda lowest and ascends through the Vaiṣṇava and the Śaiva to the right hand Śākta, then sets the left hand above the right, the Siddhānta above that, and the Kaula above all. In the material being ordered, the left is therefore the higher of the two; the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses the valuation. The verse is not located in an edition of the Kulārṇava here and is not held as a claim of that work. See prima-materia#56."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "Horace Hayman Wilson",
      "source": "H. H. Wilson, Sketch of the Religious Sects of the Hindus, Asiatic Researches XVII (Calcutta 1832), the sections headed Dakshinas, or Bhaktas and Vamis, or Vamacharis",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A cross-tradition structural analogue, weaker than owl:sameAs and distinct from skos:closeMatch in that it asserts role-in-process similarity rather than lexical overlap."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "id": "pm:analogueConcept",
      "label": {
        "en": "analogue concept"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where more than one oracle is consulted the lesser is always consulted before the greater, in the order rubbing-board, termites, poison. A verdict of the termites is not put to the rubbing-board for confirmation, and a verdict of the poison oracle is not put to the termites. On all important matters the decision of the termites must be corroborated by the poison oracle."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OracleRankOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rangordnung der Orakel",
        "en": "The graded order of the oracles"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The order is a rule about the direction in which confirmation may run and not merely a ranking by esteem. It is what makes the cheaper oracles usable at all: a man narrows the field with the termites and spends poison only on the final decision."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The report adds, of this class and of no other in its list of specialists, that these were the least esteemed, because they always lied."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTheFireReaders",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Feuerleser galten am wenigsten, weil sie immer logen",
        "en": "The fire-readers were least esteemed because they always lied"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It bears directly on the claim, open elsewhere in this project, that the old traditions test origin and fruits and never a rate of hits. Here a transmission is reported as having formed a standing comparative judgement about a whole class of practitioners on the ground that they were always wrong, which is an aggregation over cases however loosely it was arrived at. Whether the judgement was reached by counting or was itself a piece of received esteem is not decidable from the one sentence, and no examination node is written on it.",
          "The most valuable sentence found in this run. It is a ranking of divinatory techniques by reliability, made inside the transmission, with the reason given as repeated failure rather than as impurity, wrong descent or broken taboo. Every other failure in this file is attributed to something about the person; this one is attributed to the method."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:FireReading"
      },
      "source": "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The course the seed-star is said to run around Sirius: perpendicular to the horizon, of a period of some fifty years counted double to a hundred because Sigui are paired as twins, with the star turning on itself once a year. Sirius is said to brighten when the seed-star is nearest and the seed-star to scintillate so that an observer believes he sees several stars when it is farthest."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PoToloOrbit",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bahn des Pô tolo um Sirius",
        "en": "The course of pô tolo around Sirius"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The article calls the same course the figure of circumcision and excision: the near and far points of the orbit are the foreskin and the knife. The astronomical reading and the ritual reading are the same drawing, and the account gives no priority to either."
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 281-283",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Chinese transmission of elixirs compounded from mineral substances, with an explicit catalogue of the capacities each elixir is said to confer."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Waidan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Waidan, äußere Alchemie",
        "en": "Waidan external alchemy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Ge Hong's inner chapters list attainments in the same enumerated form as the siddhi lists and were compiled without access to them. The comparison is one of the sharpest tests the project can set itself."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian; Zhouyi cantong qi",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The channels of the subtle body through which the winds are held to move; Tsongkhapa's presentation counts one hundred and twenty principal ones."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Rtsa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kanäle",
        "en": "Channels"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Whether a route of transmission is known between a tradition and those the graph already holds. It decides whether an agreement may be read as independent attestation or only as reception, and it is therefore a property of the tradition and not of the individual correspondence."
      },
      "id": "pm:ContactRoute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg",
        "en": "Contact route"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Jewish transmission of an ascent through palaces guarded by gatekeepers, entered by seals and passwords, and of an adjuration by which knowledge is obtained without study."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hekhalot",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hekhalot- und Merkava-Literatur",
        "en": "Hekhalot and Merkavah literature"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Sar ha-Torah material states a learning capacity plainly enough to be tested: knowledge of Torah acquired by ritual rather than by study, in a stated time."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hekhalot Rabbati; Hekhalot Zutarti; Sar ha-Torah; Šiʿur Qomah; 3 Enoch",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "He returns with a knife, a ring or some other token of his knowledge of what is done in those parts, and on rising shows what he has brought from thence, so that the account may seem certain. The recorder notes that the other report he uses mentions no such thing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TokenBroughtBack",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Zeichen wird von dem Ort mitgebracht",
        "en": "A token is brought back from the place"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "That the two reports the compiler used disagree about whether the token exists is recorded with the claim and not smoothed away. He names the report that lacks it. Which of the two is right is not decidable here, and the disagreement is itself the reason no examination node is written.",
          "This is the most examinable claim in the file. It is not a report of a perception but the production of a physical object said to have come from a named distant place, in front of witnesses, at a stated moment. What would refute it is obvious and requires nothing but the object and the place, and it is entered chiefly for that reason."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The modern transmission of root races, planes and masters, presented as the recovery of a single ancient doctrine underlying all religions."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Theosophy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theosophie",
        "en": "Theosophy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The pedigree claim is the tradition's own central assertion and is contested; it belongs in the graph as a claim about origin and not as a fact about transmission. Much of what circulates today as the doctrine of older traditions entered general circulation here."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Blavatsky, Isis Unveiled; Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine; Leadbeater, The Chakras",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Irish and Welsh transmission of poetic knowledge as an acquired faculty, with named divinatory procedures and the ecclesiastical prohibitions attached to them."
      },
      "id": "pmt:InsularCeltic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Inselkeltische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Insular Celtic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prohibitions are in the same manuscripts as the procedures, so procedure and counter-testimony arrive together."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The legal and glossarial material on imbas forosnai, teinm laída and díchetal do chennaib; the immrama; the Book of Taliesin",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Anatolian purification and substitution rituals transmitted under the names of the practitioners who dictated them, most of them women."
      },
      "id": "pmt:HittiteRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hethitisch-anatolisches Ritual",
        "en": "Hittite and Anatolian ritual"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The CTH ritual corpus, including the rituals of Tunnawiya, Maštigga and Ambazzi",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "In the two seats the manual singles out as chief, the chin is set firmly against the chest and the gaze is fixed between the brows or at the tip of the nose."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ChinToChestInTheChiefSeats",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das an die Brust gedrückte Kinn in den beiden bevorzugten Sitzhaltungen",
        "en": "The chin pressed to the chest in the two seats the manual prefers"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the counterweight to any claim that the manual requires an erect spine throughout. The seats it recommends most warmly close the neck rather than extend it, and the same posture chapter has the tongue set against the roots of the upper teeth while the wind is drawn upward. Uprightness of the trunk is attested; uprightness carried through the neck and head is contradicted in the same work. See prima-materia#29."
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.46 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.48 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The formation of a concept as an act within awareness; instances of this class are concept nodes such as pmc:Pleroma or pmc:Hen."
      },
      "id": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Begriffsbildung",
        "en": "Conceptualizing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of messages ascribed to a command or council of non-terrestrial beings charged with the oversight of the earth, received after the first by many transmitters who do not share a lineage."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AshtarTransmission",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ashtar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Ashtar transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The motif of a federation or confederation of worlds enters general circulation here and is afterwards found in corpora that never name Ashtar. That spread is a reception history and belongs in the graph as one, before anybody reads it as agreement between independent witnesses."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Van Tassel, the Ministry of Universal Wisdom publications; the published Ashtar message collections",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The named compiler who performed the ordering act. Without it, an order made in the present is indistinguishable from an order found in the source."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Systematizing",
      "id": "pm:compiledBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "geordnet von",
        "en": "compiled by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The central Mexican transmission of address to the powers in things, of the day count, of an animal companion given at birth, and of a seed drunk in order to be questioned."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NahuaRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nahua-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Nahua ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run: the first treatise of the manual of sixteen twenty-nine, chapters one to seven, in the running text. The remaining eight treatises, which carry the incantations themselves and the calling back of the tonal, were not reached: the reproduction used delivers its running text only to that point. That is a property of the access path and not of the work, and the next run resumes there rather than at the beginning. See prima-materia#228.",
          "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the change is a correction rather than a finding. The registry recorded absent, which asserts that no route exists for the period in which the corpus was fixed. But this corpus was fixed a century after the conquest, by a Spanish priest holding an ecclesiastical office, among people who had been under mission for three generations, and the recorded material carries Christian elements in places. Whether what he wrote down transmits the pre-conquest practice, and in what proportion, is a question nobody in this graph has carried out. Undecided says that; absent said something stronger that was never earned.",
          "The edition is not fully stated, and that is a defect this file carries openly. The reproduction used gives the Spanish in an old orthography and does not name the edition it reproduces. Every source line below says so. Settling it is the first thing to do before more nodes are written on this tradition, because a later change of edition would touch all of them.",
          "The incantations were taken down in Nahuatl in order to be suppressed, so the words survive in the language they were spoken in inside a hostile frame. Both facts belong in the record."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Sahagún, Florentine Codex, especially books VI and XI; Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas (1629); Codex Borgia",
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim stands in a received work at a passage that can be named, but the passage was read in a second work that quotes or reports it, because the first could not be obtained."
      },
      "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vermitteltes Zeugnis",
        "en": "Mediated attestation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Admissible only where the work itself is out of reach — not digitised, out of print, held internally, in a language not commanded. Effort is not a reason: the mode exists so that unreachable material can enter at its true strength, not so that reachable material can enter cheaply. See SPEC §10 and SPEC §15.",
          "Every claim carrying this mode must name the intermediary with pm:readVia, enforced by pm:MediatedAttestationShape. dcterms:source keeps naming the work that carries the claim, because that is what a later reader must obtain; pm:readVia names what the writer of the node actually had in front of him. Collapsing the two would hide exactly the fact this mode exists to record.",
          "Weaker than pm:textualAttestation and stronger than nothing, and the record has to be able to say which of the two it has. A claim read through an intermediary carries that intermediary's selection, its translation and its silence; a reader who cannot see that the passage came second-hand cannot weigh any of it."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative transmission of the science of letters and of the divine names, with squares, talismans and hours of working."
      },
      "id": "pmt:IslamicLetterMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Islamische Buchstaben- und Namensmagie",
        "en": "Islamic letter and name magic"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The ascription of the best known handbook to al-Būnī is contested, and the corpus grew after him under his name. The dispute is the first node this tradition should carry."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Šams al-maʿārif corpus ascribed to al-Būnī; Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OloliuhquiYieldsAnswer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Trinken bringt die Antwort hervor",
        "en": "The drinking yields the answer"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named in the teaching list as the one who disclosed the undoing of spells."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Armaros",
      "label": {
        "de": "Armaros",
        "en": "Armaros"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Hermani"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1925",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a thing given holds a spiritual power belonging to its giver, that this power seeks to return to its place of origin, and that the obligation to make a return gift follows from it. The claim is put forward in a work of nineteen twenty-five, on the strength of the single Māori passage above."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HauAsSpiritOfTheThing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Gabe trägt einen Geist, der ihre Erwiderung erzwingt",
        "en": "The gift carries a spirit that compels its return"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as an origination and not as a Māori claim because the generalisation is the author's. The letter speaks of one kind of valuable and of a forest rite; the obligation of the return gift as a universal law of archaic exchange begins here. That the reading of the passage was itself contested from nineteen twenty-nine onward is recorded in prima-materia#107, because those sides were not read at the wording in this run."
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "the Māori doctrine of the spirit of the gift, and beyond it a universal principle of archaic exchange",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Marcel Mauss",
      "source": "Marcel Mauss, The Gift, translated by Ian Cunnison (London: Cohen and West, 1966), pp. 8-10",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the claim that the tummo practice produces inner heat, taken up because this is the one pairing in the collection whose capacity has an outward side that instruments reach."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TummoHeatExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfung der Tummo-Hitze-Behauptung",
        "en": "Examination of the tummo heat claim"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "What the measurements bear on is the somatic claim and nothing beyond it. Reported temperature rises are consistent with the practice producing heat; they say nothing about the cosmology the practice is embedded in, and the node must not be read as though they did. The reported effect also depends on the forceful-breath component together with the visualisation, which means the claim that is actually supported is narrower than the one the tradition makes."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:protocolledPractice"
      },
      "pm:baseRate": "Temperature change under comparable breathing effort without the practice, in matched participants.",
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "H. Benson et al., Body temperature changes during the practice of g-tummo yoga, Nature 295 (1982), 234-236",
        "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "H. Benson and colleagues; M. Kozhevnikov and colleagues",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "Practitioners of the forceful-breath tummo method show no rise in measured body temperature beyond that produced by the breathing alone in matched non-practitioners.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-28",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DirectKnowledgesPresupposeConcentration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die höheren Kenntnisse setzen die Sammlung voraus",
        "en": "The direct knowledges presuppose concentration"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The handbook states that the six kinds of direct knowledge are arrived at with the support of perfected concentration and by nothing besides that.",
      "pm:dependentStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Cetopariyanana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbacakkhu"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbasota"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
        }
      ],
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Samadhi"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission founded on Mani's own account of a companion who appeared to him and disclosed what he taught."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Manichaean",
      "label": {
        "de": "Manichäische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Manichaean transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Cologne Mani Codex carries a first-person account of the syzygos, which is rare for late antiquity and rarer still with a name attached."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kephalaia; Cologne Mani Codex; the Coptic psalms and the Turfan fragments",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the source says happens if the prior step is passed over."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:consequenceOfSkipping",
      "label": {
        "de": "Folge des Überspringens",
        "en": "consequence of skipping"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required wherever the strength is pm:statedNecessity. Claiming that a text sets a necessity obliges the claimant to say what the text says goes wrong without it; a necessity nobody can quote a consequence for is an order read as a necessity."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source only presents the material in a sequence. Any necessity is the reader's, and belongs to whoever read it that way."
      },
      "id": "pm:presentationOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Darstellungsreihenfolge",
        "en": "Presentation order"
      },
      "type": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Those who have reached the third degree are said to see distant things even against their will. A first-person report is given: a man who surrendered his drum to the recorder on complaint confessed with tears that though he parted with it and made no other, he would have the same visions as before; he instanced the recorder himself, giving a true and particular account of what had happened to him on his journey to Lapland; and he complained that he did not know how to make use of his eyes, since things altogether distant were presented to them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTheSightItself",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sehen kommt ungerufen und lässt sich nicht ablegen",
        "en": "The sight comes unbidden and cannot be laid down"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The same passage carries a claim of a verified hit — the man is said to have told the recorder correctly what had happened on his own journey — and the recorder reports it without endorsing it. It is a claim standing in the work and no more; nothing about the account can be checked, and the man is not named.",
          "The warning is not against misuse and not against the anger of a spirit. It is that the capacity, once complete, is not under the will and cannot be given up with the instrument. That is a caution of a kind this graph holds very few of, and it is the transmission warning against the thing it exists to produce."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:DistantSight"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1975",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the Egyptian term for becoming, coming into being, personified in the scarab god and signifying self-generation and the dawn, names the transformation and evolution of the will, and that it is uttered as the word of a new age succeeding the age of indulgence."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Xeper als das Wort der Selbsthervorbringung",
        "en": "Xeper as the word of self-generation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an origination because of the shape of the claim rather than its content. The word is Egyptian and its lexical sense is not in dispute; what begins in 1975 is its use as the formula of a path, in a sequence of ages with named predecessors. The Egyptological reference is present in the source and does not make the doctrine ancient.",
          "This is the end state the milieu sets against dissolution, and it is worth recording in the author's own terms rather than in the summaries: not the preservation of an ego as ordinarily meant, but a self-generation modelled on a god who brings himself forth. A record that reports it as mere ego-preservation reports the criticism and not the claim."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A doctrine recovered from Egyptian antiquity, reported as the teaching the word is drawn from rather than as a formula first uttered in 1975.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Michael A. Aquino",
      "source": "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsParakayaPravesa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama bringt den Eintritt in einen anderen Körper hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama yields entry into another body"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The vernacular transmission of blessings, charms and cheap printed handbooks for healing, protection and the recovery of stolen goods."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Europäische Volksmagie",
        "en": "European folk magic"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Merseburg charms; Lacnunga; Bald's Leechbook; Romanusbüchlein; Sechstes und Siebtes Buch Mosis; Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source states that no Zande would report the declaration of an oracle otherwise than it was given, and that a man who wishes to lie fabricates a declaration without consulting the oracle at all. Against the fabrication the transmission sets three things: the elder who makes an official test takes two or three witnesses with him, the counterparty consults his own oracles to check the first man's, and a man who believes he was not fairly tested may appeal to the king."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FabricationRatherThanMisreport",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Lüge ist eine erfundene Befragung, nie eine falsch berichtete",
        "en": "The lie is a fabricated consultation, never a misreported one"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The cross-check by the counterparty is the part worth noticing. It is a control against the questioner rather than against the instrument, and it is the only one in this file that does not depend on the instrument being honest.",
          "The sanction is stated and is not merely moral: the king sends for the elder, tells him he is a cheat and a liar, and bars him from ever again conducting official consultations."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 37 and p. 154",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Gabonese transmission of an initiation carried by a root preparation, in which the initiate is expected to meet the dead and to report the meeting."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Bwiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bwiti",
        "en": "Bwiti"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The expected content of the experience is stated in advance, which makes the tradition unusually well suited to an examination of what such expectation determines."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Kurdish transmission carried chiefly in hymn and recitation, teaching successive returns of the soul."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yazidi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jesidische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Yazidi transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two books circulated as its scripture are held by most scholarship to be forgeries of the early twentieth century, which makes the corpus question itself the first thing to decide."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Qewls (hymns) in the published collections; the disputed Mišefa Reš and Kitêba Cilwe",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1995",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that emotional and mental states occupy fixed positions on a single numbered scale, ascending from shame through fear, anger and pride to reason, love, joy, peace and enlightenment, and that the position can be established by testing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Ordnung der Affekte nach gemessener Stufe",
        "en": "The ranking of emotions by measured level"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No tradition located so far ranks emotions by reach or by energy on a single ordered scale. The seventeen steps, the relative order of shame, guilt, fear and anger, and the placement of willingness are the specific things a later list can be measured against, and they begin here."
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A perennial map of consciousness, reported as the distilled agreement of the mystical traditions.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "David R. Hawkins",
      "source": "D. R. Hawkins, Power vs. Force (1995)",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Cases have been recorded and show no deviation from the stated base rate."
      },
      "id": "pm:casesWithoutDeviation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fälle ohne Abweichung",
        "en": "Cases without deviation"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantHearing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernhören hervor",
        "en": "The second degree yields hearing at a distance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "One node per pairing, following traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl, because a protocol would have to take the pairings one at a time and because the four capacities of this degree are not equally examinable."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideDistantHearing"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission received by Darryl Anka since 1984, teaching that acting on the strongest available excitement is the means by which one comes to occupy a different reality."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BasharTransmission",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bashar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Bashar transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Unusually operative for a channelled corpus: the teaching is given as steps with stated conditions of failure, in front of audiences, on record."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change; the recorded session transcripts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The narrator states of her husband Padloq, whom she calls by a name she glosses as the one who keeps her alive, that he has the gift of second sight and always knows when anything remarkable is going to happen. The case she gives is a dream, on a journey from Iglulik to Tununeq, that a friend of his had been eaten by his nearest kin; on that journey they met the woman who had eaten her own family."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dem Mann der Erzählerin wird das zweite Gesicht zugeschrieben",
        "en": "The narrator's husband is said to have the gift of second sight"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is universal in form, which is what makes it examinable: he always knows. One confirming case is related and no failing case is; the case is retrospective and was told after the meeting it is said to have foretold. The protocol stands at examinations/inuit-second-sight.ttl."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSight"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": "Padloq, the narrator Takornâq's husband",
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The western alchemical transmission, in which the stages of the work are described at once as operations and as states of the operator."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LatinAlchemy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lateinische Alchemie",
        "en": "Latin alchemy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The double reading is not a modern imposition and not a settled fact either. It is a dispute the corpus carries in itself and the graph should record as one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Turba philosophorum; Rosarium philosophorum; Aurora consurgens",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PracticePresupposesInitiation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Praxis setzt die Initiation voraus",
        "en": "The practice presupposes initiation"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:SaktipataTransmission"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Diksa"
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Upaniṣads as the speculative layer of the Vedic corpus."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Upanisadic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Upaniṣadische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Upaniṣadic tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Chāndogya 7.15.1 carries the edition it was read from and was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For 1.11.5 the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Chāndogya Upaniṣad",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Balinese palm-leaf transmission of healing, protective diagrams and the composition of the person, at the far edge of the Indian sphere of influence."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BalineseTantra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Balinesische Lontar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Balinese lontar transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Written, catalogued and barely used by comparative work. The contact question is genuinely open: how much is Indian import and how much is local is undecided in the literature."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Usada healing lontar; Kanda Pat; the Aji Saraswati texts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An ascent in thirty steps, running from renunciation and exile through the active virtues to stillness, prayer, freedom from the passions and love, and passing in its course from human labour to what is given."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Leiter der dreißig Stufen",
        "en": "The ladder of thirty steps"
      },
      "source": "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An efficacy that belongs to someone. In the passages read it is always somebody's: a father's, which may inspire the son so that he becomes the living representative of that mana and of the karakia with it; or an opponent's, which is asked for by name in an invocation before a stroke is made."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Mana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mana",
        "en": "Mana"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "What the witness does not show is the thing the term is famous for elsewhere. In these passages mana is never a quantity of impersonal force at large in the world; it is held, inherited, represented, and asked for from a named holder. The reading that made it an impersonal power belongs to a later literature and to a different archipelago, and it is not entered here because that literature has not yet been opened at the wording. See prima-materia#103 for the origination and prima-materia#105 for the philological dispute over the word itself."
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 54 and 62",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The question has not been carried out. Registered explicitly rather than left absent, because an unstated contact route and a route shown to be absent are the difference between a witness and a candidate."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg unentschieden",
        "en": "Contact route undecided"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Ten states arising in practice, among them luminosity, rapture, tranquillity, pleasure and resolve, which are not defilements in themselves but become obstacles when taken as proof of attainment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Unvollkommenheiten der Einsicht",
        "en": "The imperfections of insight"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook illustrates the error with the monk who held himself awakened for sixty years. This is the tradition's own answer to the question of how a practitioner mistakes a state for a result."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:Piti"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about the age of the society. One side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, assembled from older elements in response to epidemic, trade and mission. The other points to a birchbark scroll fragment from an archaeological context, radiocarbon dated to about the middle of the sixteenth century, which is earlier than any documented European contact with the central Algonkians."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Gesellschaft dem europäischen Kontakt vorausgeht",
        "en": "Whether the society precedes European contact"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as a node because everything else in this file depends on it. If the society is a formation of the contact period, its agreement with anything the graph already holds has to be weighed differently, and the value at pm:contactRoute changes with the outcome.",
          "Only one side was read at the wording in this run. The dating, the laboratory reference and the argument against the nativistic reading were read in the note of nineteen eighty-one; the nativistic reading itself was read only as that note quotes it, and the paper of nineteen sixty-two that states it in full was not obtained. See prima-materia#118."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmt:Midewiwin"
      },
      "source": [
        "Harold Hickerson, The Chippewa and Their Neighbors: A Study in Ethnohistory (New York: Holt, Rinehart and Winston, 1970), p. 63, as quoted in Kidd 1981, p. 42",
        "Kenneth E. Kidd, A Radiocarbon Date on a Midewiwin Scroll from Burntside Lake, Ontario, Ontario Archaeology 35 (1981), pp. 41-43"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of seated practice as enactment rather than means, together with a literature on the illnesses that practice itself produces."
      },
      "id": "pmt:JapaneseZen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Japanischer Zen",
        "en": "Japanese Zen"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Hakuin's account of meditation sickness is a self-warning of the strongest kind the project can hold: a text prescribing a practice and describing its casualties, with a remedy."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Dōgen, Shōbōgenzō; Hakuin, Yasenkanna; Hakuin, Orategama",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:QiListeningPresupposesEmptying",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Hören mit dem Qi setzt das Leeren des Herzens voraus",
        "en": "Attending with the qi presupposes the emptying of the heart"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "In the dialogue the disciple is told outright that he is not yet ready, and is sent back to empty the heart before anything further; the hearing that stops at the ears and the heart-mind does not reach the qi at all.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Qi"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart"
      },
      "source": "Zhuangzi 4",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of ascribing a capacity, property, or deed to a subject. A lost faculty is never a finding; it is an ascription made in a work, and the work is what the graph can hold."
      },
      "id": "pm:Attributing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zuschreiben",
        "en": "Attributing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of change-based divination, of fate calculation from birth data and of the siting of dwellings and graves."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChineseDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chinesische Divinations- und Siedlungslehre",
        "en": "Chinese divinatory and siting transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Claims here attach to datable outcomes, which makes them among the few in the corpus that could be falsified without a laboratory."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhouyi with the Xici commentary; Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing; Guo Pu, Zangshu",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The word as the recorder of sixteen twenty-nine glosses it: one veiled or disguised under the appearance of an animal. He connects the name with calendars he had found among such people, which distribute the names of animals across the days."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Nahualli",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nahualli",
        "en": "Nahualli"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The gloss is the recorder's and is given in Spanish; the Nahuatl word is his lemma. That he offers an etymology at all is worth noting, because it shows he had the word from the people and not from a predecessor's book."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative Jewish transmission of adjurations, amulets and bowls, distinct from the speculative literature and older than it in its written witnesses."
      },
      "id": "pmt:JewishPracticalMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jüdische Praxismagie",
        "en": "Jewish practical magic"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Sefer ha-Razim; Ḥarba de-Moshe; the Cairo Genizah magical fragments; the Aramaic incantation bowls",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsThoughtReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der vierte Grad bringt das Gedankenlesen hervor",
        "en": "The fourth degree yields the reading of thoughts"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideThoughtReading"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The undoing of a binding laid by another, held in the text as a distinct art from the laying of it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LoosingOfSpells",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lösen von Beschwörungen",
        "en": "Loosing of spells"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first object of the ascent, from which the climb begins."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BeautyOfOneBody",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schönheit eines Körpers",
        "en": "The beauty of one body"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 210a-211c",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Platonic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The standing of a protocol: how far the attempt to fail a claim has actually been carried."
      },
      "id": "pm:ExaminationState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfstand",
        "en": "Examination State"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The first two states and the third must never collapse into one another. A claim nobody has devised a way to examine is not a claim that has been examined and found wanting. A record that reports both as unsupported would not be reporting on the claims but on the reach of its own instruments."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stated condition under which the claim counts as refuted."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:falsifiedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "widerlegt durch",
        "en": "falsified by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the soul leaves the body and returns to it, reported of Aristeas of Proconnesus and of Hermotimus of Clazomenae."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SoulJourney",
      "label": {
        "de": "Seelenreise ohne den Körper",
        "en": "Journey of the soul apart from the body"
      },
      "source": [
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KokabelTeachesConstellations",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kokabel offenbart die Sternbilder",
        "en": "Kokabel discloses the constellations"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Constellations"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Kokabel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A mixture of air and fire that pervades bodies and, by its tension, holds them in cohesion; a principle of the world rather than a capacity of a practitioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Pneuma",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pneuma",
        "en": "Pneuma"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The associated doctrines are that of tension and that of total mixture, by which two bodies occupy the same place throughout."
      },
      "source": "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:StoicPhysics"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The continuous inward prayer that gives the tradition its practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Herzensgebet",
        "en": "The prayer of the heart"
      },
      "source": [
        "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The record the priests keep on birchbark, incised in fine lines, giving pictorially the ground plan of the degrees to which the owner is entitled."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BirchBarkRecord",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Birkenrindenkarte",
        "en": "The birchbark chart"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two things follow from this and both bear on how the rest of the file is to be read. The society keeps a written record by its own means, which is unusual among the transmissions this graph registers as lacking a corpus. And the capacities of the degrees are stated as readings of marks on such a chart, so what the report carries is an informant's exegesis of a pictograph and not a free verbal account; that is a different kind of witness, and arguably a firmer one, because the marks were there before the question was asked."
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-165",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The gathering and preparation of plant material for pharmacological or operative use."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RootCutting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wurzelschneiden",
        "en": "Root-cutting"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A negative finding, held as a node so that it is not lost: the old traditions test a claimed movement by its origin and its fruits, and none of those examined here tests a claimed capacity by counting hits against the rate obtainable by guessing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NoQuantitativeVerification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Keine Zählung gegen den Zufall in den alten Quellen",
        "en": "No count against chance in the old sources"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Any protocol that counts outcomes against a base rate is a modern addition and is to be recorded as one, not as the recovery of an ancient method."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:TestingOfThoughts"
      },
      "source": [
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 313-336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission received by Jane Roberts from 1963, teaching that reality is constructed by belief and that the constructing can be observed and altered by the one who does it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SethMaterial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Seth-Material",
        "en": "The Seth material"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "States its central claim in a form that asks for a procedure: beliefs are to be examined and changed, and the change is said to show. The sessions were numbered and dated from the start."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Roberts, Seth Speaks; Roberts, The Nature of Personal Reality; The Early Sessions",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of Greek and Latin historiographical and doxographical reports of extraordinary capacities."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GraecoRoman",
      "label": {
        "de": "Griechisch-römisches Zeugnis",
        "en": "Graeco-Roman testimony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The book and chapter numbers are stable across the modern critical editions, which is why the loss is smaller here than elsewhere; it is still a loss, because it is unknown whether the passages were read in the original or in translation.",
          "The file records that a route to Indian material has been proposed for the older Greek transmission and not shown. Disputed is therefore the state of the question, not a hedge."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The counting of the sixty-year interval by means that are stated to be mnemonic rather than astronomical: thirty biennial millet-beer drinkings, each marked with a cowry laid down by the eldest; a millet-porridge figure above the sanctuary door in which a point is added each year; and a painted line of chevrons called sigi lugu, count of the Sigui, each tooth standing for twenty years."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SiguiReckoning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Auszählen des Sigui-Abstands",
        "en": "Reckoning the interval between two Sigui"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorders report that the rule is theoretical and that the practice departed from it: between the Sigui of the beginning of the century and 1931 only one drinking was held, and the cowries for the missing thirty years were laid down together in a single heap afterwards. The reckoning that is described as regular is thus reconstructed in arrears, and the article says so."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 275-277",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account gives the period of the seed-star's orbit as some fifty years and the interval between two Sigui as sixty, and states in a footnote that there is here a contradiction which has not yet been resolved. It then offers, as a question and not as a finding, that the last ceremony may have been brought forward and that the profane may be kept in the belief that the official period is sixty years."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FiftyAgainstSixty",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fünfzig gegen sechzig: Bahnperiode und Zeremonienabstand",
        "en": "Fifty years against sixty: the period of the orbit and the interval of the ceremony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered as a dispute although both sides stand in the same work, because the work states the disagreement itself rather than smoothing it. The dispute is decisive for everything the outside reception built on the passage: fifty years is the figure that matches the companion of Sirius, sixty is the figure the ceremony actually runs on, and only the second is attested by anything a later worker could count.",
          "The same footnote that reports the fifty-year period cites a French astronomical periodical of 1931 for the closeness of that period to the companion of Sirius. Whether the fifty came from the informants or from the reading is the question this graph cannot decide from the article alone; see prima-materia#250."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:PoToloOrbit"
      },
      "source": [
        "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 282 with its first footnote",
        "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 283 with its fifth footnote"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The pronominal reach of an awareness act, ranging from individual through dyadic and collective to universal."
      },
      "id": "pm:AwarenessSpace",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bewusstseinsraum",
        "en": "Awareness Space"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about a claim, carried on within a tradition, between traditions, or among the scholars who edit their texts."
      },
      "id": "pm:Disputing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bestreiten",
        "en": "Disputing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Without this class a disagreement can only be recorded by silently preferring one side, or by leaving the claim out because it is contested. Both losses are worse than the disagreement. A dispute node names what is at issue and cites each side, and a dispute with only one source cited is not a dispute."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The restoration of a withered hand and of sight, ascribed to the touch of a ruler in Alexandria."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HealingByTouch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Heilung durch Berührung",
        "en": "Healing by touch"
      },
      "source": [
        "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The ascription under which the claim is commonly reported: the tradition, the age or the authority it is credited to and does not have."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Originating",
      "id": "pm:circulatesAs",
      "label": {
        "de": "zirkuliert als",
        "en": "circulates as"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first of the three trainings, on which the handbook is built as a commentary on the verse about the tangle."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Sila",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sittlichkeit",
        "en": "Virtue"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two marks set on the practice: one is not to hear the sound of one's own breathing, and a feather held before nose and mouth is not to stir; and one is always to give out less than one has taken in."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der für den Übenden selbst unhörbare Atem, kürzer aus als ein",
        "en": "The breath inaudible to its own bearer and shorter out than in"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The intake is at the nose and the release at the mouth. The distribution is the reverse of the one a modern draft assigns, which treats mouth breathing as a fault on the intake and says nothing of the release. The passage also fixes the pace by an external test rather than by a count of seconds, which is what makes it examinable at all. See prima-materia#26."
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The witness reports that a person would not dare to spit in the presence of anyone he feared might be disposed to injure him, if that person had a reputation for skill in this art."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MakutuCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nicht spucken in Gegenwart eines Kundigen",
        "en": "Not to spit in the presence of one skilled in the art"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A caution that names its own detection problem: the material the art needs is produced involuntarily, so the guard is against being present rather than against acting."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Makutu"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Whoever is to enter one of the greater offices fasts: a month, in other places six, in others a year, eating neither salt nor chilli, not sleeping with his wife, not washing and not combing. In some places he must not bring his hands to his body, and one who kept it said that in the time of his fast he scratched himself with a little stick."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fasten vor dem Antritt eines höheren Amtes",
        "en": "The fast before entering a major office"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of Chinese texts and practices concerned with the cultivation and circulation of qi."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Daoist",
      "label": {
        "de": "Daoistische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Daoist tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Deliberately not documented. From the arrival of Buddhism in China the route is plain and the file's material lies mostly after it; for the earlier strata it is not, and the whole independence value of the Chinese material hangs on where that line falls.",
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citations of the jade inscription name the work they were read in. For Zhuangzi 4, Baopuzi neipian 8 and Chen Zhixu's Jindan dayao the edition or translation is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those three passages are to be reopened, and for the Jindan dayao the juan division is itself edition-dependent.",
          "Thin on purpose, and the thinness is the finding. Of the material this tradition is credited with, two places could be run down to a text that names its own words: the jade inscription, which is the earliest datable witness located for a deliberate circulation of qi through the body, and the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, which states the manner of the breathing in terms an examination could take hold of. How early the inscription is remains contested, and the dispute node holds that. What is widely reported and was not run down to a citable passage stays out rather than being approximated: the absorption of solar, lunar and stellar qi (fuqi) and the abstention from grain (bigu) are recorded at prima-materia#17, not here."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the bounded self is to be strengthened and kept as its own absolute, and treats its dissolution as what is to be avoided."
      },
      "id": "pm:separativeTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Absonderndes Ziel",
        "en": "Separative telos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Distinct from self-assertion, ambition or the pursuit of power, none of which say anything about an end state. What places a path here is a claim about where it ends, not about how its practitioners behave."
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A tradition attesting the correspondence for which no route of contact is documented, so that its testimony is not the same statement counted a second time."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:independentAttestation",
      "label": {
        "de": "unabhängige Bezeugung",
        "en": "independent attestation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The earlier definition called this the only kind of attestation that makes a correspondence a finding. It is not: it is the kind that makes the count honest. A correspondence carried by reception is still a finding about what was carried; what it may not be is a tally of independent witnesses."
      },
      "range": "pm:Tradition",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Operative consciousness in which an assertion is made from direct insight rather than from historical reconstruction."
      },
      "id": "pm:GnosticState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gnostischer Zustand",
        "en": "Gnostic state"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Use this context when a triple expresses a claim from within a tradition's first-person perspective."
      },
      "type": "pm:AwarenessContext"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus",
        "en": "Concentration presupposes virtue"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Samadhi"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Sila"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Vital breath, presented as that on which the parts of the person rest and out of which beings come forth, in the image of a wheel resting on its hub."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Prana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prāṇa",
        "en": "Prāṇa"
      },
      "source": [
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 1.11.5",
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Upanisadic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what the source text says, not about how to interpret it. The manuscript reads the possessive of the second person, which makes the hau that of the giver and keeps the giver attached to the thing after it has changed hands. The printed version of 1909 has the definite article in both key sentences, which makes the hau that of the object. Every later reading of the passage rests on one of the two."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HauOfTheGiftWordingDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wessen Hau: der Wortlaut des Ranapiri-Briefes",
        "en": "Whose hau: the wording of the Ranapiri letter"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as a dispute over the text rather than over its meaning, because that is the part a later reader can check and because the substitution was made silently. Both sides were read in the same study that reports the substitution, so the collation itself has not been verified against the manuscript or against the 1909 printing in this run."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:HauOfTheGift"
      },
      "source": [
        "Elsdon Best, Maori Forest Lore, part III, Transactions and Proceedings of the New Zealand Institute 42 (1909), p. 439",
        "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271, as collated in Hēnare, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A star named after the fonio grain, said to be white where Sirius is seen red, to be the first thing created, to be the egg of the world out of which everything visible and invisible developed, and to consist of a metal called sagala. It is called at once the smallest of all things and the heaviest, weighed in the account at four hundred and eighty donkey loads, so that all earthly beings together could not lift it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PoTolo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pô tolo, der Digitaria-Stern",
        "en": "Pô tolo, the Digitaria star"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The number four hundred and eighty is glossed in the article itself as the product of the base eighty and the count of tens in the older base sixty, and is said to be used as the symbol of the greatest of numbers. A reader who takes it for a measured mass is reading against the note printed beneath it."
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 284 and 287",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Whoever comes into contact with the poison abstains from sexual relations, from the flesh of the elephant, from fish, from two glutinous vegetables named in the source, and from smoking hemp. Sexual abstinence is set at five or six days and abstinence from the forbidden foods at three or four, though the source records that the period is not fixed and that men give different estimates."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BengeTaboos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Tabus vor der Berührung des Orakelgifts",
        "en": "The taboos kept before touching the oracle poison"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A man asked to operate who has not kept the sexual taboo says that he has eaten the first of the two vegetables, and everyone understands the euphemism. The source adds that he may use the same words when he simply does not want the work, which makes the phrase a refusal as well as a confession."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 132-133",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that self and non-self are mutually superimposed, that this superimposition is what learned men call nescience, and that it is the presupposition of every practical distinction — those of ordinary life and those laid down in scripture alike — between means of knowledge, objects of knowledge and knowing persons, in texts of injunction and prohibition and in texts concerned with final release."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SuperimpositionUnderliesEveryPath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Überlagerung trägt jeden Weg, auch die Texte über die Befreiung",
        "en": "Superimposition underlies every path, including the texts on release"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "For the axes in prima-materia#52 this is neither a value nor a third position but a denial of the question's standing. It does not say that the bounded self is dissolved or kept; it says that the level at which either could be said is the level of nescience. Recorded so that a scheme which files it as a unitive telos can be checked against the argument. See prima-materia#66.",
          "The clause that decides the matter is the last one. Not only the ordinary distinctions but the texts on release itself are said to rest on the superimposition. A path, a choice between paths and the scripture that recommends one are therefore all inside what the argument calls nescience — not false, but valid only where the superimposition holds.",
          "The same section removes the one who would choose. Without the superimposition the self does not become a knowing agent at all, and where there is no knowing agent the means of knowledge do not operate. The agent that a choice between paths presupposes is thus itself among the things superimposed."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction, at the definition of superimposition as nescience and the statement that all practical distinctions including the texts on final release presuppose it (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AdvaitaVedanta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The clearing of impurity from the channels, at which the breath practice is said to aim, and whose accomplishment the text says shows outwardly in the body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NadiPurification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Reinigung der Kanäle",
        "en": "Purification of the channels"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher whose teaching concerns celestial signs; the two recensions differ on which."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Baraqel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Baraqel",
        "en": "Baraqel"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Baraqijal"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "During the fast everyone confesses to the holder of that office, both sitting on the ground, at places appointed in the open country. What is confessed is theft, ill-treatment of others, having more than one woman, adultery, and failure to attend to the huacas; inward faults are not confessed, and simple fornication is not held a fault at all. Powders of the offerings are laid on a flat stone and the penitent is made to blow them; a small stone called pasca, which means pardon, is rubbed on his head with ground white maize, and the head is washed in a stream or where two rivers meet, which is called tincuna."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Beichte während des Fastens",
        "en": "The confession made during the fast"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "It is also done outside the feasts, when a man is sick, because they understand that their ancestors and huacas are angry at their faults and that this is why they fall ill. Some purify themselves without telling anyone, by rubbing the head with the pasca and washing it in a river, saying that the water carries the faults away; and one man said the specialist took him and his wife to a large ditch, washed them, made them put on new clothes and leave the old ones on the road, saying that their faults stayed there and that whoever took the clothes would take them."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SuccessPresupposesNadiPurification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erfolg der Praxis setzt die Reinigung der Kanäle voraus",
        "en": "Success in the practice presupposes purification of the channels"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The text directs the breath practice at clearing the impurity of the central channel and gives the outward signs by which accomplished purification is known. It makes success depend on the purification without stating in these verses what befalls one who proceeds without it, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NadiPurification"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A division of practitioners into three kinds, the bound, the heroic and the divine, which governs the rites a person is competent for. The source describes the divine as all but a god, pure of heart, alike towards opposites and unattached, and the bound as one who must gather his own offerings and who may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BhavaClassification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drei Dispositionen: paśu, vīra, divya",
        "en": "The three dispositions: paśu, vīra, divya"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the tradition's own answer to the question why one person takes one way and another another: not by choosing, but by what he is held competent for. The text presses the point polemically — it argues that if the bound disposition cannot exist in this age, the divine cannot either, and that only the heroic rites bear visible fruit. A record that reports the two paths as a choice between camps reports the later Western reading and not this one. See prima-materia#54."
      },
      "source": [
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.50-57 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.19-20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two pieces of wood, the upper rubbed across the moistened surface of the lower. Sticking gives one answer and smooth running the other, and a lid that runs from side to side or alternately sticks and runs is taken as a refusal to answer. The instrument is the cheapest and most frequently used of the oracles and the one least trusted."
      },
      "id": "pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung des Reibbrettorakels",
        "en": "Consulting the rubbing-board oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The earliest witness describes the same instrument two generations before the monograph, under the name borru, made from the wood of a tree he names, and reports that it was consulted before any undertaking of importance and hidden from the Muslim traders. The two descriptions of the mechanism agree; the names do not, and the divergence stands on the dispute node at the end of this file."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 170-171",
        "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The eastern European transmission of spoken charms for healing, protection and binding, fixed in writing only by nineteenth-century collectors."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BalticSlavicCharm",
      "label": {
        "de": "Baltische und slawische Besprechungstradition",
        "en": "Baltic and Slavic charm transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The late fixation is the whole difficulty: the corpus is a collection and not a work, and the project has no attestation mode for that yet."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The published zagovory collections; the Latvian dainas collections",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A bowl of hollowed pine, fir or birch with a skin stretched over it, painted with figures of the gods, the angels, the evil spirits, the associates of the noaidi, the sun, the dwellings, the sacred mountains and the beasts of the country. A ring or a brass cover is laid on the skin, the skin is struck with a hammer of reindeer horn, and the ring travels across the painted figures. Where it comes to rest, or which way it runs, gives the answer."
      },
      "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung der Trommel",
        "en": "Consulting the drum"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two recorders describe the same instrument ninety years apart and their descriptions of the mechanism agree in every part that matters: the hammer of horn, the ring or cover laid on the skin, the painted field, the reading from where the ring goes. The later adds what the earlier does not: that the drum is not thought fit for use unless got by inheritance, and that women may not touch it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48 and p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, their Language, Manners and Religion, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, Of the Magic Arts of the Laplanders, pp. 474-477"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Refining essence into breath, breath into spirit, and spirit back into emptiness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NeidanThreeStages",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drei Stufen des inneren Elixiers",
        "en": "The three stages of the inner elixir"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A dating warning belongs on this node. The sequence is routinely attributed to the Cantong qi or to the Wuzhen pian, but neither carries it as a fixed numbered progression; the Wuzhen pian sets only a precedence of vital force before inner nature, and of doing before non-doing. The earliest ordered witness located for the full triad is the Yuan-period Jindan dayao."
      },
      "source": "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The keeping of the great mask carved for each Sigui in a rock shelter beside those of the preceding ceremonies, and of the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each Sigui in the hogon's house. Both accumulate as series, and the article treats the series as the firmer evidence that a ceremony took place at all."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SiguiMaterialRecord",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bewahrung des Sigui in Holz und Faser",
        "en": "Keeping the Sigui in wood and fibre"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the one part of the account that leaves an object a later worker could count and date independently of any informant. The article reports nine masts at Ibi in 1931 with traces of three more, and eight or fourteen ferment-holders at Sanga and Arou. Held as a practice here and as a protocol at pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination in examinations/dogon-sirius.ttl."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 277-279",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Yoruba divinatory transmission: an enumerated set of signs obtained by casting, each sign carrying narratives that a diviner is expected to hold by memory and to recite on the sign falling."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Ifa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ifá",
        "en": "Ifá"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run: the section on Ifa in chapter II of the account of eighteen ninety-four, and its chapter IV for what that author says about the origin of the god; and the appendix of the volume of nineteen hundred and six, which reprints an extract on Ifa by a Yoruba bishop and a second extract by a Sierra Leonean author on geomancy. Left unread: the remaining chapters of both volumes, and the twentieth-century monographs named in the principal corpus, which are in copyright and available only as lending copies. The text of eighteen ninety-four was read in an electronic reproduction that carries no printed pagination, so the places are given by chapter and section and not by page.",
          "The closest thing to a written corpus that was never written. It is fixed, enumerated, internally ordered and transmitted through a pupilage of stated length, which is what made it the entry point chosen from the registry.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the correction is the main finding of the run that opened this file. The registry had recorded no route because none was looked for. One of the two extracts read here identifies the sixteen figures of the oracle with the apparatus of geomancy — twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, a judge and a further judge — which is the apparatus of the Arabic sand science and, through it, of material this graph already holds on its Mediterranean side. Until that identification is settled, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all.",
          "Two of the works cited in this file were read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints them. Neither extract carries an imprint there, so neither citation gives place or year. A separately published copy of the bishop's pamphlet was searched for and not found: under both the author's name and the confusable name of the Yoruba historian, in the large text archives and in a library catalogue aggregator, and in a bibliography of early Yorùbá books. The nodes resting on those extracts therefore carry pm:mediatedAttestation and name the appendix with pm:readVia, and they remain open posts."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Odù corpus as published in Bascom, Ifa Divination, and Abimbola, Ifá: An Exposition of Ifá Literary Corpus",
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The assignment is made at birth and not chosen later: the child is dedicated or subjected to the animal at the time it is born, on the strength of an agreement the recorder attributes to the parents."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NahualliGivenAtBirth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Tier wird bei der Geburt zugeteilt",
        "en": "The animal is assigned when the child is born"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder frames the agreement as a pact with the devil, which is his own theology and not the tradition's account of itself. What is left when that frame is subtracted is the timing — at birth — and the fact that someone else contracts it for the child. Both are entered; the frame is not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ApatheiaPresupposesAscesis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Apatheia setzt asketische Arbeit voraus",
        "en": "Apatheia presupposes ascetic labour"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "Apatheia is called the flower of ascetic labour and ascetic labour is defined as the keeping of the commandments, so that where the labour is absent the freedom from the passions does not appear at all rather than appearing in a lesser degree.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Apatheia"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Praktike"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An experiential account given in the first person by the one who had it, recorded once."
      },
      "id": "pm:firstPersonReport",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erfahrungsbericht",
        "en": "First-person report"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The doctrine holds that a man may carry the substance and never use it: it may remain inoperative, cool, throughout his life, and a man whose witchcraft never functions is hardly counted a witch. In practice the question asked of the oracles is never whether a man is a witch but whether at this moment he is bewitching the questioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ManguMayRemainInoperative",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Substanz kann kühl bleiben",
        "en": "The substance may remain cool"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The clause is what keeps the post-mortem from telling against the inheritance rule, and it belongs in the record beside that rule for exactly that reason. A finding of the substance where no injury was ever ascribed costs the doctrine nothing, and the absence of the substance is the only outcome that decides anything."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Repentance, placed first among the stations in the handbook enumeration."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Tawba",
      "label": {
        "de": "Umkehr",
        "en": "Turning back"
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission holds both at once: that the objects the witch-doctors draw from the bodies of their patients are produced by sleight of hand, with the manner of the deception described in detail by laymen, and that some of them nevertheless work real cures. Scepticism is directed at particular practitioners and turned into an explanation of their failures, so that it supports the standing of the rest."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FraudKnownAndBeliefHeld",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Betrug ist bekannt und der Glaube bleibt",
        "en": "The fraud is known and the belief is held"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A layman's account taken down by the recorder explains not only how the trick is done but how a cured man comes to believe he was cured by it. The doubt is stated in the terms of the transmission itself: a witch-doctor is a cheat because his medicines are poor and a liar because he has no witchcraft in him.",
          "This is the passage this graph most needs from the tradition, and it cuts against the usual report of it. What is described is not credulity but a transmission that has a settled place for its own failures, states its scepticism openly, and is not thereby weakened. Whether that makes it closed against refutation is the whole later argument, and none of that argument was read here."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 105-107",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a dead man over whom the words were spoken can come back among the living, held here to record that the one reported failure was assigned to a broken prohibition and that no condition for failing the claim itself is available."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitReturnFailureExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Wiederaufleben in der berichteten Fassung überhaupt scheitern kann",
        "en": "Whether the return of the dead can fail at all as the tradition reports it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No falsifying condition is given, and this is the permitted omission: the state says outright that none has been devised. Stating one here would misreport the situation, because the obstacle is not that nobody has looked but that the claim as transmitted has no failing case by construction. Whether a condition can be recovered by fixing the interval and the announcement in advance is an open question and belongs to whoever takes it up.",
          "The asymmetry with the protocol above is worth naming. Both claims come from the same narrator on adjacent pages; one is universal and therefore refutable by a single case, the other is hedged by a clause that absorbs every case. The difference is in the form of the claim and not in the strength of the testimony behind it.",
          "The passage is unusual in reporting a failure at all. What it does with the failure is the finding: the attempt is said to have miscarried because a third person had sewn new caribou skin where that is forbidden, so the practitioners and the claim are both left untouched. Success shows the power, failure shows the breach, and no outcome is left over that could count against the claim."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-31",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The root class of all ongoing acts modelled by prima-materia. Every substantive class in the ontology is a subclass of pm:Process."
      },
      "id": "pm:Process",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prozess",
        "en": "Process"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of imposing an order on material that did not carry it: a list, a sequence, a correspondence table. Named, dated, and attributed to the compiler who performed it, never to the source that is being ordered."
      },
      "id": "pm:Systematizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Systematisieren",
        "en": "Systematizing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Mangu, described as an oval blackish swelling attached to the edge of the liver and lying beneath the xiphoid cartilage, in which small objects are sometimes said to be found. A witch performs no rite, utters no spell and possesses no medicines: the act is held to proceed from the substance itself. The location is known because the substance was formerly extracted by autopsy."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ManguSubstance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hexerei als Substanz im Leib",
        "en": "Witchcraft as a substance in the body"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder adds that he believes the organ in question to be the small intestine in certain digestive periods. That is his identification and not the transmission's, and it is recorded here as his."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 1-2",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmt:Valentinian",
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      }
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that in the present age success is attained by Kaula worship alone, and that the mantra received from a Kaula brings knowledge of Brahman, where the mantra received from one of the lower dispositions reproduces that disposition in the receiver."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KaulaAboveTheOtherWays",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die kaulische Verehrung als der in diesem Zeitalter tragende Weg",
        "en": "Kaula worship as the way that succeeds in this age"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Kaula is set above the other ways here, not beside them. That is the same order Wilson translates from a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava, in which the left hand stands above the right and Kaula above all — an order the Western pair of names keeps while reversing its valuation. Two witnesses, one of them located by chapter and verse in a named translation and one not; the second is still to be found. See prima-materia#56."
      },
      "source": [
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra XIV.203 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Fon transmission of vodun as powers received into a person, with an initiation understood as a death and a return under a new name."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Vodun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fon-Vodun-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Fon vodun transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Maupoil, La géomancie à l'ancienne Côte des Esclaves; Herskovits, Dahomey",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The sign held to be the principal one, called the leader, the commander and the king. Its appearance on the divining bowl is taken to mean that the message delivered is of very great weight."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EjiOgbeFirstOfTheSigns",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eji Ogbe als erstes der Zeichen",
        "en": "Eji Ogbe as the first of the signs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A stated order of rank among the signs, and therefore a place where a later reader can check whether a modern list has kept the tradition's own order or imposed another."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Conditions that descend on the wayfarer as a gift and pass, distinguished in the handbooks from the stations precisely by not being attainable through effort."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ahwal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zustände",
        "en": "States"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No prerequisite claim is entered for these. The tradition holds that they cannot be worked for, which is a claim about the limits of any ordering and not an omission in this record."
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourteenfoldMastery",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierzehnfache Beherrschung voraus",
        "en": "Supernormal power presupposes the fourteenfold mastery"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The handbook states that it is not possible for a meditator even to begin to accomplish transformation by supernormal power without having first completed the development of control of the mind in those fourteen ways.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:FourteenfoldMastery"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two transmissions state the same claim in the same form. A named procedure, entered by stages, is said to produce two named capacities: perception of what is beyond the range of the senses, and knowledge of what is in another mind. In the one the procedure is the sustained application of attention to a single object, and the capacities follow verse by verse. In the other the procedure is an initiation into a graded society, and the capacities are read off the marks of the degree."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GradedAttainmentYieldsDistantPerceptionAndOtherMinds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine gestufte Erlangung soll Fernhören und Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervorbringen",
        "en": "A graded attainment is said to yield hearing at a distance and knowledge of other minds"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The contact question, carefully. No route is proposed by anyone between the Anishinaabe of the upper Great Lakes and the Sanskrit commentarial literature, and the dispute recorded at pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute does not touch this: whether the society formed before European contact or in reaction to it, a seventeenth-century Great Lakes formation has no access to the third pāda either way. What the dispute does affect is the age of the Midewiwin claim, not its independence.",
          "The one real objection to independence lies in the recording and not in the traditions, and it is stated here rather than left for a reader to find. The Midewiwin account was written down between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine, by which time the capacities of the third pāda had been in English-language circulation for a decade through theosophical publishing. Two things weigh against the objection. The recorder was a bureau ethnographer and not a member of that milieu, and the capacities are given as readings of lines incised on birchbark charts — marks that were on the bark before anyone asked what they meant. The objection is not thereby closed; it is entered so that it can be closed or sustained by someone who reads the charts.",
          "Two differences that a correspondence must not smooth. The Indian text ranks its capacities as obstacles with respect to the goal of the practice; the American report names no such rank and warns instead against the member who turns them to harm. And the Indian claim is a claim about attention, repeatable in principle by anyone who applies it, where the American one is a claim about an institution, obtainable only from priests and only for a fee that the report says impoverishes the candidate. The capacity is the same; what it costs and what it is for are not.",
          "What makes this worth holding is not the capacities, which are common, but the form of the claim. Both traditions bind the capacity to a procedure and to a place in a sequence, rather than ascribing it to a figure or a birth. That form is what a protocol can address; an ascription to a hero is not."
        ]
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideDistantHearing"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideThoughtReading"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "This is the case in the collection where the gathering situation decides, and it is entered in full because the date alone would clear a claim the date cannot clear. The American material was gathered between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine and printed in eighteen ninety-one; the capacities of the third pāda had been in English-language circulation for roughly a decade by then through theosophical publishing, so the fixation does not precede the material it agrees with. What carries the independence instead is twofold: the recorder was a bureau ethnographer and not a member of that milieu, and the capacities are given as readings of lines incised on birchbark charts, marks that were on the bark before anyone asked what they meant. The second point is what a date cannot supply, because it puts the agreement in an object rather than in an answer. The ground is not thereby closed; it can be sustained or overturned by someone who reads the charts.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": {
        "id": "pmt:Midewiwin"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
        "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168 and 170"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Yogasūtra of Patañjali and its commentarial literature."
      },
      "id": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pātañjala-Yoga",
        "en": "Pātañjala Yoga"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Only Yogasūtra III.38 carries the edition it was read from; it was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For every other citation in this file, and for the Yogabhāṣya, the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable: the session that entered them did not record it, and it cannot be reconstructed from the wording of the nodes. It is stated here rather than guessed, because an edition entered by inference would be a claim about the compiler's work that is not true. Until the passages are reopened, the verse numbers in this file stand on the received four-pāda division and nothing further."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "First-person exclusive awareness shared between two subjects (I and you)."
      },
      "id": "pm:dyadicAwareness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dyadisches Bewusstsein",
        "en": "Dyadic awareness"
      },
      "type": "pm:AwarenessSpace"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state of being sacred and thereby prohibited, held to be communicated from an atua or a sacred person to whatever they touch, and from that object onward to whatever it touches in turn."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Tapu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tapu",
        "en": "Tapu"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The re-transmission by contact is the part later theory generalised. A record that carries only the prohibition and not the transmissibility loses exactly what the nineteenth-century comparativists picked up and turned into an impersonal magical fluid. See prima-materia#103 and prima-materia#104 for the originations that are not yet entered."
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 25-26",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The seat, treated in the first chapter as the first limb of the practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Asana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sitzhaltung",
        "en": "Posture"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Vaiṣṇava tantric transmission, in which the practitioner's body is dissolved and rebuilt by placement before any worship may proceed."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Pancaratra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pāñcarātra",
        "en": "Pāñcarātra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The body-construction sequence is a prerequisite chain of unusual explicitness and belongs in the graph for that reason before any doctrine of the school does."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Jayākhya Saṃhitā; Lakṣmī Tantra; Sātvata Saṃhitā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Breath or spirit, named as the first of the ten emanations, from which air issues, from air water, and from water fire."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ruach",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ruach",
        "en": "Ruaḥ"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The citation was extended from 1:9-10 to 1:9-12 when the passage was reopened: in this numbering the first emanation is breath, the second breath from breath, and only the third and fourth carry water from breath and fire from water. The earlier range stopped two verses short of the sequence the definition states. The numbering is recension-dependent throughout, and the long and Saadia versions divide the same material otherwise.",
          "The text also has the patriarch stirring the letters with breath, which is the closest the source comes to breath as an operative medium; it remains an act of formation, not a conduction through the body."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)",
        "Sefer Yetzirah 6:7 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SeferYetzirah"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The emergence of a form out of a prior undifferentiated ground."
      },
      "id": "pm:Manifesting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Manifestieren",
        "en": "Manifesting"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Jain transmission of restraint, austerity and the shedding of karmic matter, with its own catalogue of powers said to arise along the way."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Jain",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jainistische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Jain transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A second capacity list formulated in the same region and period as the yogic one and not derived from it. Within the Indian sphere this is the closest thing to a control case."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ācārāṅga Sūtra; Umāsvāti, Tattvārthasūtra; Hemacandra, Yogaśāstra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ThreefoldAscentOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Naturschau setzt asketische Praxis voraus",
        "en": "Contemplation of nature presupposes ascetic practice"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Physike"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Praktike"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The deliberate use of dreams as a source of knowledge, treated in this literature as available to anyone and requiring no instrument beyond the sleeper."
      },
      "id": "pmp:DreamDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumdivination",
        "en": "Dream divination"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The absence of an instrument and of an office is the point at which this tradition differs from the incubation cult: no temple, no priest, no fee."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1981/1984",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a being becomes harvestable on one of two opposed paths at unequal thresholds — a slight majority of service to others on the one, an approach to totality of service to self on the other, with a region of indifference between them — and that the negative path is nevertheless abandoned at the density of unity, because it rests on an omission and what is not cannot endure."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PolarityHarvestThresholds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die zwei Schwellen der Polarität und der Zusammenbruch der negativen",
        "en": "The two thresholds of polarity and the collapse of the negative one"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an origination rather than as the teaching of a tradition. The material was produced between 1981 and 1984 by three named people in a stated procedure, and it presents itself as received rather than composed. That presentation is what pm:circulatesAs records; the node makes no claim about the source of the material, only about where the text begins.",
          "One caution for anyone opening the passages. The published wording of the omission argument names the wrong polarity at its first occurrence, and the correction is made in the exchange itself; a reader who quotes the sentence as printed quotes a slip. And the passage about abandoning the negative polarity in the early sixth density stands at 36.12, not at 78.25 as an earlier note in prima-materia#62 had it. 78.25 carries the different statement that the two paths must take in each other.",
          "The two halves of the claim do not sit easily together, and the record keeps them apart rather than choosing. Read from the thresholds alone the system is a dualism with two valid outcomes; read from the argument about the omission it is a monism in which one outcome is provisional. Whoever cites only the thresholds reports a symmetry the same text withdraws. See prima-materia#69."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A cosmology of great antiquity, received from a source outside the authors, rather than a text composed between 1981 and 1984.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Carla Rueckert as instrument, with Don Elkins as questioner and Jim McCarty as scribe (L/L Research)",
      "source": [
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 36.12 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 78.25 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 85.11 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), sessions 17.32 and 17.33 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of first putting forward a claim that no transmission carries, in a work that can be named and dated, where the claim afterwards circulates as though it were old."
      },
      "id": "pm:Originating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Urheben",
        "en": "Originating"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which arranges material that was already there and names what it arranges. An origination arranges nothing: the claim begins with it. Holding the two apart is what keeps the record from reporting an invention as an ordering of something ancient, which is the commonest way a modern construction acquires a warrant.",
          "The circulating ascription is the point of the class, not an addendum to it. A node that names only the author and the work is a bibliography; a node that also names what the claim is taken for is the finding, and it is the kind of finding this project exists to make."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How long he lies is stated to depend on how near or far the place is of which he makes enquiry — an hour or two for a near one — and to have an upper bound: the time never exceeds twenty-four hours, let the place be at never so great a distance."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DurationTracksDistance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Dauer des Liegens folgt der Entfernung und endet bei einem Tag",
        "en": "The time he lies still follows the distance, and stops at a day"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A quantity that varies with a measurable quantity, and a ceiling that does not. Both halves are unusual: the first makes the claim comparable across cases, and the second makes it refutable by a single case that runs longer. Nothing else in this file states a number."
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim this protocol attempts to fail."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:tests",
      "label": {
        "de": "prüft",
        "en": "tests"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The sacred shell of the society, said in the origin account to have been shot into the body of the first recipient so that he might have immortality and be able to confer the secrets on his kinsmen."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Migis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mī'gis",
        "en": "Mī'gis"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The shell is not a token of membership but the vehicle of the transfer: the account makes the entry of the shell into the body the act by which the capacity passes, and the same act is repeated at every degree."
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 167",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reported attitude that these are great and difficult questions best left alone, that men know so little beyond food and sleep and rest that it might easily seem presumptuous to form any opinion about hidden things, and that people who are content should not trouble themselves with thinking."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstOpinionAboutHiddenThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Warnung davor, sich über die verborgenen Dinge eine Meinung zu bilden",
        "en": "The caution against forming opinions about hidden things"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A tradition warning against the very enquiry the rest of it makes possible. It is entered because a record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports what it holds, and because this caution is directed at the enquirer and not at the practitioner: it does not say the hidden things are unreal, it says that opinion about them is presumption.",
          "The passage also gives a second and worldlier ground for the same reticence, which the ethnographer reports of himself and not of the tradition: that his hosts feared their séances and their taboo observances would be laughed at. Both grounds are in the same pages, and separating them is the reader's work."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitHiddenThings"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-20",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A dream held to come from a god rather than from the sleeper, and distinguished from the ordinary dream not by its content but by the state in which it arrives."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Theopemptos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gottgesandte Traum",
        "en": "The god-sent dream"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The state is described as the one in which sleep is departing and waking beginning, or as a condition between the two, accompanied by a voice, by a light, and by an inability to move the eyes. This is the closest description in the collection to what modern accounts call the hypnopompic state, and the immobility is named as part of the sign rather than as an obstacle. What arrives in it is a communication received, not a faculty exercised."
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a person is not born with the bodies beyond the physical, that these can only be cultivated artificially and only where favourable conditions, inner and outer, are present, and that immortality therefore is not a property one is born with but something acquired."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FinerBodiesNotInnate",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die feineren Körper sind nicht angeboren, sondern gezüchtet",
        "en": "The finer bodies are not innate but cultivated"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also fixes what the paths in pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal are for. If the goal were innate, a way would be a matter of recognising it; here it is a matter of building something, which is why the classification falls entirely on how much of a person is worked on. See prima-materia#65.",
          "The claim is stated against the traditions it borrows its terms from: most teachings that repeat the divisions are said to have forgotten or omitted precisely this feature. That makes it a correction offered to a corpus rather than a report of it, and it is the load-bearing move of the whole system — everything about ways, effort and time follows from a possession that must be made and can fail to be made."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "The most important feature of an ancient teaching, lost by the systems that preserved its divisions, rather than a claim first published in this form in 1949.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the chapter on the four bodies of man",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The date on which the count was last carried forward. A protocol without this date claims a scrutiny it does not document."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:protocolUpdated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Protokollstand",
        "en": "protocol updated"
      },
      "range": "xsd:date",
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The teaching that the figures met in the intermediate state are appearances of the one who meets them and not independently existing beings, and that recognising them as such is what frees."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AppearancesAreSelfArising",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Erscheinungen sind Selbst-Erscheinung",
        "en": "The appearances are self-arising"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Tibetan rang snang, self-appearance. A dating warning belongs on this node: the work is traditionally ascribed to Padmasambhava in the eighth century but is a treasure text of the fourteenth, revealed by Karma Lingpa; the older ascription is not held in the scholarship. Note also the scope. The doctrine holds of all appearance in this tradition, waking included, and is therefore not a statement about the threshold state in particular."
      },
      "source": "Bar do thos grol (Karma Lingpa recension, 14th century; trans. Gyurme Dorje 2006), the intermediate state of reality",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement between the two recorders about the extent and the naming of the Zande set of oracles. The monograph gives the generic term as soroka and names four principal oracles: the poison, the rubbing-board, the termites and a three-stick oracle called mapingo. The earlier Belgian ethnography gives the generic term as lora, lists eight procedures, and assigns the pile-of-sticks oracle to the Abarambo, Mangbetu and Bakango and to Azande of those origins rather than to the transmission at large. It also describes the termites procedure differently, as a plaited grass braid laid in a small pit and read from whether the termites cut it through or gnaw only its underside."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZandeOracleSetDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Welche Orakel die Überlieferung als die ihren zählt",
        "en": "Which oracles the transmission counts as its own"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The disagreement matters beyond terminology. Which procedures belong to the transmission and which were taken over from neighbours is exactly the question a contact route turns on, and the two recorders answer it differently for the same set. Neither was read against the other in a modern study in this run.",
          "The two accounts also differ on who receives the poison. The Belgian ethnography, written sixteen years earlier and on the other side of the border, gives the ordeal as administered to a suspected person or to a fowl, in the present tense and without qualification. The monograph gives administration to human beings as a thing of the past and very rare even then. Whether that is a change over sixteen years, a difference between the two administrations, or a difference of observation is not decidable from the two passages."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:OracleRankOrder"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande: introduction à une ethnographie générale des bassins de l'Ubangi-Uele et de l'Aruwimi (Bruxelles: Maurice Lamertin, 1921), chapter V, Procédés divinatoires (mantique), pp. 183-184",
        "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, p. 228, and p. 164"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Iranian transmission of a dualist cosmology, of xvarənah as a conferred radiance, and of a soul journey undertaken and reported."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Zoroastrian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zoroastrische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Zoroastrian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Ardā Wīrāz Nāmag is a substance-induced journey with a named traveller, a preparation and a report, and therefore closer to a protocol than most of what the graph holds."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Avesta (Gāthās, Yasna, Vīdēvdād); Bundahišn; Dēnkard; Ardā Wīrāz Nāmag",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to certain persons of coming to life again after death and returning to their place among people, held to belong to those over whom particular words were spoken at their birth."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wiederaufleben nach dem Tod",
        "en": "Coming to life again after death"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The narrative gives the being a name in a phonetic transcription that the digitised text renders unreliably; the spelling is therefore not carried into this node, and the term is to be taken from a clean copy of the page before it is asserted anywhere. The claim itself does not depend on the spelling."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A knowledge tradition understood as an ongoing act of transmission rather than a static corpus."
      },
      "id": "pm:Tradition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tradition",
        "en": "Tradition"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol the tradition states for itself. During the hair-cutting of a child under tapu nobody attending may eat; the witness says that a breach is discovered at once, because the skin of the child's head is cut while the hair is being cut, and calls this a sure sign."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TapuBreachSign",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die verletzte Kopfhaut als Anzeichen des gebrochenen Fastens",
        "en": "The cut scalp as the sign that the fast was broken"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because the tradition names an outward, observable event as the detector of a hidden breach and calls it certain. That is a falsifying condition offered by the material itself, which is rare enough in this collection to be worth holding as a protocol rather than as a remark."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded case in which the fast was kept and the scalp was cut all the same, or in which the fast was broken and the scalp was not cut. The tradition claims the sign is sure, so a single clean counter-case on either side falls due against it.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-08-30",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Tapu"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 42",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Graeco-Egyptian transmission of an ascent through the spheres in which the ascending one sheds a vice at each, and of a rebirth reported as having happened to the speaker."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hermetic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hermetische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Hermetic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Discourse on the Eighth and the Ninth is an initiation with a sequence, a moment of change and a demand that the initiate confirm what he now sees. It has no equivalent in what the graph currently holds."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Corpus Hermeticum; Asclepius; the Stobaean excerpts including Korē Kosmou; Nag Hammadi VI,6",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An epistemic mode in which a statement is asserted. Individual instances are defined in ontology/consciousness.ttl."
      },
      "id": "pm:AwarenessContext",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bewusstseinskontext",
        "en": "Awareness Context"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The statement that carries a claim of independent attestation: when the testimony relied on was fixed or gathered, and against which publication or route that date stands."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:independenceGround",
      "label": {
        "de": "Grund der Unabhängigkeit",
        "en": "ground of independence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Required wherever pm:independentAttestation is set, enforced by pm:IndependenceGroundShape. The burden is reversed on purpose. Feedback from a published account back into the field it describes can almost never be demonstrated, so a predicate that marked only the demonstrated cases would let the ordinary case pass in silence — the failure pm:CoverageState and pm:ExaminationState exist to prevent. A claim of independence must instead carry the fact that makes it checkable.",
          "The date alone does not settle it, and the sentence must say more where more is at stake. Feedback within a single enquiry — few informants, several years, the same team returning, material gathered by an informant between visits — precedes any publication and no date catches it. Where the corpus was fixed that way, the ground states the gathering situation as well. Whether it does so honestly is settled at the merge and not by a shape; naming that limit is better than a guard that pretends to check it."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the earth."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Araqiel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Araqiel",
        "en": "Araqiel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EzeqeelTeachesKnowledgeOfClouds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ezeqeel offenbart die Wolkenkunde",
        "en": "Ezeqeel discloses the knowledge of the clouds"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfClouds"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Ezeqeel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement among editors about what belongs to the manual and how its verses are numbered. The widely circulated editions descend from single manuscripts and differ in the count and extent of their chapters; a critical edition drawn from more than two hundred manuscripts, with the oldest dated 1496, settles neither question in favour of the numbering that most citations use."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Textbestand und Verszählung des Handbuchs hängen von der Ausgabe ab",
        "en": "The text and the verse numbering of the manual are edition-dependent"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node because every citation in this file carries a verse number, and a number is only as stable as the recension it comes from. The numbering used throughout this file follows the widely circulated vulgate text; a reader working from the critical edition may find the same passage under a different number. Naming the manual without naming the edition conceals that anything is at issue."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmt:Hathayoga"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, Kaivalyadhama S.M.Y.M. Samiti, Lonavla",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, ed. J. Mallinson, J. Birch, J. Hanneder, M. Demoto and N.-J. Liersch (2024)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā, ed. and tr. P. Sinh (1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of a body of practice concerning attention, dreaming and the assemblage point, presented as the teaching of a Yaqui man of knowledge."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CastanedaToltec",
      "label": {
        "de": "Castanedas toltekische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Castaneda's Toltec transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The fieldwork is not credible and the informant is not attestable, and the practices are nonetheless transmitted today by people who follow them. Both facts belong in the graph, which is why the tradition is registered rather than dismissed."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan; Castaneda, The Art of Dreaming",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A luminosity held to arise at the passage into sleep, which the practitioner is trained to recognise and hold rather than to pass through unconscious."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ClearLightOfSleep",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das klare Licht des Schlafs",
        "en": "The clear light of sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Tibetan 'od gsal, Sanskrit prabhāsvara. The state is not that of a body whose motor system has shut down while the mind stays awake; it is a luminosity placed at the moment of falling asleep, and its purpose in the sources is the recognition of emptiness and the preparation for dying, not the acquisition of a faculty."
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of one hundred and six sessions received between 1981 and 1984 by a group of three working in fixed roles, teaching a cosmology of densities, a choice between service to others and service to self, and a harvest at the close of a cycle."
      },
      "id": "pmt:RaMaterial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Ra-Material",
        "en": "The Ra material"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The best documented reception in the block. Questioner, instrument and scribe are named, the sessions are numbered and dated, the working conditions are described including the instrument's physical decline, and the transmission continued under a different contact after this one ended. The cosmological claims are largely undecidable; the claims about the conditions of contact and about what the work cost the instrument are not, and that is where an examination would begin."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Elkins, Rueckert and McCarty, The Law of One, Books I to V; the L/L Research session transcripts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The late antique Platonic transmission concerned with the ascent of the soul and with the criteria by which a divine communication is told from a human one."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neuplatonische Theurgie",
        "en": "Neoplatonic theurgy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The De mysteriis citations name the edition they were read in. For Synesius, De insomniis neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened.",
          "The file records that this layer underlies the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory, so agreement among those three may not be counted as independent."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Three transmissions make the same claim about spoken ritual language: the effect is not obtained unless the words are rendered as they stand. Two of them also say what a lapse does. One holds that an error, and equally a hesitation, is fatal to the efficacy of the address. Another holds that an error in a song may be fatal to the ceremony and that in some cases a single syllable does irreparable harm. The third requires the publicly chanted parts to be letter perfect and, in the passages read, states no consequence."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Genauigkeit der Rezitation ist als Bedingung ihrer Wirksamkeit gesetzt",
        "en": "The exactness of the recitation is set as the condition of its efficacy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A defect in the vocabulary shows at this node and is recorded rather than worked around. pm:contactRoute is a property of a single tradition, but contact is a relation between two. The value pm:contactRouteAbsent as the registry uses it means that no route runs to what the graph already held at the time of registration; it cannot answer whether a route runs between two registered traditions that are later brought together in one correspondence, which is exactly the question a convergence asks. See prima-materia#101 for the vocabulary defect and prima-materia#102 for the registry entries it affects.",
          "The counter-search did not produce a tradition that denies the claim. What it produced is of a different kind and belongs here: the recorded versions of the same Māori address differ from one another across the eighteen-forties to the early nineteen-hundreds, and which rendering was valid was disputed among Māori at the time. A doctrine of one correct wording and a transmission that visibly carries several are not a contradiction, but they are the point at which the claim becomes examinable instead of edifying. The place that says so has not been read at the wording and stands at prima-materia#110.",
          "Three witnesses agreeing on the form of a rule is a weaker finding than it looks unless the alternative is stated. The alternative exists and is common: traditions in which the sense of a prayer carries it and the wording may be varied, and traditions in which improvisation is the mark of the competent practitioner. That the three named here all fall on the fixed side is what the correspondence asserts. Where the rule holds and where it is absent has not been surveyed; the spheres left unsearched stand at prima-materia#116, the Indic parallel at prima-materia#109.",
          "What the node claims and what it does not. Independent attestation is asserted for the two North American transmissions with respect to the Polynesian one and with respect to everything else this collection holds; no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the periods in which any of these corpora were fixed. It is not asserted between those two: they are neighbours in one region, and a route between Pueblo and Athabaskan ceremonialism is neither absent nor established here. The Māori witness is therefore the third leg and not a third independent one, because its own contact route is entered as disputed."
        ]
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:MaoriExactRecitation"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:NavajoSongExactness"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "Not a claim of priority in time. The two American corpora were fixed in eighteen ninety-seven and, from fieldwork of the nineteen-twenties, in nineteen thirty-two, both after the Polynesian witness was printed in eighteen eighty-two. What carries the independence is that no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the period in which any of the three corpora were fixed, so agreement between them cannot be one statement counted twice. Two things this ground does not settle and does not hide: both American corpora were gathered by ethnographers working in a discipline to which the rule of exact recitation was already familiar, and neither passage says whether the rule was volunteered in the field or asked for. The second is the gathering question and it stands open.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": [
        {
          "id": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
        "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 615-616",
        "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), sections 41-42, pp. 24-25"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act by which a claim entered the record. Instances of this class are modes of entry, not claims; a claim points at one of them through pm:attestedBy."
      },
      "id": "pm:Attesting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bezeugen",
        "en": "Attesting"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Orthogonal to pm:AwarenessContext, which describes the state the asserter was in rather than the route by which the assertion reached the record."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity that an attribution ascribes."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Attributing",
      "id": "pm:ascribedCapacity",
      "label": {
        "de": "zugeschriebenes Vermögen",
        "en": "ascribed capacity"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The training of awareness within the dream, set out in the treatise as a course of exercises in apprehending, multiplying and transforming what appears."
      },
      "id": "pmp:DreamYoga",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumyoga",
        "en": "Dream yoga"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Tibetan rmi lam, transliterated milam. Older witnesses stand behind the fifteenth-century treatise: the dream-yoga manuals in the collected works of Gampopa (1079-1153) and the root verses ascribed to Tilopa. That ascription is traditional and only partly secured philologically. Note also that the list of the six is unstable between witnesses, and that the name six yogas is a western coinage for what the tradition calls six dharmas."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the dream state",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Maya transmission of creation, of the calendar as a moving structure of obligation, and of divination by counting seeds."
      },
      "id": "pmt:MayaRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Maya-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Maya ritual transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Popol Vuh in the Ximénez manuscript; the Books of Chilam Balam; the Dresden Codex; the Ritual of the Bacabs",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The canonical Chinese account of qi, the channels and the spirits resident in the organs, framed as a clinical rather than a soteriological doctrine."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chinesischer medizinischer Kanon",
        "en": "Chinese medical canon"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Huangdi neijing (Suwen and Lingshu); Nanjing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, and holds only instruction to be reliable; the display of power for the sake of persuasion is refused as something to be ashamed of."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zurschaustellung der Kräfte verweigert",
        "en": "Display of powers refused"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Vinaya rule against displaying superhuman states to laypeople stands alongside it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Cetopariyanana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbacakkhu"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbasota"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Manomayakaya"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Dīgha Nikāya 11 (Kevaṭṭa Sutta)",
        "Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Cases have been recorded and show a deviation from the stated base rate. What deviates is what the protocol measured, which is rarely the whole of what the claim asserts."
      },
      "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fälle mit Abweichung",
        "en": "Cases with deviation"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rule by which an oracular verdict stands or falls: across the two tests one fowl must die and the other must live. If both die or both live the verdict is contradictory and therefore void, and the matter must be put to the oracle again on another occasion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Huhn muss sterben und das andere leben",
        "en": "One fowl must die and the other live"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is a falsifying condition stated by the transmission itself, applied by its own practitioners, and printed with four worked cases. It is the reason this tradition was opened before others in the registry. What the transmission does with the condition when it is met is a separate matter and stands on its own node below."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139, the four cases lettered A to D",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Those who burned llama fat and coca in the fire and saw in the shapes and the marks made at the time of the burning what was to come, and told it to whoever consulted them."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FireReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Lesen am Verbrennen von Fett und Coca",
        "en": "Reading the burning of fat and coca"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement among editors about the age of the inscription on circulating the qi and about how its opening is to be construed. One dating places it in the late fourth or early third century before the common era, another in the middle of the sixth; one reading takes the opening characters as a title and begins the text at the second line, another counts and construes differently."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiInscriptionDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Datierung und Lesung der Jadeinschrift",
        "en": "The dating and reading of the jade inscription"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node rather than as a remark, because the dating decides whether this is the earliest datable witness to deliberate circulation of the qi or one witness among several, and because a citation that names only the inscription conceals that anything is at issue."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmp:Xingqi"
      },
      "source": [
        "D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Stages of the path attained by effort and held by the one who reaches them, enumerated in the handbooks in a set order."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Maqamat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stationen",
        "en": "Stations"
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the poison oracle is chiefly consulted for: to learn whether witchcraft threatens an undertaking or a person's health, and if so which neighbour is at this moment exercising it, so that he may be approached and asked to withdraw it. The question is never whether a man is a witch but whether he is now bewitching the questioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Benennung dessen, der gerade behext",
        "en": "Naming the one who is bewitching at this moment"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4 and pp. 35-36",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The bearer to whom the capacity is ascribed: a named figure, a class of beings, humanity before a stated event."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Attributing",
      "id": "pm:ascribedTo",
      "label": {
        "de": "zugeschrieben an",
        "en": "ascribed to"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The office named first in the earlier report: those who see the fortune and outcome of what is asked of them. For this they killed birds, lambs and llamas, and blowing through a certain vein into the lungs found in them signs by which they said what was to happen."
      },
      "id": "pmp:LungReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Lesen an der Lunge des getöteten Tieres",
        "en": "Reading the lungs of the killed animal"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The one procedure in this file that reads a variable natural object rather than a count. It is the Andean counterpart of the guinea-pig inspection that the later account gives under two other names, and the two together make entrail reading the best-witnessed technique of the transmission."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the calparigu",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Book of Formation and its commentaries, in which the world is formed through letters, number and breath."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SeferYetzirah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sefer Jetzira",
        "en": "Sefer Yetzirah"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the breath is drawn in through the mouth with the tongue between the lips, producing a hissing sound, and is not let out the same way."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Sitkari",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sītkārī",
        "en": "Sītkārī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.54-56 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Cuban transmission of Kongo derivation, working through a charged vessel and a pact with a named spirit of the dead."
      },
      "id": "pmt:PaloMonte",
      "label": {
        "de": "Palo Monte",
        "en": "Palo Monte"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Cabrera, Reglas de Congo",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A heat said to arise when the raised wind reaches the region of fire, lengthening its flame, until the sleeping power is warmed through and straightens."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KindledInnerFire",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das entfachte innere Feuer",
        "en": "The kindled inner fire"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the stars; the two recensions differ on whether it is the constellations or the signs of the stars."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kokabel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kokabel",
        "en": "Kokabel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An ongoing attempt to fail a claim under a stated condition. A protocol that names no condition under which the claim would be refuted is not a test of it."
      },
      "id": "pm:Testing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfen",
        "en": "Testing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "An instance exists only where somebody has taken the examination up, and its absence is therefore informative rather than empty: it says that no procedure has been taken up, not that the claim has been examined and found wanting. A stub for every claim in the collection would double the graph and say nothing the absence does not already say."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorders state once and for all that what they print represents one stage of the revelations granted to initiates of high standing who were not themselves responsible for the calculations concerning this part of the sky, and that the stage is deliberately distorted in both directions."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightKnowledgeCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das veröffentlichte System ist eine Enthüllungsstufe, nicht das System",
        "en": "The published system is a stage of revelation, not the system"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A caution that cuts against the account carrying it, which is why it is held as a node. It makes the text unfalsifiable in the direction its later readers wanted: any discrepancy can be assigned to the withheld stage. It also means the article cannot be cited as the Dogon system without citing this sentence, and the reception has cited it without."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:LightKnowledge"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of terrestrial import from the position of the heavenly bodies."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Astrology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Astrologie",
        "en": "Astrology"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of bracelets and ornaments, of antimony and the painting of the eyelids, of precious stones and colouring tinctures."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schmuck und Kosmetik",
        "en": "Adornment and cosmetics"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DiviningPresupposesPupilage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Befragen setzt die Lehrzeit voraus",
        "en": "Divining presupposes the pupilage"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage rather than of the tradition. The source sets the pupilage, its minimum length and the division of its course, and says nothing about what befalls a man who divines without it. A stated consequence would raise this to a necessity; none stands in what was read."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:IfaCasting"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BabalawoPupilage"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConsultationPresupposesTaboos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung setzt die Tabus voraus",
        "en": "The consultation presupposes the taboos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: the source does not merely prescribe the abstinence but says what its want does to the instrument. An unclean man may still address the oracle from a distance, which is a graded rather than an absolute exclusion and is recorded here rather than smoothed away."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Contact of an unclean person with the oracle is certain to destroy its potency, and even the close proximity of an unclean person may have that result."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeTaboos"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 132",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A modern work bearing on the claim under examination, given as a bibliographic reference or a work identifier."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:evidenceFrom",
      "label": {
        "de": "Evidenz aus",
        "en": "evidence from"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A modern study is never a witness to a tradition and must never appear as the source of one of its claims; it bears on a claim only as evidence inside an examination. Keeping the two predicates apart is what allows research to enter the record at all without loosening the rule that sources are received works."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantTouch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernberühren hervor",
        "en": "The second degree yields touching at a distance"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideDistantTouch"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of Gurdjieff's teaching: a claim that ordinary waking is a form of sleep, and a work on attention conducted in the middle of ordinary life."
      },
      "id": "pmt:FourthWay",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Vierte Weg",
        "en": "The Fourth Way"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The graph already holds the Fourth Way anthropology as an origination, because material from it circulates under ancient ascription. Registering the transmission does not touch that finding: the tradition transmits, and the pedigree remains unsupported."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous; Gurdjieff, Beelzebub's Tales to His Grandson",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The final object of the ascent, seen only by one who has climbed by way of the intervening rungs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BeautyItself",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schönheit selbst",
        "en": "Beauty itself"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 211c-212a",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Platonic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The purgative part of the exercises, given over to the consideration of sin."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FirstWeek",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die erste Woche",
        "en": "The first week"
      },
      "source": "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state ascribed to the cooling retention: fever, bile, hunger, thirst and the effect of poisons are said to be put down."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CoolingAndQuenching",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kühlung und das Stillen von Hunger und Durst",
        "en": "Cooling and the quenching of hunger and thirst"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The single rule by which the drum is read. If the ring travels round towards the right hand, with the course of the sun, health, fortune and increase of men and beasts are promised. If it goes the contrary way, towards the left, sickness and all the evils of ill success are expected. The later recorder gives the same rule in weaker terms on the negative side: with the sun it augured a favourable event, against the sun a doubt then arose of a prosperous one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SunCourseRule",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Regel des Sonnenlaufs",
        "en": "The rule of the sun's course"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One further reading is given by the later recorder alone, from a named practitioner: that if the copper bird laid on the skin falls from the drum to the earth, it is a sign that the person enquired of will soon die. That is a third outcome beside the two directions, and it is entered here because a binary instrument with a third outcome is not binary.",
          "The difference between them is not nothing. The earlier makes the contrary turn a prediction of evil; the later makes it only a doubt. Whether that is a change in the practice, a difference between regions, or the difference between a compiler stating a rule and a missionary reporting what a man told him is not decidable from the two passages.",
          "Two recorders, ninety years apart, one compiling from priests' reports in Uppsala and one living among the people, give the same reading rule tied to the same physical direction. It is the best-attested single claim in this file."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 474 and 477"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A limit named as the end of twelve, at which the mind or the risen power comes to rest. The verses give the number and leave the unit unstated."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Dvadasanta",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Ende der Zwölf",
        "en": "The end of the twelve"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The count itself fluctuates. What is verse 50 in the numbering used here appears as 51 in other editions, and the following verse shifts with it. A reader working from a different edition should expect the two verses cited here to sit one number further on.",
          "Two things this node deliberately does not say. It does not fix the unit: the base text says end-of-twelve, and the reading that makes this twelve finger-breadths from the tip of the nose belongs to the commentarial layer, not to the verse. And it does not fix the place: the exegesis of Lakṣmaṇjoo counts several such limits in the body, at the crown, the brows, the throat, the base of the heart and the navel, and the verse that follows says outright that it may be reached in any manner and in any place. Nothing here yields a fixed radius. See prima-materia#38."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 50 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 51 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:JhanaPresupposesAbandoningTheHindrances",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vertiefung setzt das Aufgeben der Hemmnisse voraus",
        "en": "Absorption presupposes the abandoning of the hindrances"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The discourse places the abandoning of the five immediately before entry into the first absorption in a fixed formula, without stating at that place what befalls one who enters otherwise."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FiveHindrances"
      },
      "source": "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A conclusion drawn by an editor or compiler that the source does not state."
      },
      "id": "pm:compilerInference",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schluss des Bearbeiters",
        "en": "Compiler inference"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Admissible as attestation of an ordering act, never of the claim being ordered. Enforced by pm:AttributionShape."
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Three positions are reported side by side. Some hold that those who cast themselves down are not really but only in appearance dead. Others hold that the soul departs from the body and, after its travel abroad, returns again. The compiler holds both to be false, on the ground that it is impossible for either man or devil to restore a soul to the body it has once left, and concludes that the devil only stifles the faculties of the soul for a time and hinders their operations."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhatLiesStillWhileHeTravels",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was da stillliegt",
        "en": "What it is that lies still"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No dispute node is written, and the reason is a rule of this repository rather than a judgement about the material. A dispute cites each side from its own work; here all three positions stand in one compilation, reported by one man, and two of them without an owner. What the passage establishes is that the question was open among those the compiler drew on, not who held what.",
          "The compiler's own position is reached from a doctrine and not from the material: he rejects the departure of the soul because it would be theologically impossible, not because anything he was told tells against it. That is worth recording, because the same move settles the question for him everywhere else in the chapter."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 56",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The node carrying what this station added and the exemplar does not have. Required: an addition that cannot be pointed at is an assertion about a chain and not a link in it."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Reworking",
      "id": "pm:addedElement",
      "label": {
        "de": "hinzugefügtes Element",
        "en": "added element"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "First-person inclusive awareness bounded to an identified group."
      },
      "id": "pm:collectiveAwareness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kollektives Bewusstsein",
        "en": "Collective awareness"
      },
      "type": "pm:AwarenessSpace"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative means take what the surrounding order prohibits and use the force of the prohibition itself as the vehicle. The transgression is prescribed, not incidental, and it is bounded by the rite that prescribes it."
      },
      "id": "pm:transgressiveMethod",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übertretende Methode",
        "en": "Transgressive method"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Not the same as licence. A path is transgressive on this axis only where a source sets the breach as a means; a practice that merely fails to observe a norm is not oriented by it at all."
      },
      "type": "pm:MethodOrientation"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsSoulSeizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der vierte Grad bringt das Ergreifen des Schattens hervor",
        "en": "The fourth degree yields the seizing of the shadow"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideSoulSeizing"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The late twentieth century transmission treating belief as a tool to be adopted and discarded, and holding that the symbolic system employed is arbitrary if the technique is sound."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChaosMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chaosmagie",
        "en": "Chaos magic"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim that content is interchangeable and only technique carries effect is directly examinable, and if it held it would reorganise every convergence question the project asks."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Spare, The Book of Pleasure; Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of weather and import from cloud formations."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfClouds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wolkenkunde",
        "en": "Knowledge of the clouds"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Chinese transmission of calming and contemplation, with a systematic account of the obstacles, illnesses and demonic states that arise in practice."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Tiantai",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tiantai",
        "en": "Tiantai"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhiyi, Mohe zhiguan; Zhiyi, Xiao zhiguan",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Śaiva and Śākta tantric corpus and its Kashmirian exegesis."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SaivaSakta",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śaiva- und Śākta-Tantra",
        "en": "Śaiva and Śākta tantra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A caution governs everything read out of this tradition. What is old is a set of subtle centres carrying seed syllables, and the older tantras vary in how many they count. The fixed system of seven centres with spectral colours and assigned psychological qualities belongs to the twentieth century and to no tantra; it is held in this file as pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping so that it is available as what it is, and it must not be read back into the material it was built on.",
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Vijñānabhairava citations name the numbering they were read in. For the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa, the Tantrāloka, the Tantrasāra and the Mālinīvijayottaratantra the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those passages are to be reopened, and the chapter divisions of the Kashmirian exegesis are edition-dependent."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "No route is available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. Agreement with traditions already held is a candidate for independent attestation."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kein Kontaktweg",
        "en": "Contact route absent"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A warning that the breath is to be mastered by degrees as a lion, an elephant or a tiger is mastered, and that it destroys the one who proceeds otherwise; performed wrongly, the practice is said to generate the very ailments it removes when performed rightly."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ForcedBreathCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein erzwungener statt allmählich gezähmter Atem tötet den Übenden",
        "en": "Breath forced rather than tamed by degrees kills the practitioner"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The named consequences are hiccough, laboured breathing, cough, and pain in head, ears and eyes. This is the strongest thing the manual says about the manner of breathing, and it concerns pace and force, not the opening the breath passes through. See prima-materia#26."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.15 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.16-17 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A power held to rest coiled at the base of the subtle body and to rise through it when roused."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kundalini",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kuṇḍalinī",
        "en": "Kuṇḍalinī"
      },
      "source": "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Freedom from the disturbance of the passions, called the flower of ascetic labour."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Apatheia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Apatheia",
        "en": "Apatheia"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A graded series in which each stage is reached by letting go of a factor still present in the one before it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vier Vertiefungen",
        "en": "The four absorptions"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Kongo transmission of composed objects charged to act, and of the land of the dead as a place with a stated geography."
      },
      "id": "pmt:KongoNkisi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kongolesische Nkisi-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Kongo nkisi transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Laman notebooks were written in Kikongo by Kongo authors describing their own practice, which makes them one of the few sources in this file that is not an outsider's account."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Laman notebooks in Kikongo; Laman, The Kongo",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree to call forth the shadow, that is the soul, and to retain it within his grasp at pleasure."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideSoulSeizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Herausrufen des Schattens und sein Festhalten",
        "en": "Calling forth the shadow and holding it"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Valentinian school of the second century, known in this file through the account of an opponent and not yet through its own writings."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ValentinianGnosis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Valentinianische Gnosis",
        "en": "Valentinian gnosis"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A caution governs everything read out of this file at present. What is held here comes from a refutation: the heresiologist reports the doctrine in order to defeat it, selects what serves that end, and is under no obligation to state it as its holders would. Every node therefore carries pm:thirdPartyAscription and not textual attestation, and none of them may be read as a Valentinian self-statement until the same claim is found in a text of the school itself.",
          "The three decisions that precede a tradition file, stated rather than left implicit. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Indian or Chinese material, so a correspondence with either would count as a finding rather than as reception — but no such correspondence is asserted here yet. Edition: the account is read in the Keble translation of 1872, named on each node; the Nag Hammadi witnesses are not yet read and their translation is not yet chosen. Number of citable passages: one. That is thin, and deliberately so, on the model of traditions/daoist.ttl — a file grows in passages, and a heading without passages under it would be a claim about the corpus that the corpus does not carry."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DreamYogaPresupposesClearLight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumyoga setzt das Halten des klaren Lichts voraus",
        "en": "Dream yoga presupposes holding the clear light of sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The treatise takes the recognition of the clear light before the training in the dream. It does not state at that place what befalls one who begins elsewhere, which is why the strength is order and not necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:DreamYoga"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ClearLightOfSleep"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earliest alchemical transmission, in which the operation on matter is reported together with dreams that interpret it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Griechisch-ägyptische Alchemie",
        "en": "Graeco-Egyptian alchemy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zosimos of Panopolis, the Visions and the authentic memoirs",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The mid twentieth century transmission of teachings ascribed to visitors from other worlds, reported first as bodily meetings and later as messages received without one."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ContacteeTransmission",
      "label": {
        "de": "Contactee-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Contactee transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The shift from a claimed physical encounter to a claimed interior reception happens inside this literature and is datable. It is the hinge between the older revealed corpora and the later channelled ones."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Adamski, Inside the Space Ships; Menger, From Outer Space to You; van Tassel, I Rode a Flying Saucer",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The uttering of efficacious words over a person at birth, said to determine that the person will be able to come to life again after dying."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte",
        "en": "The words spoken at a birth"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of kami, of purification as the central operation, and of formal address preserved in the imperial liturgies."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shinto",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shintō",
        "en": "Shintō"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kojiki; Nihon shoki; the norito of the Engishiki",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The requirement that the songs be known in a most exact manner, down to the syllable, no important mistake being tolerated."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NavajoSongExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Genauigkeit der gesungenen Worte",
        "en": "Exactness of the sung words"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Islamic mystical path, systematised in the handbooks of the tenth and eleventh centuries."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Sufism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sufismus",
        "en": "Sufism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both works the edition or translation the passages were read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. Neither citation carries a page or section number, and the printed editions of the Kitāb al-Lumaʿ and the Risāla divide their material differently, so the passages are to be reopened before anything in this file is cited by place."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second of the three trainings, named in the handbook as the condition for the six direct knowledges."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Samadhi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sammlung",
        "en": "Concentration"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that four terminologies name the same four bodies: carnal, natural, spiritual and divine; carriage, horse, driver and master, glossed as body, feelings and desires, mind, and the I with consciousness and will; physical, astral, mental and causal; with the assertion that comparisons of this kind are found in most systems that grant a person more than a physical body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourBodiesAcrossTerminologies",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine Vierteilung unter vier Namensreihen",
        "en": "One fourfold division under four sets of names"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held here because of the shape of the claim, not its content. An identity is asserted between a Pauline triad extended to four, an unnamed eastern set of images, and a nineteenth-century theosophical scheme. No route of contact is given for any of the three, and none is asserted by this node. The correspondence is recorded as claimed and not as established: this is exactly the case pm:Converging exists to keep separate from, and it does not meet that bar, because a shared table of names is not a shared claim.",
          "The theosophical column is the one that dates the table. Physical, astral, mental and causal in that sequence is a set fixed in the late nineteenth century; a table containing it cannot be older than that, whatever the antiquity of the images beside it. Anyone reading the four columns as four independent witnesses to one ancient doctrine has taken a nineteenth-century arrangement for a convergence."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A correspondence found in most teachings, presented as recovered rather than as drawn up.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), at the table of the four bodies and its four terminologies",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:StationsPresupposeTheOneBelow",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine Station setzt die darunterliegende voraus",
        "en": "A station presupposes the station below it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbooks set turning back as the first station and hold that the one below must be kept before the one above is reached. The enumeration and the count of the stations differ between the handbooks; the precedence of turning back does not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Maqamat"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Tawba"
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:LimbPracticeYieldsLightOfKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Übung der Glieder bringt das Leuchten der Erkenntnis hervor",
        "en": "Practice of the limbs yields the light of knowledge"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sūtra makes the arising conditional on the wearing away of impurity, which is itself said to come from the practice. The condition is part of the claim and is not to be dropped when the claim is quoted."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:LightOfKnowledge"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Indian medical transmission, including the rasāyana section that claims restoration of vigour and extension of life by regimen."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Ayurvedic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Āyurvedische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Āyurvedic transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Caraka Saṃhitā; Suśruta Saṃhitā; Vāgbhaṭa, Aṣṭāṅgahṛdaya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Transformation by supernormal power, the first of the direct knowledges treated in the handbook."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Iddhividha",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Arten übernatürlicher Kraft",
        "en": "The kinds of supernormal power"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source holds that the question is malformed for its path: there is no bounded self that could either be dissolved or preserved, so neither end state is what it aims at."
      },
      "id": "pm:selfBoundaryDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Abgelehnte Selbstgrenze",
        "en": "Self boundary declined"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "As with pm:normDistinctionDeclined, this is a positive claim about a source and not a place to put an undetermined case.",
          "The value was first written as the single place for everything that is neither dissolution nor separation, and the material has since shown that too coarse: a marriage is not a non-position, and a non-position is not a claim about which level the question belongs to. The two clearest of those have their own values below. What remains here is the plain form — the source denies that there is a bounded self to be dealt with either way — and the case where a source instead declares the whole level of the question superseded, which is a further distinction still to be made when a second instance of it appears."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The work in which the passage was actually read, given as a bibliographic reference, where that work is not the one carrying the claim."
      },
      "id": "pm:readVia",
      "label": {
        "de": "gelesen über",
        "en": "read via"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Deliberately separate from dcterms:source, for the same reason pm:evidenceFrom is. dcterms:source names what a reader must obtain to check the claim; this names what the writer of the node had in front of him. A record that fused them would report second-hand reading as first-hand and would lose the one distinction that makes an unreachable source admissible at all.",
          "Required wherever pm:attestedBy is pm:mediatedAttestation. Carries a bibliographic reference and never a URL, enforced by pm:ReadViaIsLiteratureShape."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AscentPresupposesTheRungs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schau der Schönheit selbst setzt den Aufstieg über die Stufen voraus",
        "en": "The vision of beauty itself presupposes the ascent by rungs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The word rendered as rungs stands in the base text at 211c, which is why this counts as a stated ordering and not as a modern reading of a sequence of paragraphs."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The ascent is described as rightly conducted only when it proceeds as by rungs of a ladder, from one body to two, from bodies to pursuits, from pursuits to studies, and thence to beauty itself; the one who does not go this way does not arrive at the final vision.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BeautyItself"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BeautyOfOneBody"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 211c",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Platonic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from meteors, given in place of the knowledge of the clouds in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfShootingStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Sternschnuppen",
        "en": "Signs of the shooting stars"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The apāna is raised from below while the prāṇa is pressed down from the throat, and the two are brought together in the region of the navel."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die beiden Winde werden zugleich in entgegengesetzte Richtungen getrieben",
        "en": "The two winds are driven in opposite directions at once"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The manual assigns direction to the two winds, not to the two phases of breathing. Both directions run in the same operation and against each other, so a scheme that reads inhalation as upward and exhalation as downward, or the reverse, has no purchase here. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.50 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earliest European account, from travels of eighteen sixty-eight to eighteen seventy-one, already describes the whole apparatus: an oily preparation from a red wood, given to a hen, whose death or survival decides the question; the rubbing-board consulted before any undertaking of consequence; and the use of both to establish the guilt or innocence of the accused, with suspected witches tried by the same ordeal. It records a case in which a hostile chief called off an attack because his fowl had died."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeAtFirstContact",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Giftprobe beim ersten dauerhaften Kontakt",
        "en": "The poison ordeal at the first sustained contact"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The corroborative second test is not in this account. That is an argument about the account and not about the transmission: the writer was a botanist passing through with an ivory company, not an observer of consultations, and he reports the outcome of single fowls because that is what he saw. What the silence does establish is that the double test is not attested before the monograph in anything read here, and the question of when it can first be shown is left open as an issue.",
          "The passage is the reason the contact route on this tradition is not simply documented. The oracles stand in the record at the first sustained contact with the Islamic north and cannot have come in with it."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KevalaPresupposesSahita",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das alleinige Anhalten setzt das Anhalten mit Füllen und Leeren voraus",
        "en": "Retention alone presupposes retention with filling and emptying"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The instruction is to keep practising the accompanied form until the unaccompanied one succeeds. Recorded at prescribed order because the verse sets a sequence without saying what befalls one who skips it. The direction of the breath drops out at the top of this sequence, which is what makes the claim that inhalation draws and exhalation expels a claim about the lower rungs only. See prima-materia#27."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that all known ways to immortality fall into three — that of the fakir working on the body, that of the monk on the emotions, that of the yogi on the mind — each long, hard and one-sided; and that a fourth works on all three at once, requires no withdrawal from life, and reaches the same goal sooner for that reason."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vier Wege zu einem Ziel, unterschieden danach, wie viel am Menschen sie bearbeiten",
        "en": "Four ways to one goal, distinguished by how much of a person they work on"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Recorded chiefly as a negative finding. The fourth way is regularly listed among the third options beside a right-hand and a left-hand path, and the source will not carry that. All four ways are said to lead in one direction, and the direction is named: immortality. What separates them is how much of a person is worked on and whether life must be left. That is an axis of completeness, not of the direction of the will, and it is compatible with either end of the other one. See prima-materia#65.",
          "The case is more useful than a mere correction, because it is a whole system in which the goal is held fixed and the entire classification falls on the means. Whoever doubts that method and goal can be varied independently has here a taxonomy that varies only one of them. See prima-materia#52 and prima-materia#67.",
          "The goal as named — an immortality that the person acquires and is not born with — sits on the separative side of the telic axis rather than the unitive one. That placement is not asserted on this node: it would need the passages on the four bodies read as well, and those have not been read here."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "Fragments of an unknown and older teaching, as the book's own subtitle has it, rather than a doctrine set out in talks of the early twentieth century and published in 1949.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the chapter on the ways to immortality and the fourth way",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about whether the eight limbs form a sequence that must be climbed. The sūtra that names them enumerates and says nothing about order; the commentarial layer introduces the categories of outer and inner limb, and later teaching hardens these into a rule of practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LimbOrderDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Glieder der Reihe nach zu durchlaufen sind",
        "en": "Whether the limbs must be traversed in order"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The disagreement is not between two traditions but between a base text and its readers, which is the commonest shape a dispute takes in this collection and the easiest to lose, because the readers usually win by default."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
        "Vyāsa, Yogabhāṣya on Yogasūtra II.29"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first of the three parts of the way, consisting in the keeping of the commandments and the labour against the passions."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Praktike",
      "label": {
        "de": "Praktikē",
        "en": "Ascetic practice"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where the oracle contradicts itself the failure is attributed to one of eight causes: the wrong variety of the creeper having been gathered, the breach of a taboo, witchcraft, the anger of the owners of the forest in which the creeper grows, the age of the poison, the anger of the ghosts, sorcery, and use. Two varieties of the creeper are distinguished by name, and poison that kills without discrimination is set aside for some months to cool and is thrown away if it does not."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeFailureCauses",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die acht Ursachen, denen ein Versagen des Orakels zugeschrieben wird",
        "en": "The eight causes to which a failure of the oracle is attributed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The list is the reason the falsifying condition on the neighbouring node does not function as one inside the transmission. Every occurrence of the condition is assigned to a cause that leaves the oracle itself untouched, and the recorder reports that the errors as well as the sound judgements are taken as proof of its accuracy. That sentence is the recorder's summary of an attitude and not a quoted Zande statement, and it is entered as such.",
          "This is also the passage on which the whole later argument about whether Zande thought is closed against refutation rests. None of that argument was read at the wording in this run; it stands as an issue with the works named."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 155 and pp. 155-157",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An early twentieth century system of three selves and directed vital force, presented as the secret teaching of Hawaiian priests."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Huna",
      "label": {
        "de": "Huna",
        "en": "Huna"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Constructed by an outsider from theosophical and New Thought material and rejected by Hawaiian scholars as a representation of anything Hawaiian. Registered as the transmission it actually is, so that the ascription can be entered as false without the material vanishing from the record."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A falsifying condition is stated and no case has yet been recorded against it."
      },
      "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases",
      "label": {
        "de": "Verfahren ohne Fälle",
        "en": "Procedure without cases"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsParacittaJnana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama auf die Vorstellung bringt Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama on the presented idea yields knowledge of another mind"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that in identifying a person is absorbed into what occupies them and becomes a thing, and that those who observe this state in themselves regularly take it for a merit, calling it enthusiasm, zeal, passion, spontaneity or inspiration, and believing that good work is possible only in it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der aufzulösende Zustand wird für den erstrebten gehalten",
        "en": "The state to be undone is mistaken for the state to be sought"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It stands beside the self-caution recorded at pmc:Panchatattva, where a text prescribes a rite and warns in the same breath that men of the age will take it up for pleasure. Two traditions with no route between them both place the danger inside the practitioner's own approval. Not asserted as a convergence here; noted as a second instance of the same shape.",
          "Recorded as a caution that names its own misrecognition, which is the form worth collecting. The warning is not that the state is unpleasant — it is that the state is valued, under names that make it sound like the goal. A caution of this shape cannot be tested by asking practitioners how they feel, since the claim predicts they will report it favourably."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "An observation of the school, reported as long known.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the passage on identifying and the names given to it",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of several thousand readings given in a sleep-like state, most of them addressed to a named person about a stated condition, and taken down stenographically."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CayceReadings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Cayce-Readings",
        "en": "The Cayce readings"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The one corpus in this block built for examination without intending to be: individual readings, individual recipients, dated, indexed, and with outcomes that were sometimes followed up. Whatever it yields, it yields countably."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The indexed Cayce readings held by the Association for Research and Enlightenment; Sugrue, There Is a River",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the metals of the earth and of working them into blades, shields and body armour."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Metalworking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Metallverarbeitung und Waffenherstellung",
        "en": "Metalworking and weapon-making"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the attempt to fail the claim has been carried."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:examinationState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfstand",
        "en": "examination state"
      },
      "range": "pm:ExaminationState",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Shia transmission of a graded emanation and of an inner meaning disclosed by initiation, together with the encyclopaedic Neoplatonism of the Brethren of Purity."
      },
      "id": "pmt:IsmailiCosmology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ismailitische Kosmologie",
        "en": "Ismaili cosmology"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Sijistānī, Kashf al-Maḥjūb; Nāṣir-i Khusraw, Jāmiʿ al-Ḥikmatayn; Rasāʾil Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The lowland South American transmission of songs used to direct the effect of a plant preparation, and of a body that can be exchanged for another."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Amazonian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Amazonische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Amazonian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No corpus in its own language and no writing before contact. What exists is transcribed song and ethnographic report, which this project cannot yet admit as a source. Registered so that the exclusion is visible rather than silent."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "No transmitted written corpus; the published icaro transcriptions and the ethnographic record stand in its place",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When a man struck by lightning is left alive, injured but living, he is held to be as if divinely chosen for the service of the huacas. The earlier report gives the claim in the first person of the transmission: those called camascas said that the grace and virtue they had was received from the Thunder — that when lightning fell and left a man in terror, he said on coming to himself that the Thunder had shown him that art, whether curing with herbs or giving answers to what was asked; and the same was said of a man who escaped a river or a great danger."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightningStrikeElection",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der vom Blitz Getroffene gilt als erwählt",
        "en": "The lightning-struck is taken as elected"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The two do not say the same thing about what is received. The later gives a status — he is as if elected. The earlier gives a content — the Thunder showed him an art, and names two arts. The difference is recorded rather than merged, because a status can be conferred by the community after the fact and a taught art cannot.",
          "Two recorders, forty-six years apart, in different regions, on different sides of the colonial administration, one writing in Cuzco and one in the archdiocese of Lima, give the same claim. Neither cites the other. This is the best-attested single claim in the file, and the only one for which the internal date spread is wide enough to be worth stating."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fols. 6v-7r, on the camascas",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ZuniEfficacyPresupposesExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das öffentlich gesprochene Gebet setzt buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe voraus",
        "en": "The public prayer presupposes letter-perfect delivery"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered one step below the Māori and Navajo parallels and for a reason that is a property of the passage and not of the tradition: the requirement is stated, the consequence of failing it is not. Raising this to stated necessity would need a place in the corpus that says what a misrecited prayer does, and the body of the collection was not read. See the convergence node and prima-materia#112."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment of the path, held here to record that no procedure for failing it has been devised."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SeparativeEndStateExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der absondernde Endzustand überhaupt geprüft wurde",
        "en": "Whether the separative end state has been examined at all"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No pm:falsifiedBy is given, and the omission is the permitted one: the state says outright that no procedure has been devised. Everywhere else its absence would let a claim drift from unexamined to examined-and-unsupported.",
          "The asymmetry is worth naming because it is easy to mistake for evidence. States of dissolution have measurable correlates and a literature; the end state claimed on the other side has neither instrument nor operationalisation. That is a fact about the instruments. Whoever infers from it that one path is better attested than the other has read the reach of the measuring apparatus as a finding about the paths. See prima-materia#73.",
          "This node exists to prevent a confusion, not to report a result. Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted, and that is a statement about the means of examination and not about the claim. A record that left the protocol out entirely would say the same thing by silence; a record that reported the claim as unsupported would say something else and something false."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-29",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The diviner by dreams. Someone comes to ask whether he will recover or die, or whether a lost horse will be found. If the questioner is a man the diviner asks for the band from his head, or his bag, or his cloak, or some other part of his clothing; if a woman, for her sash or the like. He takes it home, sleeps on it, and answers according to what he dreams. Where the question is about love, he asks for the hair or the clothing of the person he is to divine about."
      },
      "id": "pmp:AndeanDreamDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrsagen durch Schlafen auf dem Kleidungsstück des Fragenden",
        "en": "Divining by sleeping on the questioner's garment"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The identifier carries the name of the tradition because the plain one was already taken by a practice of another transmission in this graph. That is a property of a flat identifier space and not of the practice; see prima-materia#275.",
          "The instrument is a possession of the person the question is about, not of the questioner, wherever the two differ. That distinction is stated in the source and is the only place in this file where the procedure names what it is a procedure on."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the moscoc",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Awareness whose reach is unbounded across all possible subjects."
      },
      "id": "pm:universalAwareness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Universelles Bewusstsein",
        "en": "Universal awareness"
      },
      "type": "pm:AwarenessSpace"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The whole is contemplated in the form of the path of worlds and the other paths, in sequence, in its coarse, subtle and supreme condition, until the mind dissolves; and the same method carried to the limits of the universe is said to bring the great awakening."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WorldPathTraversal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Durchquerung des Weltenpfads",
        "en": "The traversal of the path of worlds"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the old form of the claim that attention placed in a centre opens an assigned layer. The layers here are worlds, bhuvana, and they belong to a cosmology written into the body, not to a list of psychological qualities. The distance between the two is the whole point of the mapping node above. See prima-materia#39."
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 57 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Perception of what is distant, hidden or obstructed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Auge",
        "en": "Divine eye"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A candidate for the office is expected to be the pupil of a babalawo known for wide knowledge of the religion and for skill in divining, for at least three years, and the course is divided into three parts covering those years. In the first the candidate learns the names of all the signs and the marks that stand for them and shows his command of them in practice; in the second he learns the traditions attached to them."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BabalawoPupilage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Lehrzeit des Anwärters auf das Amt des Babalawo",
        "en": "The pupilage of a candidate for the office of babalawo"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "What the third year requires was not recovered from the portion of the extract delivered in this run. The gap is a property of the retrieval and not of the work, and it is recorded so that the next reader opens the appendix at that place rather than repeating the whole."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorder states that the practitioner is paid very well for the consultation and is additionally courted with food and drink after their own fashion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheSpecialistIsPaid",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Fachmann wird bezahlt, und gut bezahlt",
        "en": "The specialist is paid, and paid well"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because payment is one of the gates this project counts, and because it stands beside the other one: the same chapter says the practice is hidden from outsiders. A transmission can be closed by price and by secrecy at once, and the two are not the same gate."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission holds the rubbing-board inferior to the other oracles because the man who works it can cheat with it, and only he may work it. The charge is raised in practice when the oracle has spoken against the questioner or when he dislikes the operator. Of the poison oracle, by contrast, the source records that a manipulation is never even suggested."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTheRubbingBoard",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Reibbrett lässt sich vom Betreiber führen",
        "en": "The rubbing-board can be worked by the operator"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The warning names the failure mode of an oracle whose outcome one party controls, and names it from inside the transmission rather than from the recorder's side. It is the counterpart of the passage on the second test that is not made."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 173 and p. 153",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BaraqelTeachesAstrology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Baraqel offenbart die Astrologie",
        "en": "Baraqel discloses astrology"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Astrology"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Baraqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The two recorders describe the same instrument and disagree about what is invoked over it. The compilation of sixteen seventy-four has the drum made from a tree whose grain turns with the course of the sun, because the tree is held acceptable to the sun, which they worship under the image of Thor; the whole art is treated as heathen and the answers as the devil's. The account of seventeen sixty-seven reports a named practitioner who, going to use his drum, marked himself and it with the sign of the cross as an earnest of good success, said the Lord's Prayer, invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel to assist him, and in the middle of the work broke out into a prayer to the God who created the heaven and the earth, calling himself a man liable to error, old and unworthy, and saying it were better that he should die than that God should deny help to the one on whose behalf they entreated."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhoseTheDrumIs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Trommel der Sonne gehört oder Gott",
        "en": "Whether the drum belongs to the sun or to God"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Ninety years lie between them and the second recorder had read the first, so this is not two independent descriptions of one moment. What it is, is a transmission caught in the middle of taking something over: the instrument, the hammer, the ring and the rule of the sun's course are unchanged, and what is said over them has been replaced. The reading rule survived the theology that was given for it, which says something about which of the two the practice actually rested on.",
          "That the later station added something to what it received is exactly the shape pm:Reworking is for, and no reworking node is written: the addition here is Christian and the transmission it came from is not the one that performed the act. The class as it stands wants one tradition taking over from another, and this is a tradition taking over from the mission that was suppressing it. The gap is recorded rather than forced.",
          "The second recorder notices one detail against himself and reports it: that in calling the Holy Ghost an angel the man used a word he had never heard the Lapps use for it anywhere he knew, where they say Passe Vuoign. He records the anomaly instead of correcting it, which is why the passage can be used at all."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 476-477"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
