    {
      "id": "pmc:ValidConfessionPresupposesCompleteness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine gültige Beichte setzt voraus, dass nichts verschwiegen wurde",
        "en": "A confession that holds presupposes that nothing was withheld"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source names the outcome of the want and not merely the rule: the confession was not good and is to be made again. That the finding of the want is itself made by a randomising device is the point of the neighbouring node and does not weaken the necessity, which is stated independently of how it is detected."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Where the bead breaks in two rather than three, or the straws come out odd, the confession was not good and the penitent is told to confess his faults again."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ConfessionValidityTest"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the mouth is held closed and the breath is drawn through both nostrils so that it grazes the throat and sounds as it passes."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Ujjayi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ujjāyī",
        "en": "Ujjāyī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.51-53 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is addressed falls into two kinds. The fixed, which cannot be taken away because they do not move: the sun, the moon, the Pleiades, the lightning, the sea, the earth, springs, rivers, snow peaks, high hills and certain very large stones, and the pacarina, the place each lineage says it came out of. And the movable, which are the ordinary huacas of a place: mostly stone, mostly without a figure, each with its own name, each belonging to a lineage, each with its own priests. Beside them stand the malquis, the bones or whole bodies of the ancestors, held to be children of the huacas and given the same offerings; and the conopas, held in each house and inherited by the eldest son, which are the same worship made private."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HuacaAsAddressedPower",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Huaca als angesprochene Macht",
        "en": "The huaca as a power that is addressed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The division is the recorder's and it is his working distinction as an extirpator: the movable ones could be seized and burned and the fixed ones had to be taken out of the heart instead. It is entered because it is also a real division in the practice — a lineage huaca is public and a conopa is secret — and not because his purpose was neutral."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The fragmentary hexameters that the later Platonists treated as revealed, and on which their theurgic practice was built."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChaldeanOracles",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chaldäische Orakel",
        "en": "Chaldean Oracles"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The graph already holds the theurgy that rests on this text without holding the text."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
