      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ReturnPresupposesUnbrokenSinging",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rückkehr setzt voraus, dass der Gesang nicht abbricht",
        "en": "The return presupposes that the singing does not stop"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in plain terms and twice over: the singing must not cease and no one may stir him, and no fly or living creature is suffered to touch him. The recorder adds that the watching is for this reason and not, as another writer held, out of fear that the devil would take the body away; he names that writer and rejects him. The correction is his and is recorded as his."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "He would never recover out of the trance if they either ceased singing, or any one stirred him with hand or foot."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:DistantSight"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 56-57",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wurzelsiegel bringt das entfachte innere Feuer hervor",
        "en": "The root seal yields the kindled inner fire"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The pairing is set at the fire and not at the reversal on purpose. The manual defines the root seal as the drawing upward of the downward wind, so a claim that the seal produces that upward movement would assert only that the practice is what it is. The heat, the lengthened flame and the waking of the power are consequences the verses state beyond the definition, and they are what a protocol could address."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Mulabandha"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:KindledInnerFire"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An attempt to fail the claim that a misrecited ritual utterance does not obtain its effect. The claim is unusually well placed for this, because two of the three traditions name an outward event that follows a lapse, and one of them names who notices it and when."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfprotokoll zur Genauigkeitsregel",
        "en": "Protocol on the exactness rule"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The Navajo witness supplies the sharpest form of the test and it needs no laboratory: at the public ceremony many present know the songs, an error is caught, and the ceremony is broken off as fruitless. The condition can therefore be failed by the tradition's own reporting rather than by an outside measurement, which is what the falsifier below states.",
          "The examination is entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised, and the difference is the whole point of the scale. A condition has been stated here; nobody has yet collected the cases against it."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the three witnesses; no cases collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded case, from within one of these transmissions, of a ritual utterance known to have been misrecited and nonetheless reported by that transmission as having obtained its effect; or, on the Navajo side, a recorded public ceremony in which a detected error did not break off the proceeding.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-08-30",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How firmly the source sets the prerequisite."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:prerequisiteStrength",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stärke der Voraussetzung",
        "en": "prerequisite strength"
      },
      "range": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The works in which this transmission is chiefly carried, named so that a later reader can find them."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Tradition",
      "id": "pm:principalCorpus",
      "label": {
        "en": "principal corpus"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A source carries a passage that was read, with its place and its edition; this names a corpus that was not read. Using dcterms:source here would let a registration claim a textual knowledge it does not have. The literature rule applies all the same: a work, never a URL."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Concealing a fault in confession is held a great fault, and the confessor makes great efforts to find it out, by different ceremonies in different places. In some, the penitent first calls on the hills around, the plains, the condors that fly, the owls, saying that he wishes to confess his faults; he says them holding a small bead of shell fixed on a thorn between two fingers of the right hand, the thorn raised; when he has finished he gives it to the confessor, who drives the thorn into his cloak and presses until the bead breaks, and looks at how many parts it broke into. If it broke into three the confession was good; if into two it was not good, and he tells him to confess his faults again. In other places a small bundle of the grass called ichu, from which the confessor's name is taken, is divided in two and straws are drawn one from each side until it is seen whether they come out even, and then the confession was good, or odd, and it was bad. In others they divine it by the blood of guinea pigs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConfessionValidityTest",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Test, der entscheidet, ob eine Beichte galt",
        "en": "The test that decides whether a confession held"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A falsifying condition stated by the transmission, applied by its own practitioners to its own act, with a stated outcome and a stated remedy: the confession is void and must be made again. It is not a test of whether the specialist is right; it is a test of whether the layman held something back, so its target is the penitent and not the instrument. That is the opposite arrangement from the one this graph holds for the Zande poison oracle, and the difference is worth more than the resemblance.",
          "The same passage records what the procedure is when it fails to persuade: in one village the penitent's hands were tied behind him and squeezed with a cord until he told the truth; a man said before the visitor that his confessor beat him with a stick and another that he was beaten with a rope. A validity test with a coercive fallback is not an oracle, and both are in the same paragraph.",
          "The three variants are three different randomising devices — a break count, a parity count and an inspection of blood — attached to one function. That the function is stable across three devices, and that a fourth device with the same parity mechanism is used for divination proper elsewhere in the same corpus, is the finding here."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the account of the confession before the ichuri",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "First-person singular awareness bounded to a single subject."
      },
      "id": "pm:individualAwareness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Individuelles Bewusstsein",
        "en": "Individual awareness"
      },
      "type": "pm:AwarenessSpace"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The art of bringing the resentment of another person's own family atua down upon them, worked upon something that has been part of them or has touched them: a portion of spittle, or what is left of their food."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Makutu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Makutu",
        "en": "Makutu"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The operation runs through the same transmissibility by contact that tapu runs through, which is why the two belong in one file and not in two."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The early Chinese transmission of breath circulation, guided stretching and dietary regimen for the prolongation of life, with illustrated postures."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yangsheng",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yangsheng-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Yangsheng nourishing-life transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Mawangdui and Zhangjiashan manuscripts are dated by their burial, which puts the breath material before any documented contact with Indian practice. This is the single most valuable registration in the file for the independence question."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Mawangdui manuscripts, including Daoyin tu and Quegu shiqi; the Zhangjiashan Yinshu; Huangting jing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seal in which the tongue is turned back and set above the palate while the gaze rests between the brows; the manual describes a months-long preparation of the tongue before the position can be held."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Khecari",
      "label": {
        "de": "Khecarī",
        "en": "Khecarī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.32-33 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Lakota transmission of the seven rites, of the vision sought under fasting, and of the pipe as the instrument of address."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LakotaRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lakota-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Lakota ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The best known account passed through two editors, and the difference between what was said and what was printed is itself documented. The tradition should enter the graph together with that dispute, not before it."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Brown, The Sacred Pipe; Neihardt, Black Elk Speaks; DeMallie, The Sixth Grandfather, as the record of the mediation",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The twentieth century Burmese reform transmission of insight practice as a lay technique detachable from monastic life, taught in fixed courses."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ModernVipassana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Moderne Vipassanā-Bewegung",
        "en": "Modern vipassanā movement"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered separately from the canon and the commentaries because it is a distinct transmission with its own founders and manuals, and because its relation to the older material, in particular its treatment of absorption, is contested inside the tradition itself."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Manual of Insight; Ledi Sayādaw, the Manuals of Buddhism",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what becomes of the bounded self at the end of the path, carried on inside the modern occult literature with the same term on both sides. One party holds that a self which is not surrendered ossifies and is consumed by time, and calls that the second death; the other holds that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment, and names it with a word of self-generation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhetherTheSelfIsKeptOrSurrendered",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Bewahren des Selbst das Ziel des Weges ist oder sein Scheitern",
        "en": "Whether keeping the self is the goal of the path or its failure"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One asymmetry the record should not smooth. The earlier side values the matter from a unitive end and treats the surviving self as a remainder that time consumes; the later values it from a separative end and treats the same survival as the work itself. Neither is a misreading of the other. A continuity story running from the one to the other as a single tradition with a changing mood reports a break as a development.",
          "That is why the disagreement is worth a node. Read as a quarrel about practices it dissolves into nothing, because neither side defines the path by practices. Read as a quarrel about the end state it is sharp and not decidable from inside either work, because each describes the same movement and calls it by the opposite name. See prima-materia#61 and prima-materia#68.",
          "The sides do not differ over means, and both say so. The one states that the signpost of a right-hand and a left-hand path is barely legible, that the two look alike for a long way, and that the question is at which point they diverge; he puts the divergence at the crossing of the Abyss and separates the sorcerer from the brother of the left-hand path as sharply as a thief from a tyrant. The other derives his word from an Egyptian term for coming into being and applies it to the transformation of the will, not to any particular practice. Whatever is at issue between them, it is not method."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. Crowley, Magick Without Tears, chapter XII, on the left-hand path and the black brothers (electronic text of the collected letters)",
        "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierte Vertiefung voraus",
        "en": "Supernormal power presupposes the fourth absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook assigns the task to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption the natural plane for obtaining the power. It does not, at these places, say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourthJhana"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.12-13 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:CastingYieldsForeknowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Wurf bringt das Vorauswissen hervor",
        "en": "The casting yields foreknowledge"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:IfaCasting"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:IfaForeknowledge"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The breath practice of the second chapter, directed at the clearing of the central channel."
      },
      "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atemregulierung im Haṭhayoga",
        "en": "Breath regulation in Haṭhayoga"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorder states that the people of this land, like those of Peru, hide the matter with the greatest diligence; that they deny it stubbornly even when accused and convicted, until they die impenitent; and that neither the fear of God nor an oath nor anything else avails to make them confess."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConcealmentFromTheRecorder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Praxis wird dem Fragenden verborgen",
        "en": "The practice is concealed from the questioner"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The comparison with Peru is the recorder's and is not evidence of any connection between the two. It is entered because it shows he was generalising across two colonial situations, which is one of the ways a report acquires content its informants never supplied.",
          "This is the most valuable sentence in what was read, and it cuts two ways. It records a gate: the transmission was held closed against an outsider who had the power to punish, which is a fact about the tradition. And it records the limit of the whole manual as a witness, in the words of the man who wrote it: what he has is what could not be hidden from him. Every other node in this file is to be read against it."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos I and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practitioner beats the drum and sings, others present singing with him in higher and lower notes. He then casts himself on the ground, grows black in the face and lies as if dead, the drum laid as near as possible on his head, sweating. Those present do not leave off singing for the whole time. When he awakes he relates all the circumstances of the business enquired after."
      },
      "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reise bei stillliegendem Leib",
        "en": "The journey made while the body lies still"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of one form standing for another within awareness."
      },
      "id": "pm:Symbolizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Symbolisieren",
        "en": "Symbolizing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Korean transmission of the mudang, whose office is entered through a sickness resolved only by accepting it, and whose ritual is a negotiation carried out in the voice of the dead."
      },
      "id": "pmt:KoreanMuism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Koreanischer Musok",
        "en": "Korean muism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Chiefly oral. The corpus is a collection of transcribed ritual songs, which the project has no attestation mode for yet."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The published muga song transcriptions; Akiba and Akamatsu's collected materials",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Restraint, observance, posture, breath regulation, withdrawal of the senses, concentration, meditation and absorption, listed together in a single sūtra."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die acht Glieder",
        "en": "The eight limbs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sūtra names them and says nothing about traversing them in order."
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of ritual handbooks for the constraint of spirits, carrying preparation, circle, seal, adjuration and dismissal as a fixed sequence."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SolomonicGrimoire",
      "label": {
        "de": "Salomonische Grimoire-Tradition",
        "en": "Solomonic grimoire transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Clavicula Salomonis; Lemegeton; Heptameron; the Book of Abramelin",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the distinction of bitter and sweet and the art of writing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Penemue",
      "label": {
        "de": "Penemue",
        "en": "Penemue"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The substance is held to be transmitted by unilinear descent from parent to child of the same sex: all the sons of a male witch are witches and none of his daughters, all the daughters of a female witch are witches and none of her sons."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ManguSexLinkedInheritance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Hexerei geht auf die Kinder desselben Geschlechts über",
        "en": "Witchcraft passes to the children of the same sex"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is checkable in a way few in this graph are, because the transmission itself draws consequences from it in practice and then declines the widest of them: in theory every clansman of a proven witch would be one, and in practice only close paternal kinsmen are so regarded."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 2-3",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:GadreelTeachesWeaponsOfDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gadreel offenbart die Waffen des Todes",
        "en": "Gadreel discloses the weapons of death"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:WeaponsOfDeath"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Gadreel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese esoteric transmission claiming buddhahood in the present body, achieved through the coordination of gesture, speech and visualisation."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shingon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shingon",
        "en": "Shingon"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kūkai, Sokushin jōbutsu gi; Kūkai, Hizō hōyaku",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An Iranian transmission teaching successive bodily lives as a purification, carried in Gorani verse."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yarsan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yarsanische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Yarsan transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kalām-e Saranjām",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the bounded self is dissolved into, or united with, something that exceeds it, and treats the persistence of the separate self as what is to be given up."
      },
      "id": "pm:unitiveTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vereinigendes Ziel",
        "en": "Unitive telos"
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A practice repeated under a stated procedure that records its failures alongside its successes."
      },
      "id": "pm:protocolledPractice",
      "label": {
        "de": "Protokollierte Praxis",
        "en": "Protocolled practice"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A prohibition attached to a named place: garments of new caribou skin may not be sewn on the island of Oqshoriaq."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Verbot, auf Oqshoriaq neues Karibufell zu nähen",
        "en": "The prohibition on sewing new caribou skin on Oqshoriaq"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prohibition is local and specific, which distinguishes it from the general taboo system the later chapters of the volume set out. Whether it is a case of that system or a rule of its own cannot be decided from the portion read."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How firmly a source sets a prerequisite: as a necessity, as an instruction, or merely by the order in which it presents its material."
      },
      "id": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stärke der Voraussetzung",
        "en": "Prerequisite Strength"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The three must not collapse. A list of eight limbs in the order given is not the same claim as a sentence saying that the eighth is unreachable without the seventh, and reading the first as the second is the commonest way a modern system acquires an ancient warrant it never had."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:StillnessPresupposesRepentance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stille und Liebe setzen Buße und den Kampf gegen die Leidenschaften voraus",
        "en": "Stillness and love presuppose repentance and the labour against the passions"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The work is emphatic that neither stillness nor the love of God is reached without the prior repentance and the fight with the passions. It does not, however, state for every rung that the next is unreachable without it, and the image of the ladder is didactic before it is ontological, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Praktike"
      },
      "source": "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Steiner's transmission of a schooling of perception said to open supersensible cognition, presented as a discipline with stated stages and moral prerequisites."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Anthroposophy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Anthroposophie",
        "en": "Anthroposophy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prerequisites are stated as conditions without which the exercises are held to be harmful, which makes them a prerequisite chain of the strong kind rather than an exhortation."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten; Steiner, Die Geheimwissenschaft im Umriss",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A claim held in the graph solely so that a protocol has something to address. It witnesses no transmission, claims no source, and belongs to no tradition."
      },
      "id": "pm:Positing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Setzen",
        "en": "Positing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The class exists because a protocol needs an object and because the claims most worth examining are often the ones no received work carries. It is deliberately narrow: an instance may exist only where a pm:Testing points at it, enforced by pm:PositingRequiresExaminationShape. Without that restriction the class would become the channel through which an unpublished draft enters the record after all, and the direction the project runs in would be reversed."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The sixteen figures the casting can produce, named mothers in the account of eighteen ninety-four. A figure formed by taking one column from one mother and one column from another is named a child."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaSixteenMothers",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sechzehn Figuren, Mütter genannt",
        "en": "The sixteen figures called mothers"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A scribed corpus in three parts, teaching that the perceived world is a projection of guilt, and carrying a workbook of three hundred and sixty-five daily exercises in fixed order."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CourseInMiracles",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Kurs in Wundern",
        "en": "A Course in Miracles"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The workbook is a stated curriculum with an order and a duration, which is rare in this block and puts the corpus closer to the manual traditions than to the revealed cosmologies."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "A Course in Miracles: Text, Workbook for Students, Manual for Teachers",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The knowledge of what the seed-star is made of, how heavy it is and how it moves, said in the account to have been unavailable to men while the star's existence was known but its characteristics unrevealed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kenntnis der Merkmale des Same-Sterns",
        "en": "Knowledge of the characteristics of the seed-star"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282 and 284",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Associates a MeaningCluster with the individual nodes that constitute it as a bounded subgraph."
      },
      "domain": "pm:MeaningCluster",
      "id": "pm:hasClusterMember",
      "label": {
        "en": "has cluster member"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1916",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a creature which ceases to distinguish falls into the undifferentiated fullness, ceases to be a creature and is given over to dissolution in nothingness, so that its natural striving runs towards distinctiveness against a primeval and perilous sameness; and that the striving is nevertheless not after difference but after the creature's own being."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Unterschiedenheit als Streben des Geschöpfs gegen die Auflösung",
        "en": "Distinctiveness as the striving of the creature against dissolution"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Placed on the telic axis as pm:individuatingTelos. The placement is a reading and rests on the two refusals the passage makes in the same breath: dissolution is called the death of the creature, and the striving is said not to be after difference. Stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted.",
          "Recorded because it is the only position located so far that names both ends of the axis as a loss and then states a third movement. Dissolution into the fullness is called the death of the creature outright, which rules out the unitive end; and the striving is expressly not after difference as it is thought, which rules out the separative one. What remains is a striving after one's own being, and the source gives it the name principium individuationis.",
          "The attribution is why this is an origination and not a witness of gnostic transmission. The author printed the text under the name of a second-century teacher of Alexandria who did not write it, and it continues to be read and cited as gnostic teaching. pm:circulatesAs records that, and the node makes no judgement about the author's own reading of the ancient material.",
          "The god named above the god of the pairs of opposites belongs to the same argument and not to a separate doctrine: the qualities of the fullness are given as pairs, and what is set above them unites the full and the empty. A reading that takes this for a third party in a pantheon takes the figure for the argument."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A gnostic text of the second century, printed under the name of Basilides of Alexandria and read as ancient teaching rather than as a work written in 1916.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "C. G. Jung",
      "pm:telicOrientation": {
        "id": "pm:individuatingTelos"
      },
      "source": "C. G. Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos, Sermo I (English translation of the 1916 private printing, Gnostic Society Library electronic edition)",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Sámi transmission of a drum read by the travel of a ring across painted figures, of an office entered through a threefold seizure, and of a journey to a distant place undertaken while the body lies as if dead and from which a token is brought back."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SamiNoaidi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Samische Noaidi-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Sámi noaidi transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run, both at the wording: the compilation of sixteen seventy-four in its Oxford English edition, chapter VIII on the religion and chapter XI on the magical ceremonies; and the account of Danish Lapland of seventeen sixty-seven in the English translation printed in a collection of eighteen hundred and eight, its chapter on the magic arts. Left unread: the remaining thirty-three chapters of the first and the remaining twenty-two of the second, and the treatise on the pagan religion of the Finns and Lapps printed with the second.",
          "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as undecided, and the ground is inside the practice rather than around it. By seventeen sixty-seven a named practitioner marked himself and his drum with the sign of the cross, said the Lord's Prayer over it and invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel before striking it. That is a route to the Christian material this graph already holds, visible in the operation itself and not inferred from proximity. It says nothing about the route to the Siberian material or to the Norse, which is the question the registry entry meant and which is still not carried out; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers, so it holds the stronger. See prima-materia#101.",
          "The recorder of seventeen sixty-seven applies source criticism to his own material, and it is entered here because it is rare and because it bears on how the rest of this file is to be read. Of the confessions of witchcraft in the Norwegian trials he says that it is probable they were extorted, partly by violence and fear, and that it appears from the law books that some were put to the torture before they would confess. He then separates that material from what he goes on to report of the Sámi themselves. The separation is his and it is the reason the trial material is not in this file.",
          "The two are of different kinds and that is why both were opened. The first was written by a professor at Uppsala who never went to Lapland, on the commission of the Chancellor of Sweden, out of the written reports of parish priests whom he names in the text — so what it carries is at one remove and its author says so. The second was written by a missionary who lived among the people he describes and who reports named practitioners in their own words. The first is a compilation with a stated method; the second is first-hand and confessional. Where the two agree, the agreement is worth something; where the first stands alone, it stands on reports it names but this graph has not seen."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Schefferus, Lapponia, in the English edition The History of Lapland; Leem, Beskrivelse over Finmarkens Lapper; the eighteenth-century missionary manuscripts of Skanke and Randulf; Jessen, De religione paganorum Fennorum et Lapponum",
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 45-47",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The last entry: further feasts, the personation of the bear spirit, the sacred drum and the chanted prayers."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree",
      "label": {
        "de": "Einweihung in den vierten Grad",
        "en": "Initiation into the fourth degree"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source holds that the distinction does not apply to its path: nothing is kept and nothing is broken, because it denies that anything needs to be renounced, purified or transformed."
      },
      "id": "pm:normDistinctionDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Abgelehnte Normunterscheidung",
        "en": "Norm distinction declined"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The value exists because a path that declines the axis is not the same as one whose position on it is unknown, and a scale without it would force the first to be recorded as the second. It is asserted only where a source says so."
      },
      "type": "pm:MethodOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The body of narratives through which a sign delivers its answer, given in parables. One thousand six hundred and eighty are counted to each Odu, and everyone aspiring to the office of babalawo is expected to commit them, together with those of the other signs, to memory."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaVerseCorpus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die den Zeichen anhängenden Erzählungen",
        "en": "The narratives attached to the signs"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OfficePresupposesTheFast",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Amt setzt das Fasten voraus",
        "en": "The office presupposes the fast"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage. The source sets the fast, its lengths and its abstinences, and says nothing about what befalls a man who takes the office without it. A stated consequence would raise this; none stands in what was read. The variation between one month, six and a year is itself against reading a necessity into it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Strictly the rubbing-board requires the same corroborative second test as the poison and termites oracles, with the opposite outcome for the verdict to be valid. In fact the second test is very seldom made, and important questions are taken to the poison oracle instead."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RubbingBoardSecondTestSeldomMade",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Test am Reibbrett wird selten gemacht",
        "en": "The second test at the rubbing-board is seldom made"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The passage is a self-warning of the transmission against a rule it lays down, and it is the sharpest one this file carries: the control procedure exists for the instrument that is used most often and is precisely the one it is not applied to. The recorder adds the inference that the Azande must be aware that the second test always confirms the first. That is his conclusion and not a reported Zande statement, and it is not entered as a claim of the transmission."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 171",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The awareness context in which a statement is asserted."
      },
      "domain": "rdf:Statement",
      "id": "pm:assertedIn",
      "label": {
        "en": "asserted in"
      },
      "range": "pm:AwarenessContext",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Norse transmission of runic and sung operations and of a divinatory practice performed from a raised seat with a stated arrangement of singers and questioners."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NorseSeidr",
      "label": {
        "de": "Altnordische Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Old Norse ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Hávamál names eighteen effects in list form, which is a capacity catalogue in the same shape as the siddhi lists. Written down by Christians centuries after the practice, so the contact question is a question about the recording hand."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hávamál; Grímnismál; Snorri, Ynglinga saga; Eiríks saga rauða",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The scribal craft of the āšipu: a trained office with its own curriculum, treating affliction as an assault to be countered by named procedure."
      },
      "id": "pmt:MesopotamianExorcism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mesopotamische Beschwörungskunst",
        "en": "Mesopotamian exorcistic craft"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The oldest procedural corpus in writing anywhere, and older than every tradition the graph currently holds. Whatever it shares with the Greek and Jewish material is therefore a question of derivation running one way only."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Maqlû; Šurpu; the exorcist's manual KAR 44; Ludlul bēl nēmeqi; Enūma Anu Enlil",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Inuit transmission of an office whose holder is said to perceive what is hidden, to call up helping spirits and to act through them, acquired through solitude and privation and exercised for a household rather than for an institution."
      },
      "id": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq",
      "label": {
        "de": "Inuitische Angakkuq-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Inuit angakkuq transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Contact route, stated rather than assumed. No route is proposed by anyone between the Iglulik Inuit and the literate transmissions this graph already holds, and none is available for the doctrinal content. The corpus was nevertheless fixed in nineteen twenty-one and twenty-two, well after contact: the narrative itself names whalers, and the region was under mission influence at the time of the recording. The value here is therefore contact-route-disputed and not contact-route-absent, following the correction made for the Māori entry, and the tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence until the dispute stands as its own node. See prima-materia#101 and prima-materia#102.",
          "Read in the opening of the volume: the account of the working method, the first meeting near Repulse Bay, the old singer Ivaluardjuk, and the autobiography of Takornâq, printed pages ten to thirty-two. The chapters on the shamans, on the spirits and on the taboo system, which carry the terms this tradition is usually reported by, were not reached in this run. The limit is one of the access path and not of the work: the volume is digitised and public, and the portion delivered by the tool in use broke off at printed page thirty-two. See prima-materia#99 and prima-materia#100.",
          "The material read is testimony of an unusual kind for this graph. It is not a doctrinal corpus but a life story told in the first person by a woman who is herself said to have practised, recorded by an ethnographer who spoke a related language and worked without an interpreter. What that buys is a speaker attached to each claim; what it costs is that almost every capacity is ascribed by the narrator to somebody else, which is why the nodes below stand on third-party ascription and not on first-person report."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos; Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Caribou Eskimos",
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-32",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The binding of persons or events by spoken formula."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SpellCasting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Beschwörung",
        "en": "Spell-casting"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An address to the spirits of dead ancestors, invoked in genealogical order and closing in a petition that the invoked power give the address its effect. The witness insists that the ordinary rendering as charm is wrong and that invocation comes nearer."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Karakia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Karakia",
        "en": "Karakia"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The genealogies are said to be preserved because they form the ground-work of the invocation, so that the descent recited and the efficacy claimed are the same fact for this tradition rather than two."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 10-11, and the vocabulary entry karakia, p. 108",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state of the contact question between this tradition and those the graph already holds."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Tradition",
      "id": "pm:contactRoute",
      "label": {
        "en": "contact route"
      },
      "range": "pm:ContactRoute",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1997",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the left-hand path is constituted by the goal of self-deification rather than by any particular practice, and that it falls into two branches: an immanent one, materialistic in outlook and pronounced in its transgression, and a transcendental one, centred on intellect and eternal forms."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LeftHandPathTwoBranches",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der linke Pfad, über sein Ziel bestimmt, mit zwei nach der Methode unterschiedenen Zweigen",
        "en": "The left-hand path defined by its goal, with two branches differing in method"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "That is the case this graph was given a separate method axis for. In the same work the goal is held constant across both branches while the degree of transgression varies between them — an author varying one axis and holding the other fixed, without drawing the axes. See prima-materia#52 and prima-materia#67.",
          "The passage that matters most for this record is the one that denies a methodological definition outright. The source states that there are no categorical methodological proscriptions on the path in any of its traditions, eastern or western, and that methods are chosen pragmatically; the antinomian element is described as often strong, not as constitutive. The author who is usually cited as fixing the path by transgression therefore says the opposite of what he is cited for.",
          "The transcendental branch is the standing candidate for the quadrant that a one-axis scheme cannot name: a goal of self-deification without transgression as its means. Whether it actually sits there is not settled by this node and is the question of prima-materia#72."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "The old distinction of the Indian tantric traditions, reported as though the two-criteria definition were carried by them rather than formulated in 1997.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Stephen E. Flowers",
      "source": "S. E. Flowers, Lords of the Left-Hand Path: A History of Spiritual Dissent (Smithville 1997), in the chapter setting out the criteria and the two branches",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The modern transmission of a journey undertaken to a drumbeat and taught in workshops, presented as a technique abstracted from many indigenous traditions at once."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Neoshamanism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neoschamanismus",
        "en": "Neoshamanism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The abstraction claim is the point of interest: it asserts that a core method survives the removal of every particular tradition. The graph can hold that assertion beside the traditions it claims to abstract from, which is exactly what it was built to do."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Harner, The Way of the Shaman",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Enoch literature: the Book of the Watchers, the Astronomical Book, the Book of Dreams, the Epistle and, in the Ethiopic canon alone, the Book of Parables."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Enochic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Henochische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Enochic tradition"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "G.W.E. Nickelsburg, 1 Enoch 1 (Hermeneia, 2001), introduction",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The teaching that discretion is the mother of the virtues, directed against the practitioner who is undone not by laxity but by an excess undertaken in good faith."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ExcessiveAsceticismCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unterscheidung gegen das Übermaß",
        "en": "Discretion against excess"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins"
      },
      "source": "John Cassian, Collationes II (De discretione)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seed resembling a lentil is drunk; the recorder says the draught takes away the judgement, being very vehement. Those who drink it consult it as an oracle for everything they wish to know. Some hold the drinking of the seed for such consultations as their office, and such a specialist bears his own name."
      },
      "id": "pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Trinken des Ololiuhqui, um es zu befragen",
        "en": "Drinking the ololiuhqui in order to question it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Three things stand in the passage and are kept apart here: that the substance is drunk, that the drinker is questioned as an oracle, and that there is an office for it. Only the third makes this a transmission rather than a usage."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos II and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect a chantway is held to obtain, which the witness says an error in a song may destroy."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie",
        "en": "The efficacy of the ceremony"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rate against which observed outcomes are compared, with the population it was taken from."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:baseRate",
      "label": {
        "de": "Basisrate",
        "en": "base rate"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The count of the signs as the bishop's extract gives it: behind each of the sixteen representative palm nuts stand sixteen subordinate powers, and each of the whole number is itself called an Odu, which is to say a head; this gives two hundred and fifty-six. A further sixteen are counted behind each of those, which gives four thousand and ninety-six."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaOduEnumeration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zählung der Zeichen",
        "en": "The enumeration of the signs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two accounts read in this run do not generate the count the same way. The account of eighteen ninety-four compounds the further figures from the columns of two of the sixteen; the extract used here subordinates sixteen to each of the sixteen. Both arrive at a system built on sixteen, and neither states the other's derivation. The difference is recorded rather than smoothed, because which derivation a source gives is one of the things that would distinguish a locally grown scheme from a borrowed one."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the fixed groupings of stars."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Constellations",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Sternbilder",
        "en": "The constellations"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Declares by which route a claim entered the record. Mandatory on every attribution, so that non-evidence cannot be forgotten."
      },
      "id": "pm:attestedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "bezeugt durch",
        "en": "attested by"
      },
      "range": "pm:Attesting",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the moon's path, the calendrical rather than divinatory form of lunar knowledge."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CourseOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Lauf des Mondes",
        "en": "The course of the moon"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on a dated prediction the account makes about itself. A footnote states that the next ceremony would probably fall in nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three if the reckoning holds, and adds that it was publicly known at the end of nineteen fifty that it would not be celebrated for another ten years or so."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SiguiDatePrediction",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vorhersage der nächsten Zeremonie im Artikel selbst",
        "en": "The article's own prediction of the next ceremony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A rare thing in this collection: a claim that fixes its own due date, published before the date fell. Whether the ceremony fell in those years is a matter of record, and the record was not read at the wording in this run. The date is therefore not entered and the state stands at procedure without cases. See prima-materia#254."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account's own footnote; the date of the ceremony has not been established from a work",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A dated report of the ceremony beginning in a year other than nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three refutes the reckoning as the account states it, and with it the claim that the interval is governed by a fifty-year period doubled.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-01",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Sigui"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of a detailed geometry of subtle anatomy together with a claimed ancient lineage of guardianship, delivered through a living transmitter and taught in graded courses with their own technical vocabulary."
      },
      "id": "pmt:KeylonticScience",
      "label": {
        "de": "Keylontic Science und die MCEO-Lehren",
        "en": "Keylontic Science and the MCEO teachings"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two claims to keep apart. That it transmits is a fact: texts, a transmitter, courses, students, successors, and it is registered on that ground alone. That it descends from an ancient priesthood is an ascription with a modern author and a modern date, attested nowhere outside the corpus, and it enters as a pm:Originating node when it enters. The material is under active copyright, so nothing is reproduced; the definitions here are the compiler's own paraphrase, as everywhere in this graph."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ashayana Deane, Voyagers I and Voyagers II; the published MCEO Freedom Teachings course materials",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShemihazahTeachesSpellCasting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schemichasa offenbart die Beschwörung",
        "en": "Shemihazah discloses spell-casting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SpellCasting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Shemihazah"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the practice of discerning the origin of inner movements, running from the desert monastic literature through the Byzantine ascetic corpus into the Latin spiritual exercises."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment",
      "label": {
        "de": "Christliche Unterscheidung der Geister",
        "en": "Christian ascetic discernment"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The gap is load-bearing here: Evagrius is transmitted in Greek and in Syriac with divergent chapter counts, the Collationes are numbered differently in the older editions, and the marginal numbering of the Spiritual Exercises belongs to a modern editorial layer and not to the autograph."
      },
      "source": "John Cassian, Collationes I-II",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:CompletionPresupposesGeneration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vollendungsstufe setzt die Erzeugungsstufe voraus",
        "en": "The completion stage presupposes the generation stage"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The order is set by the arrangement of the treatise, which takes the generation-stage meditations before the completion-stage yogas. That the six yogas belong to the completion stage places tummo after the generation stage as well."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:CompletionStage"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:GenerationStage"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A leader among the Watchers, named in the Book of the Watchers as the one who disclosed metallurgy, weaponry and adornment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Azazel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azazel",
        "en": "Azazel"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Asael"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The guarding of the heart and the attention to what arises in it, treated as the groundwork on which the prayer of the heart is built."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Watchfulness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wachsamkeit",
        "en": "Watchfulness"
      },
      "source": [
        "Philokalia I, Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters",
        "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Crowley's transmission of a received book, of the discovery of one's own will as the central task, and of a practice curriculum with a demand for recorded results."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Thelema",
      "label": {
        "de": "Thelema",
        "en": "Thelema"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The insistence on keeping a magical diary and treating claims sceptically is a methodological demand made from inside a tradition, and belongs in the graph as such."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Crowley, Liber AL vel Legis; Crowley, Magick in Theory and Practice; Crowley, The Vision and the Voice",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to the witch-doctors: to see what is unseen, to tell who the witches are, to see the emanations of witchcraft floating about as small lights invisible to the uninitiated, and to resist a fatigue that the source says could not otherwise be borne."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SeeingWitchcraft",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sehen der Hexerei und das Weissagen",
        "en": "Seeing witchcraft and prophesying"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative Vedic corpus: spells for healing, protection, harm, love and kingship, with the ritual manual that tells how each is employed."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Atharvavedic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atharvavedische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Atharvavedic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The oldest Indian material the project would hold that is a working corpus rather than a doctrine."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā (Śaunaka recension); Kauśikasūtra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, made within a tradition, that a named practice produces a named capacity."
      },
      "id": "pm:Yielding",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hervorbringen",
        "en": "Yielding"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node and not as an edge for the same reason pm:Attributing is: only a node can carry its own source, its own mode of attestation, and a protocol that tries to fail it. A bare edge would make every such claim look alike, whatever it rests on, and would leave nothing for pm:Testing to point at. The question of which capacities a practice actually develops is answerable only where the pairing itself is addressable."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission is registered and its principal corpus is named. No passage has been read, no edition has been settled, and no node stands under it. A tradition in this state witnesses nothing and may not be counted as a party to a convergence."
      },
      "id": "pm:corpusNamed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Korpus benannt",
        "en": "Corpus named"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Māori transmission of mana as an efficacy that attaches to persons and things, of tapu as its restriction, and of karakia as the form of address."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Maori",
      "label": {
        "de": "Māori-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Māori transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Shortland 1882, chapters II to V and the appendix, in the 1882 London edition. Chapters VI and VII, on claiming and naming land, were not read; nothing else of the principal corpus was opened.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the reason is a rule of this project rather than a judgement about Polynesia. pm:contactRouteAbsent asks whether a route was available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. This corpus was fixed in writing from the eighteen-forties onward, a generation after the first mission stations, by Māori writers and by European recorders working with mission orthography. Shortland himself reports a rite of removing tapu that was carried out with a Christian morning service inside it and adds that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial; that is not a suspicion about the material but an observation printed in the witness. The transmission is older than the contact; the corpus this graph can cite is not. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry, and prima-materia#108 for the source criticism that decides it.",
          "The word from which every western theory of an impersonal magical force was built. The graph uses the notion throughout and has never held the tradition it came from."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ngā Mōteatea; Best, The Maori; Best, Tuhoe",
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Finnish and Karelian transmission of the tietäjä, in which power over a thing follows from knowing and reciting its origin."
      },
      "id": "pmt:FinnicIncantation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Finnische Zaubersprüche",
        "en": "Finnic incantation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Kalevala is a nineteenth-century compilation and belongs in the record as an ordering act, not as the corpus. The corpus is the collected incantations."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot; the loitsurunot collections",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The mid twentieth century transmission of an initiatory craft with a coven structure, a seasonal calendar and an inherited book copied by hand."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ModernWicca",
      "label": {
        "de": "Moderne Wicca",
        "en": "Modern Wicca"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Presented at its founding as the survival of an old religion. The construction is documented in detail, so the tradition and the refutation of its pedigree can be held together without either being lost."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Gardner, Witchcraft Today; the Book of Shadows in the published recensions",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of a healing energy conveyed by the hands, transmitted through an attunement conferred by a teacher in a stated lineage."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Reiki",
      "label": {
        "de": "Reiki",
        "en": "Reiki"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Attunement is a transmission claim in the strict sense: the capacity is said to pass from person to person by a discrete act. That is unusually well suited to examination, because the tradition itself distinguishes those who have received it from those who have not."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Usui Reiki Ryōhō Hikkei; Hayashi, Reiki Ryōhō Shishin",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Haitian transmission of spirits who arrive in a named person and speak, with a liturgy that assigns each an appearance, a rhythm and a manner."
      },
      "id": "pmt:HaitianVodou",
      "label": {
        "de": "Haitianisches Vodou",
        "en": "Haitian Vodou"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deren's account is a first-person report of being mounted by a spirit written by the observer herself, which is a rare form: participant testimony with the observation intact."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Métraux, Le vaudou haïtien; Deren, Divine Horsemen",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity the consultation is undertaken for: to be told whatever the questioners wish to know, with no restriction of subject stated in the passage."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wonach das Orakel befragt wird",
        "en": "What the oracle is asked for"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practice, at the public night ceremony, that many of those present know the songs well enough to catch a deviation, whereupon the ceremony is broken off as fruitless and the gathering disperses."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NavajoPublicErrorDetection",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zuhörerschaft als Fehlerinstanz",
        "en": "The audience as the detector of the error"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The part of the claim that makes it examinable rather than merely asserted. The tradition does not only say that an error destroys the effect; it names who notices, when, and what visibly follows. Neither of the two parallel witnesses in this bundle carries that."
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rendering of an invocation in which neither a mistake nor a pause occurs. The tradition sets the two on the same footing: hesitating is treated as failing, not as taking care."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MaoriExactRecitation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wiederholung ohne Fehler und ohne Zögern",
        "en": "Repetition without error or hesitation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AnapanasatiYieldsPiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atemachtsamkeit bringt Verzückung hervor",
        "en": "Mindfulness of breathing yields rapture"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Rapture appears in the handbook as a factor of absorption reached through the meditation subject, not as something aimed at directly. The pairing is recorded at that strength."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Piti"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Böhme's transmission of a divine self-disclosure through opposition, reported as having been seen rather than reasoned."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChristianTheosophy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Christliche Theosophie",
        "en": "Christian theosophy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Böhme, Aurora; Böhme, De signatura rerum",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Jung's transmission of an objective psyche and of active imagination as a procedure for engaging figures encountered in it, with a record kept of the engagement."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AnalyticalPsychology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Analytische Psychologie",
        "en": "Analytical psychology"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as a tradition and not as science on the rule stated at the head of this file: its central claims rest on testimony, including the author's own record of his experiment, and not on procedures that could have come out otherwise. Where it does produce evidence, that evidence enters at an examination node like any other."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Jung, Liber Novus; Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos; Jung, Mysterium Coniunctionis",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    }
  ]
}